//@version=6
// MAR1 ROTATION - the sweeper as a cockpit, and it chooses the length itself
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The seventh file of the MAR1 family. The pairs sweeper's core, kept verbatim
// (its essays below still speak in its voice about the swept lengths and the
// table - all of that is true here too), with every swept length now handing
// back its LIVE state as well as its totals, and six pieces of output on top:
//
//   the ► row       the length being TRADED, under the ranking's own column
//                   headers - the row this file exists to point at
//   the ROTATION    the traded length and since when, how many wins have
//   row             closed since the last recompute and what ends the next
//                   one, what the ranking would pick if the round ended now,
//                   the pair to enter (first ARMED pair in slot order - the tie
//                   rule is slot order, deliberately nothing cleverer), and the
//                   armed/held/running/excluded counts
//   the WORST       statistics, and nothing acts on it: the Worst column of
//   WINDOW line     the ranking averaged DOWN THE SWEPT LENGTHS, beside the
//                   deepest of them and the length and pair it happened on,
//                   and beside the traded length's own figure. It is the
//                   watchlist's scale - "a run costs about eleven entries at
//                   worst here, and the length being traded is seven" - which
//                   is what makes one number readable as good or bad
//   the PAIRS       mar1Scanner.pine's open-pairs table, on the traded length:
//   table           the same seven columns - Pair, State, Market, Entries,
//                   Open since, Streak / gate, Last - the same words, the same
//                   sort key, and rows wearing the colour of the last small
//                   diamond on that pair. Every pair that is not idle is on it
//                   - pre-armed, armed, held, running - and a LAST WIN line
//                   underneath keeps the most recent close on screen
//   the win alert   a dynamic alert() naming the pair whose traded-length
//                   series just CLOSED ON A WIN, read from the closure
//                   counter's delta on confirmed bars only
//   the STATUS      the same PAIRS table again, packed into the status line in
//   LINE            mar1Scanner.pine's own format so the browser extension in
//                   po-payout-filter can read it: three ten-digit numbers
//                   behind an 8, the pair-list checksum, and the traded MA
//                   length. See THE STATUS LINE at the foot of the file - it
//                   is what puts the OPEN & TRADEABLE line, payouts included,
//                   on the chart maRejection.pine is drawing
//
// THE LENGTH IS NOT TYPED IN ANY MORE, and that is the whole of the second
// version. There is no 'Active MA length' input: the traded length is the
// ranking's answer at position 'Ranking position to trade', re-read at every
// ROUND BOUNDARY, and everything below the ranking describes THAT length.
//
// A ROUND ENDS ON ONE EVENT AND ONE ONLY: some pair closed a window that cost
// MORE than 'Recompute the MA after a win costing more than' (3) entries. Any
// watched pair, on the traded length, inside the trading window. Until that
// happens nothing moves - the length on screen is the one being traded, cheap
// closes are counted and reported and change nothing, and the ordinary band
// is one to three entries with the fourth as the alarm.
//
// IT ALSO USED TO END ON A COUNT OF PAIRS - three distinct pairs of a set
// snapshotted at the round's start (added and then reversed on 2026-08-15).
// What that bought was frequent re-reads of the ranking; what it cost was a
// rotation every few minutes on a watchlist that was doing nothing wrong, and
// every rotation is a number that has to be retyped into maRejection.pine by
// hand. A run past the band is the one event a recovery ladder cannot absorb,
// so it is the one that gets to interrupt a length that is working.
//
// On a boundary the ranking is recomputed AS OF THAT BAR - no lookahead, the
// totals are what the machines had actually closed by then - and the length
// holding the picked position becomes the traded one. It very often is the one
// already traded, and then nothing moves but the round. Over the loaded history
// this makes the file a PLAY of the rotation rather than a snapshot of it: the
// length on screen is the one the rule arrived at, boundary by boundary, from
// the first bar the ranking could trust anything.
//
// THE STATISTICS ARE ALL-HOURS AND THE TABLE IS NOT, and since 2026-08-15
// those are TWO MACHINES PER LENGTH rather than one machine argued over.
// This is the final design of the day, and the first that holds both
// requirements at once - they genuinely could not share a machine:
//
//   the RANKING is fed by the shared MAR1 block, which grades every candle
//   of the loaded history around the clock. 'Fire signals only inside those
//   hours' stays OFF and nothing narrows the sample - a length is judged on
//   everything it did, on every pair, at every hour;
//   the PAIRS TABLE is fed by a SECOND state machine per length, built into
//   the ROTATION TAIL of sweepOne, which grades the same candles with one
//   difference: a signal outside the 'Trading hours' window is never found.
//   That is exactly how mar1Scanner.pine's machine treats a signal outside
//   its own hours filter, so the table shows what a 4-16 trader actually
//   faces - its streaks, its gate, its windows and its 'Open since' stamps
//   all live inside the trading window BY CONSTRUCTION, and there is no row
//   filter anywhere in this file any more.
//
// Why the split had to happen, so it is not re-litigated: the hours gate
// runs BEFORE the streak is counted, so one machine can serve one master -
// all-hours statistics OR in-hours states, never both. Filtering the rows
// was tried twice and flipping the signal filter once, all three reverted
// the same day, before the second machine was written.
//
// What the second machine costs: one more set of counters per length per
// pair, its live state REPLACING the all-hours live value (which nothing
// read once the table switched), no extra MA and no extra trend test, since
// the geometry is shared - plus, since 2026-08-16, NINE extra
// request.security values per pair: three carrying its closure counters at
// three digits per length, three its window ages the same way, and three the
// age of the entry that pushed a window past the round threshold. Expect
// the script somewhat slower; the levers under
// AND IT IS SLOW ON PURPOSE apply unchanged.
//
// The round follows the machine too (2026-08-16, user decision): it ends on
// a deep window of the TRADING machine - see rotWatch - so the day-closes
// record, the ► on it, the LAST WIN line, the alert and the round all
// describe the same closes mar1Scanner.pine's log holds. Only the RANKING
// stays all-hours. (Since 2026-08-16 the two grids sit one step apart - the
// sweeper reads 16..44, this file 15..43 - and the sweeper scans twenty-five
// pair slots to this file's twenty-one, so the two first places need not
// agree.)
// An armed pair whose clock is outside the window reads
// HELD, is counted, and is never proposed.
//
// WHAT STILL NEEDS A HUMAN, and it is one step: copying the traded length into
// maRejection.pine, where the real entries are taken. Pine cannot cross-write
// an input, so nothing here can do it and nothing checks the two agree. The
// ROTATION row turns amber and names the pending pick before a boundary moves
// it, which is the cue to be ready.
//
// The ranking underneath all of that can be HIDDEN - 'Hide the ranking table'
// in the Rotation group - and hiding it keeps ONE row of it: the traded length,
// under its own column headers, above the ROTATION row. The other fourteen,
// the sweep header, the footer and the verdict line are what go. Everything
// stays live either way: the ranking is computed whether it is drawn or not.
//
// THE STAGE-2 JOURNAL of .specify/consilium/2026-08-15-mar1-rotation.md IS
// HERE (2026-08-16), as ONE table on a position of its own:
//
//   the DAY         every window the day closed, one per line, with nothing
//   CLOSES          left out: time, pair, entries, the MA it closed on, and
//                   a mark on the one that ended the round. It fills a block
//                   downwards and wraps sideways into the next, and pages
//                   past four of them
//
// It reads ONE day - the latest the loaded history reached, or a day picked
// from a calendar - and is drawn from arrays the rotation writes as it runs,
// so it is a record rather than a second opinion.
//
// IT ARRIVED AS TWO TABLES AND LASTED ONE DAY AS TWO. Beside it stood a
// ROTATION JOURNAL, one row per recompute, with four of that round's closes
// shown in the last column as a taste of what had happened. Once the day
// closes table existed the journal was four records of what that holds in
// full, so it went (user decision, 2026-08-16) along with its two inputs.
// Nothing is missing: a recompute that MOVED the length is the line where the
// MA column changes, and the window that caused it is the ► above it. A
// recompute that changed nothing leaves no trace any more, which is the whole
// of what was given up.
//
// WHAT IS STILL NOT HERE is an alert on the length CHANGING - see the note
// beside the win alert at the foot of the file for what it would take and
// what it is waiting for.
//
// ALERT SETUP, once per chart and OPTIONAL: Create Alert -> Condition:
// MAR1 Rotation -> 'Any alert() function call'. Without that subscription the
// alert() calls here reach nobody - which is fine on a plan without alerts:
// the PAIRS table and its LAST WIN line put the same facts on the table.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE COSTS: the tuple is 39 wide - fifteen packed totals, fifteen
// packed live states, three floats of trading-machine closure counters, three
// of the window ages those closes carried and three of when each window
// crossed the round threshold - so the main scope holds 21 x 39 = 819
// destructured names plus the rest, comfortably under the 1200 ceiling.
// The variable budget is what halved the sweep the first time (forty lengths
// at one number each became twenty at two) and what took the four empty pair
// slots back out; the later cut to eleven and the step back up to fifteen
// (2026-08-16, user decisions) are the RUNTIME memory limit's - see the
// note at SWEEP_N. The
// shared state machine is fenced by SYNC markers inside sweepOne and checked
// byte-for-byte against maSweepPairs.pine by checks/block-sync.js.
//
// MAR1 MA Sweep, across the watchlist - which MA Length to trade, on ALL pairs.
//
// One row per MA Length, and each row is the line the scanner prints at the top
// of its finished-series log:
//
//   CLOSED LOGGED  ·  210  ·  450 entries, 2.14286 avg
//
// closed windows, entries spent, and the average of the two - summed across
// every ticked pair, over the whole loaded history. The scanner answers that
// question for the MA Length you happen to have set. This one asks it fifteen
// times at once and puts the answers side by side, which is the only way to see
// whether the length you are trading is the one the watchlist actually likes.
//
// It also answers the question the average cannot. An average is paid out of the
// short windows and taken away by the long ones, so two lengths can average the
// same and be nothing alike: one closes almost everything on the second entry,
// the other alternates cheap wins with runs that go five, six, nine deep. The
// RED column counts the deep ones - windows that reached the entry where the
// diamond ladder turns red - so the tail is on the table next to the mean, and
// the table can be sorted on it. Read that column, not Avg, when the stake
// climbs the ladder: a ladder does not die of a high average, it dies of a
// single long run.
//
// And the LINKS column answers the question Red cannot. Red counts deep windows;
// it does not say whether they arrived together. Evenly spaced deep runs let a
// recovery ladder return to its base stake between them; two deep runs back to
// back do not, and that is the shape a ladder actually dies in. Links counts how
// often one deep window opened shortly after another closed ON THE SAME PAIR.
// It is a tripwire rather than a scale - blank on most rows by design. The table
// CAN be sorted on it: 'Links, then Score' puts the rows with fewest links on
// top and settles the ties - which on ordinary data is every row - on Score, so
// it reads exactly like Balanced until some length actually stacks deep windows,
// and then sinks that length whatever its average says. Note the two thresholds
// are separate: Red counts at redAt, Links at deepAt, so unless you set them
// equal the two columns describe different sets of windows.
//
// Its companion is maSweep.pine, which asks the same question on ONE symbol at
// thirty-three lengths and costs almost nothing, because it needs no
// request.security at all. Use that one to comb a pair; use this one to decide
// what the whole list wants.
//
// THE LOCAL VARIABLE BUDGET - read this before changing either number
// --------------------------------------------------------------------
// TradingView allows a script 1200 local variables in its main scope, and this
// file spends nearly all of them on one thing: every pair hands back TWO packed
// values per length plus NINE floats of trading-machine state, so
// SLOTS x (2 x LENGTHS + 9) names come out of the request.security calls and
// every one is counted.
//
//     slots  x  (2 x lengths + 9)  +  about 45 others   <=   1200
//
// The product is the part that matters; the constant is everything else in the
// main scope and it grows slowly as the table does - it measured 24 before the
// Red column and its ranking switch were added, so treat 45 as a rough current
// figure rather than a fixed one.
//
// The product is measured, not guessed. Thirty slots at sixty lengths, back
// when a length cost one value, was compiled and refused:
//
//     Script has too many local variables (1824) in "#main" ... limit is 1200
//
// 30 x 60 is 1800, so the twenty-four is the whole rest of the file put
// together. The split in the sweeper is twenty-five slots by twenty lengths at
// one value each - 500. THIS file spends 819 on TWENTY-ONE slots at
// thirty-nine values each - two per length plus the nine state floats - which
// leaves about three hundred of headroom and puts the hard maximum near
// TWENTY-NINE slots. The second value is what the rotation is made of: the ranking needs
// the totals, the pairs table needs the live state, and the length the table
// is drawn for is not known until both have crossed. The nine floats are the
// trading machine's closure counters, the ages of the windows they closed and
// the ages of the entries that took those windows past the round threshold,
// five lengths to a float each - see t2v, a2v and d2v in the tail of sweepOne.
//
// Raising one means lowering the other and then regenerating the blocks marked
// GENERATED below. Nothing warns you at runtime: the compiler refuses the whole
// script before the first bar.
//
// AND IT IS SLOW ON PURPOSE
// -------------------------
// Twenty-one pairs at fifteen lengths is 315 copies of MAR1's state
// machine, each walked over that pair's whole loaded history. That is the most
// expensive thing in this project by a very wide margin, and the execution time
// limit is a second ceiling underneath the variable one - fitting the compiler
// does not mean finishing in time. It is three quarters of what the same file
// cost at twenty lengths - and three quarters of their memory bill too, which
// is the number to watch on M1 and M3.
//
// THE FOUR EMPTY SLOTS ARE GONE AGAIN (2026-08-16, user decision, the same
// day they arrived): at fifty-two values per slot they no longer fit the
// variable budget, and they were eighty idle machines of pure runtime the
// whole time - a request.security call cannot be made conditional.
//
// If it refuses to run, the levers are these, in the order worth trying:
//
//   1. untick pairs. The big one for TIME, and the only half of the product the
//      dialog can change. It does NOT help the variable budget - an unticked
//      slot still declares its thirty-nine names - so it fixes a timeout and never a
//      compile error.
//   2. run it on a higher timeframe. Fewer bars, same logic, same answer shape.
//   3. narrow First / Last. It does not save a thing - all fifteen call sites run
//      whatever the range says - but it makes the point that the range and the
//      cost are unrelated, so there is no reason to sweep wider than you read.
//   4. 'Bars of history per pair'. Last, and it changes the ANSWER rather than
//      the cost - a shorter history gives the streak gate a different threshold,
//      so the entries it counts are not the entries the chart takes. It also
//      shortens the rotation's own history, which is a bigger change than it
//      looks: the traded length is the end of a chain of round boundaries, and a
//      shorter chain can end somewhere else entirely.
//
// If it fails to COMPILE rather than timing out, none of those help: that is
// the variable budget above, and the fix is to lower SWEEP_N or the slot count
// and regenerate.
//
// What is NOT multiplied by twenty is everything the moving average does not
// enter into: the ATR, both wick averages, the body average, the geometry, both
// counter-trend paths. Those are computed once per pair and shared. What is
// multiplied is one moving average, a three-step trend loop, a handful of
// comparisons and the counter bookkeeping.
//
// The count cannot be a setting
// -----------------------------
// ta.ema demands a SIMPLE int, so a length has to be fixed before the first bar,
// and independent state per length needs a separate CALL SITE - a `var` inside a
// function belongs to the call site rather than to the function, so a loop would
// run one machine fifteen times and end with one answer. The fifteen are written
// out, and changing SWEEP_N means regenerating the blocks marked GENERATED below
// rather than editing them by hand.
//
// IT IS ALSO WHY THE TRADED LENGTH CANNOT BE ONE MACHINE OF ITS OWN. The
// obvious design - one extra call site running the chosen length - needs that
// length before the first bar, and the choice is made from a ranking that
// cannot exist until every pair has answered. So all fifteen run, all fifteen
// report their live state, and the choice is made afterwards on this side of
// request.security. The cut from forty lengths to twenty was the price of
// that sentence; the moves between eleven and fifteen are the memory limit's.
//
// Computed once, in the sense that matters
// ----------------------------------------
// The table is written on the last bar only. The state machines themselves have
// to run on every bar - that is what walking the history means, and there is no
// Pine in which it is otherwise - but nothing is drawn, ranked or totalled until
// the chart is done loading. Changing a setting reruns the whole thing; ticks
// arriving on the live bar do not.
//
// Keep the signal settings identical to maRejection.pine or the table describes
// a strategy you are not trading. There is no CFGSUM here to catch a divergence.
//
// calc_bars_count = 10000 (user decision, 2026-08-16; it was 1000 earlier the
// same day): the script computes on the LAST ten thousand bars and nothing
// before them. A plan upgrade made M1 and M3 charts load enough history that
// 420 machines across 21 request.security contexts blew TradingView's runtime
// memory limit - "Memory limits exceeded" - while M2, M4 and M5 still ran.
// NOT the same lever as 'Bars of history per pair': that input only pauses
// the counter bookkeeping and every machine still runs on every loaded bar,
// so it cannot save memory at any value - this one stops the bars from being
// computed at all, which makes it the SECOND lever against that error and
// SWEEP_N the first. Ten thousand bars is the fence the user chose; every
// counter, ranking total and table starts from it, so the totals are "over
// the last ten thousand bars", not "over all loaded history". On M1 that is
// about a week of a round-the-clock market, so the DAY CLOSES table reaches
// back past the morning comfortably - lower it again if the memory error
// comes back. The SAME number sits in maRejection.pine, mar1Scanner.pine and
// maSweepPairs.pine, and it must: scripts describing one machine have to
// read the same history or their counters disagree.
indicator('MAR1 Rotation', overlay = true, max_bars_back = 200, calc_bars_count = 10000)

// Fifteen lengths, and this time the number IS a preference - the compiler
// allowed twenty. The story is TradingView's RUNTIME memory limit: a plan
// upgrade let M1 and M3 charts load enough history that 21 x 20 = 420
// machines across the request.security contexts blew "Memory limits
// exceeded" while M2, M4 and M5 still ran, so the count was halved to
// eleven and then raised back to fifteen (both 2026-08-16, user decisions)
// - 21 x 15 = 315 machines, three quarters of the count that failed,
// covering 15..43 at step 2. Whether fifteen still runs on M1/M3 is an
// experiment; eleven is the fallback if it does not.
//
// The variable budget is still comfortable: twenty-one slots at
// thirty-nine values is 819 names against the 1200 ceiling. History of the
// number: forty while every length handed back one value, twenty once every
// length handed back two - its totals AND its live state, the second being
// what lets the traded length be chosen by the ranking instead of typed in -
// then eleven, then fifteen, for memory.
//
// Sixty was tried, back when a length cost one number, and refused by
// TradingView outright before a single bar ran:
//
//   Script has too many local variables (1824) in "#main" ... The limit is 1200
//
// Thirty slots times sixty lengths is 1800 of those 1824. That is the shape of
// the arithmetic to redo before changing either number here.
SWEEP_N = 15

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The range
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// First in the dialog, because it and the output block below it are the only
// two things touched between one run and the next. Everything under them is set
// up once, to match maRejection.pine, and then left alone.
// NOTHING BELOW PRINTS ITSELF IN THE STATUS LINE except three settings, and
// that is what display = display.none on the rest of this file buys.
//
// TradingView prints an indicator's ARGUMENTS after its name in the legend, in
// declaration order. This file has ninety-four inputs - twenty-one pair
// slots with their tick boxes are forty-two of them - and the line ran the width
// of the chart as a wall of bare numbers with nothing saying which number was
// which. A legend that has to be decoded is worse than no legend.
//
// display.none removes an input from that line and from the Data Window and
// changes NOTHING ELSE: the input is fully live, fully editable, and sits in the
// settings dialog exactly where it did. It is a display flag, not a switch, and
// it does not touch the saved-settings POSITION that everything in this file has
// to be careful about.
//
// THREE ARE VISIBLE, and the third one differs from the other files in this
// project on purpose:
//
//   MA Type        THE ONE THE OTHER FILES HIDE. Everywhere else the visible
//                  setting is MA Length, because there is one length and it
//                  defines the indicator. Here the LENGTHS ARE THE ROWS OF THE
//                  TABLE - they say themselves, in ink, fifteen at a time. What
//                  nothing on screen says is which KIND of average every one of
//                  them is, and a sweep run on SMA describes a strategy that is
//                  not being traded: maRejection.pine sits on EMA with this
//                  setting hidden, and the whole point of this table is to feed
//                  numbers back into that file. Wrong type here and every row is
//                  an answer to a question nobody asked.
//   Streak gate    with Off there is no gate at all, nothing is ever held back,
//                  and every signal counts as an entry. Every column in the
//                  table is then measuring something else.
//   Fire signals   whether the hours filter is actually biting. It runs BEFORE
//   only inside    the streak is counted, so it moves the distribution and
//   those hours    therefore the gate threshold - it is not a cosmetic filter.
//
// Everything else - the sweep range, the ranking, the payout, the block layout,
// the whole signal mirror - is either already printed by the table itself or is
// one dialog click away. There is no CFGSUM in this file to catch a divergence
// from maRejection.pine, so nothing was lost from the legend that the legend was
// ever going to catch.
//
// TO BRING ANY OTHER ONE BACK: delete ', display = display.none' from its line.
// To hide one of the three above: add it. There is nothing to keep in sync - the
// flag is per input and reaches no logic.
gSwp = 'Sweep range'

// All three are typed. Nothing here is inferred from anything else: First says
// where the sweep starts, Step says how far apart the lengths sit, Last says
// where it stops.
//
//   15, step 2, to 43    15, 17, 19 ... 43 - the default, and exact
//   15, step 1, to 30    every length from 15 to 29 - fifteen rows, half span
//   25, step 1, to 30    25, 26, 27 ... 30 - six rows, the other slots idle
//   10, step 1, to 200   10, 11, 12 ... 24 - the slots run out at 24
//
// The one thing the three of them cannot override is SWEEP_N. There are exactly
// fifteen call sites and there is no Pine in which there are sixteen, so:
//
//   fewer than fifteen lengths fit in the range  the extra slots are computed
//                                                anyway, because a call site
//                                                cannot be made conditional,
//                                                and simply not drawn
//   more than fifteen fit                        the sweep stops at
//                                                First + 14·Step, short of Last
//
// Neither case is left to be worked out from the last row. The header prints the
// range that was actually swept and, when it is not the one that was typed, says
// so in amber - and says which of the two reasons it was.
//
// Nothing here changes the cost. All fifteen machines run whatever the range says,
// so a narrow range is not cheaper - it just draws fewer rows.
//
// Both minimums are 1 so that First + i·Step can never reach zero, which is what
// lets the lengths be built from inputs at all: ta.ema needs a SIMPLE int, and
// arithmetic on inputs stays simple.
//
// 15 TO 43 AT STEP 2 (2026-08-16, user decision - the fourth grid of one
// day). The sweep was cut from twenty lengths (5..43) to eleven (20..40)
// for the RUNTIME memory limit, then raised back to fifteen the same day:
// 315 machines against the 420 that blew "Memory limits exceeded" on M1
// and M3, so whether this count still runs there is an experiment, and
// eleven is the fallback if it does not. See THE LOCAL VARIABLE BUDGET in
// the header and the note at SWEEP_N.
//
// LAST IS 43 AND NOT 44, which is the same fifteen lengths written so the
// step lands on the last one exactly: fifteen from 15 at step 2 reach 43,
// so a Last of 44 would sweep the identical set and make the header say in
// amber, on every run, that the step does not land on it.
//
// The short lengths are not gone, they are one setting away: type 5 into
// First and the same fifteen slots sweep 5..33 instead.
//
// NOTE FOR SAVED CHARTS: defaults do not overwrite saved settings. A chart
// that carried an older version still holds its old First / Last - First 16
// / Last 44 sweeps 16..44, First 20 / Last 40 sweeps 20..40 on eleven of the
// fifteen slots. Set First to 15 and Last to 43 by hand there, or reset the
// inputs once.
sweepFrom = input.int(15, 'First MA length', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, group = gSwp,
     tooltip = 'The shortest moving average in the sweep. Lowering it reaches down at the cost of the top end, since fifteen lengths is fifteen lengths.', display = display.none)
sweepStep = input.int(2, 'Step', minval = 1, maxval = 50, group = gSwp,
     tooltip = 'The gap between one swept length and the next. Fifteen lengths are computed whatever this is, so a larger step costs nothing extra and reaches further - it just skips what lies between. At step 1 the same fifteen slots cover 15..29 instead.', display = display.none)
sweepTo = input.int(43, 'Last MA length', minval = 1, maxval = 1000, group = gSwp,
     tooltip = 'Where the sweep stops. Fifteen slots exist, so if First, Step and this ask for more than fifteen the sweep ends at First + 14 steps and the header says so. If they ask for fewer, the rest of the rows are simply not drawn - 15 to 43 at step 2 is exactly fifteen and fills the table.', display = display.none)

// How many of the fifteen call sites land inside the requested range. Guarded on
// both ends: a Last at or below First gives one row rather than a negative
// count, and the cap keeps a wide range from claiming rows that do not exist.
//
// int() rather than math.floor() - a cast keeps the SIMPLE qualifier that
// ta.ema demands of the length, and the division is over positive numbers, so
// truncation and floor are the same thing here.
sweepSpan = sweepTo > sweepFrom ? sweepTo - sweepFrom : 0
sweepFit  = int(sweepSpan / sweepStep) + 1
usedN     = sweepFit > SWEEP_N ? SWEEP_N : sweepFit
sweepLast = sweepFrom + (usedN - 1) * sweepStep

// True when the range asked for is wider than fifteen slots can carry. Kept apart
// from "the step did not land on Last exactly", because the two have different
// fixes: this one is raise the Step, the other one is nothing worth doing.
slotsShort = sweepFit > SWEEP_N

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gate and window - the three settings the averages answer to
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Second in the dialog, directly under the range, because these and the range
// are the only settings touched between one run and the next. Everything below
// them is set once to match maRejection.pine and then left alone.
//
// The same three sit in the same place in mar1Scanner.pine, under a header of
// the same name, and the first two of them do in maRejection.pine as well - so
// the dialog reads the same way whichever of the three files is open. That is
// the point of the arrangement: a setting that has to be kept identical across
// files should not have to be hunted for in a different place in each.
//
//   Percentage            the cumulative cutoff the streak distribution is read
//                         at, which becomes the gate threshold in Distribution
//                         and in Max of both
//   Min entries to start  the manual floor under that threshold
//   A window is closed by which successes close a window - the definition every
//                         number in the table is counted against
//
// They belong to Filters in every sense that matters to the signal, and they
// must hold maRejection.pine's values or the table ranks lengths for a strategy
// nobody trades. Only the header above them is different.
gTune = 'Gate and window (must match MAR1)'

// 55 / 6 (2026-08-12, user request - the values the sweeps are actually run
// at). "Must match MAR1" means match whichever gate you are sweeping for: 55
// is gate A's percentage in mar1Gates.pine, and the files are free to disagree
// out of the box - a sweep is always run FOR some gate, and the gate of the
// moment is what these two should hold.
basePercentage    = input.float(90.0, 'Percentage', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, group = gTune, display = display.none)
minEntriesToStart = input.int(8, 'Min entries to start', minval = 1, group = gTune, display = display.none)

// Which successes close a window. 'Entries only' is the strict reading and the
// default on all three sides: a window is closed by a trade that was actually
// taken, not by a signal the gate held back.
successScope = input.string('Entries only', 'A window is closed by',
     options = ['Entries only', 'Entries and warnings', 'All found signals'], group = gTune, display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Output
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gOut = 'Sweep output'

// The payout the AVG columns are judged against. It changes no arithmetic; it
// decides where green stops and red starts, which is what turns 2.14 from a
// number into a verdict.
//
// At a FLAT stake and a payout of r, a length pays only while
//
//     avg  <  1 + r
//
// so 92% needs an average under 1.92. If the stake climbs the colour ladder
// instead, AVG is the wrong column and WORST is the right one: a recovery ladder
// earns a fixed amount per closed window whatever it cost, and dies on the one
// window that ran too long.
payoutPct = input.float(92.0, 'Broker payout %', minval = 10.0, maxval = 200.0, step = 1.0, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'At a flat stake a length pays only while its average is below 1 + payout. 92% gives a break-even average of 1.92.', display = display.none)

// A length that closed six windows has an average that means nothing, and left
// unmarked it would sit at the top of the ranking. Rows under this are greyed,
// cannot be best or worst, and do not stretch the bars.
//
// They are still SORTED, and they did not used to be - see sortKey. 'Ignore' is
// about trust, not about order: a row nobody should act on is still a row, and
// leaving a block of them in length order made a sorted table look broken
// exactly when the history was too short for anything else to be on screen.
minClosures = input.int(30, 'Ignore lengths with fewer wins than', minval = 1, maxval = 5000, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Below this a row is greyed and cannot be marked best or worst - an average over a handful of windows is not a finding. It is still placed in order among the other untrusted rows, so the table is sorted whatever the history has managed to produce.', display = display.none)

// The line between a window that cost something and a window that hurt - not a
// round number chosen for tidiness, but tied to the rung where the colour
// ladder turns RED in maRejection.pine and in both of the scanner's tables.
//
// A window opens on its anchor with nothing entered and every entry that misses
// moves the streak one rung along the same ladder maRejection.pine draws.
// Counting windows that reached the red rung is exactly counting the runs that
// went red at least once.
//
// It counts WINDOWS, not diamonds. A window that took many entries is still one
// red window here, and how many it took shows up in the Worst column instead -
// the two answer "how often" and "how bad", and neither substitutes for the
// other.
//
// Lower it to ask a softer question; raise it to ask only about the runs a
// recovery ladder would not have survived. Set it too low and the count stops
// being a tail and becomes most of the table; it also becomes the field of the
// packed format most likely to overflow - see the packing block. That is
// reported in the footer rather than hidden.
//
// THE DEFAULT SITS SHORT OF THE TRUE RED RUNG on purpose - "how often did it
// get close to disaster" is often the more useful ranking question than "how
// often did it actually happen", since two lengths that look alike on the raw
// average tend to separate earlier on the ladder before they separate on red
// itself. Set it to the rung the ladder actually turns red on to count only the
// genuinely red windows.
redAt = input.int(2, 'A window is RED at this many entries', minval = 2, maxval = 50, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'How deep a window has to go before the Red column counts it. The default sits short of the rung where the diamond ladder actually turns red, on purpose - two lengths that look alike on the raw average tend to separate earlier on the ladder before they separate on red itself. Set it to the rung the ladder actually turns red on to count only the genuinely red windows.', display = display.none)

// What the table is ORDERED and RANKED by. The ★ ☆ ▽ ▼ marks follow this too -
// they are the top two and bottom two of whatever is chosen here, not of a fixed
// column, so the marks and the order can never disagree.
//
// BALANCED is the default and it is the one that answers the real question: put
// at the top the length whose pessimistic average is low AND whose deep runs are
// few. Neither column alone can say that. A length can carry the best average in
// the table and get there by paying for a wall of cheap wins with the occasional
// nine-entry run, and Avg +1σ will happily rank it first.
//
// The two cannot simply be added - one is entries per window, the other is a
// proportion - so each is stretched onto 0..1 across the rows that qualify and
// the two are then mixed by Tail weight. A score of 0 is best in the table on
// both, 1 is worst on both, and the arithmetic is spelled out where it happens.
//
// The cost of that, stated: a 0..1 score is RELATIVE to the rows on screen.
// Changing the range or the win threshold changes what 0 and 1 mean, so the
// score compares lengths against each other and never against a fixed standard.
// The three plain columns are all absolute; this one is not.
//
// The other three are unchanged and each answers one question outright:
// Avg +1σ for a flat stake, Avg for the raw mean, Red share for the tail alone.
//
// LINKS, THEN SCORE is the fifth, and the only THREE-LEVEL key.
// It is not a blend and there is no weight to set - it is a strict hierarchy:
//
//   1. the raw link count - a HARD GATE. Two lengths with different counts are
//      ALWAYS ordered by the count alone, whatever the other two levels say.
//   2. a sample-size-aware reading of that count - the SAME caution Avg gets
//      turned into Avg +1σ and Red gets turned into redHi, reused rather than
//      reinvented, now applied to Links. It refines the order AMONG rows that
//      tie on the raw count - which on ordinary data is most of them, because
//      Links is a tripwire and reads zero everywhere. A length with 400 wins
//      and zero links is a more trustworthy zero than one with 40 wins and
//      zero links, and this level ranks the second one worse.
//   3. Score, the same Balanced number as everywhere else - the final
//      tie-break, for whatever level 2 still cannot separate.
//
// What to expect: with every row at zero raw links, level 1 never speaks, so
// the order runs on level 2 (bigger, more convincing samples first) with
// level 3 settling anything still tied. It differs from Balanced only where a
// length has actually stacked deep windows back to back - level 1 then drops
// that length to the bottom no matter how good its average or its sample is.
//
// All five read low-is-better, so the order is always ascending and ★ is always
// the top row.
rankBy = input.string('Balanced (+1σ & Red)', 'Rank and sort by',
     options = ['Balanced (+1σ & Red)', 'Avg +1σ', 'Avg', 'Red share', 'Links, then Score'], group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Balanced - THE DEFAULT - puts a length at the top only if it is good on BOTH the pessimistic average and the deep runs - each is scaled 0..1 across the visible rows and mixed by Tail weight. Avg +1σ, Avg and Red share sort on one column outright.\n\nLinks, then Score is a three-level hierarchy, not a blend: raw link count first (a hard gate - a real difference in links always wins), then a sample-size-aware reading of that count (the same caution behind Avg +1σ and Red\'s pessimistic share, reused for Links), then Score as the final tie-break. Since Links reads zero on most rows, the raw count rarely speaks and the order runs on sample-size confidence, settled by Score - it differs from Balanced only where a length actually stacked deep windows back to back, and then it sinks that length whatever its average says.', display = display.none)

// How much of the Balanced score is the tail. 50 is an even split; 0 makes it
// Avg +1σ exactly and 100 makes it Red share exactly, so the two endpoints
// reproduce two of the other options and everything between is a blend.
//
// Raise it as position size climbs the ladder. A flat stake cares about the mean
// and barely about the tail; a ladder is the other way round, because it earns a
// fixed amount per closed window whatever that window cost and dies on the one
// run that went too far.
//
// It does nothing unless Rank and sort by is Balanced.
tailWeight = input.int(50, 'Tail weight in the Balanced score, %', minval = 0, maxval = 100, step = 5, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'The share of the Balanced score that comes from the Red column rather than from Avg +1σ. 0 is pure Avg +1σ, 100 is pure Red, 50 weighs them evenly. Ignored unless Balanced is the chosen ranking.', display = display.none)

// When the list is longer than this it wraps into another block BESIDE the
// first, exactly the way the scanner's closed log does - same columns repeated,
// a blank column between them, each block picking up where the last ran out.
// Height is the direction a chart has least of, so the list grows sideways.
//
// Fifteen rows fit one column at the default of twenty; eight puts them into
// two short blocks side by side.
rowsPerBlock = input.int(20, 'Rows per block (wraps sideways past this)', minval = 2, maxval = 40, group = gOut,
     tooltip = 'Up to four blocks stand side by side, so 8 here holds all 15 lengths in two short columns and 20 holds them in one. Anything that does not fit in four blocks is not drawn, and the footer says so rather than letting rows go missing quietly.', display = display.none)

tablePos    = input.string('Middle right', 'Table position',
     options = ['Middle right', 'Middle left', 'Middle center', 'Top right', 'Bottom right', 'Top center', 'Bottom center'], group = gOut, display = display.none)
txtSizeName = input.string('Small', 'Text size', options = ['Tiny', 'Small', 'Normal'], group = gOut, display = display.none)

// Four, kept from the forty-length days: fifteen rows fit as one column at
// the default, and the spare blocks cost nothing unwritten.
MAX_BLOCKS = 4

// The palette, and every one of these is OPAQUE on purpose - a table has to be a
// surface, not a tint. Two row shades alternating, because a wall of one colour
// is one the eye loses its place in.
C_ROW_A  = #1F242D
C_ROW_B  = #171B22
C_ROW_NO = #101317   // below the win threshold - present, and not competing
C_TXT    = #F2F6FA
C_TXT_NO = #7C8792
C_HEAD   = #3B404A
C_FOOT   = #1A1E25

// The four ranked rows. Two greens and two reds, and within each pair second
// place is the darker one - so the ladder reads as intensity rather than as four
// unrelated colours, and a glance finds both ends of the table without reading a
// number.
//
// The winner and the loser are solid and carry dark text; the runners-up are
// deep enough to take light text. That is deliberate: solid-with-dark-text is
// the loudest thing this table can do, and only the two extremes earn it.
C_BEST     = #7CE23F
C_BEST_T   = #0A1400
C_BEST2    = #2E5E2A
C_BEST2_T  = #D8F5C8
C_WORST    = #C7442F
C_WORST_T  = #1A0500
C_WORST2   = #5E2A22
C_WORST2_T = #F5D8D0

// THE MIDDLE PAIR, and blue because it is not on the green-to-red axis at all.
// Green means take this, red means avoid it; the middle of the ranking means
// neither, and painting it a shade of either would put it on a scale it does
// not belong to. Blue is the only hue in this table that says "here, and no
// verdict attached".
//
// ONE shade for BOTH rows, unlike the two pairs above. Best and second best are
// genuinely ordered - one of them won - so intensity says which. The two middle
// rows are not ordered against each other in any sense worth showing: they are
// the two that straddle the midpoint, and there is nothing to be gained by
// asking which of them is nearer it. Deep enough to take light text, so the
// break-even hues on Avg and Avg +1σ stay readable on top of it.
C_MID   = #24486E
C_MID_T = #CFE3FF

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Five numbers through one hole
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every pair has to hand back five totals per length - closures, entries, the
// worst single window, how many windows went RED, and how many times one deep
// window followed another - and there are fifteen lengths. As a plain tuple that
// is seventy-five values crossing request.security, twenty-one times over: well
// over fifteen hundred names in the main scope against a ceiling of twelve
// hundred. See THE LOCAL VARIABLE BUDGET in the header - unpacked, this file
// could not be compiled at any useful size at all.
//
// So the five are packed into one number and unpacked on the other side. Fifteen
// values cross instead of seventy-five, and that is what leaves room for the live half.
//
//   value  =  entries · 1e12 + closures · 1e8 + red · 1e4 + links · 1e2 + worst
//
// A double holds integers exactly up to 2^53, which is 9007199254740992, and
// this layout was built backwards from that number rather than from what looks
// tidy. Sixteen digits is one more than the fifteen that always fit, so the top
// field is capped short of its full width: entries stops at 8999 and the
// largest value that can be produced is 8999999999999999 - under 2^53, and
// therefore carried across exactly. That maximum is unchanged by the Links
// column: the layout was re-cut, not widened.
//
// What each ceiling means, and the honesty of the answer differs by field now:
//
//   entries  < 9000    an entry is at most one per bar, so 90000 used to be a
//                      PROOF - longer than any history TradingView hands out.
//                      9000 is not that proof, and the comment must not pretend
//                      it is. It is an OBSERVATION: measured on the live gated
//                      configuration (checks/log-0208.csv, 88 pair-and-gate
//                      machines over Mon-Fri), the busiest single machine spent
//                      31 entries in 6459 minutes, which is about 96 entries per
//                      20000 bars. That is roughly 94x of headroom against a
//                      threshold nothing has approached - but it is a
//                      measurement on one regime, not a bound
//   closures < 10000   the same measurement gives ~50 closures per 20000 bars on
//                      that machine, about 200x
//   red      < 10000   deliberately the SAME width as closures, and that is what
//                      retires an old warning. Red is a subset of closures, so
//                      with equal widths red can no longer be the first field to
//                      break - it used to be, because closures had five digits
//                      against its four
//   links    < 100     the narrowest field in the layout, and the only one whose
//                      ceiling is justified by measurement rather than by its own
//                      contract. At the hostile corner the dialog allows -
//                      threshold 2, tolerance 480 - the worst single machine in
//                      the log carried 3 links, about 9 per 20000 bars. Roughly
//                      11x. CLAMPED rather than overflowed, see below
//   worst    <= 99     CLAMPED rather than overflowed - see below
//
// Fields have been narrowed twice before. Adding the red count cost entries one
// digit (it was < 1e6) and worst two (it was < 1e4). Adding links cost entries
// and closures one digit each. What was given up the first two times was
// headroom nothing can reach; this time it is headroom nothing has BEEN SEEN to
// reach, and the difference is written above rather than smoothed over.
//
// What makes this honest rather than merely clever: if a field DID reach its
// ceiling the packing would silently corrupt its neighbours, so it does not pack
// at all - it returns -1, that pair-and-length is counted as overflowed, left
// out of every total, and named in the footer. A number that cannot be trusted
// is never shown as one that can.
//
// Worst and links are the deliberate exceptions: they CLAMP instead of taking
// the whole pair-and-length down with them. A single window that cost a hundred
// entries is past every question this table is asked - no ladder survives it and
// the difference between 100 and 140 changes no decision - so it is shown as 99
// rather than destroying four good numbers standing next to it.
//
// Links clamps for a sharper reason, and the asymmetry is the whole argument.
// The -1 is a sentinel for the WHOLE row: a hundred links would erase Wins,
// Entries, Avg, Avg +1σ, Red, Worst and Pairs, and take the row out of the
// ranking with them. Clamping costs only the difference between 99 links and
// 140, which no decision here rests on.
//
// That argument originally ran "Links decides nothing, so it must not be able
// to delete the fields that decide everything". THAT LEG IS GONE - the Links,
// then Score ranking makes this column something the table is sorted on. The
// conclusion survives and is in fact stronger. A row that stacked a hundred
// links is the worst row in the table on precisely the column now doing the
// ranking, and the right thing to do with the worst row is SHOW it at the
// bottom, not delete it. A sentinel would remove exactly the row the ranking
// was switched on to find.
//
// What the clamp costs once the column is ranked on, stated so it is not
// discovered: a pair-and-length at 99 links and one at 400 are indistinguishable
// here, so two rows can tie at the bottom that are not really equal. At the
// bottom of the table that changes nothing anybody acts on.
PACK_W  = 100            // worst window, the low two digits - a clamp, not a ceiling
PACK_D  = 100            // links, the next two - also a clamp, not a ceiling
PACK_R  = 10000          // red windows, the next four
PACK_C  = 10000          // closures, the next four
PACK_E  = 9000           // entries, the top four - capped so the total stays under 2^53

// Where each field sits, and every one of these is the product of the widths
// below it. Written out rather than multiplied so the layout can be read off
// the numbers.
//
// INTEGER literals, not 1e12 and friends. The unpacking below builds its
// exactness on int(v) % POS_x being an INTEGER modulo; a float constant would
// quietly turn every one of those into a float modulo on values reaching 9e15,
// which is exactly the hair's width the comment above unpW warns about.
POS_D = 100              // PACK_W
POS_R = 10000            // PACK_W * PACK_D
POS_C = 100000000        // PACK_W * PACK_D * PACK_R
POS_E = 1000000000000    // PACK_W * PACK_D * PACK_R * PACK_C

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Signal settings - mirrors of maRejection.pine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MA Length is absent on purpose: it is what this file sweeps. Everything else
// must hold the values maRejection.pine holds, or the table ranks lengths for a
// strategy nobody is trading.
gSig  = 'Signal (must match MAR1)'
gFilt = 'Filters (must match MAR1)'

maType = input.string('EMA', 'MA Type', options = ['SMA', 'EMA', 'WMA', 'RMA'], group = gSig)

// A string rather than input.source, and the difference is not cosmetic. An
// input.source is bound to the series of the CHART's symbol, so passing one into
// request.security would feed every pair the price of whatever chart this script
// happens to sit on.
srcName = input.string('ohlc4', 'Source', options = ['close', 'open', 'high', 'low', 'hl2', 'hlc3', 'ohlc4'], group = gSig, display = display.none)

trendBars = input.int(3, 'Bars that must close on the trend side of MA', minval = 2, maxval = 10, group = gSig, display = display.none)
trendSep  = input.float(0.30, 'Min distance of trend closes from MA (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
wickRatio = input.float(0.60, 'Min wick share of candle range', minval = 0.30, maxval = 0.95, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
touchTol  = input.float(0.30, 'Almost-reached tolerance (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
atrLen    = input.int(3, 'ATR length', minval = 2, group = gSig, display = display.none)

allowBodyInside    = input.bool(true, 'Accept small body fully on the trend side', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowLongWick      = input.bool(true, 'Accept wick longer than its running average', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowCounterCandle = input.bool(true, 'Accept a single counter-trend candle', group = gSig, display = display.none)

// The two-candle module. Constants rather than inputs, for the reason spelled
// out in maRejection.pine: an input declared mid-file shifts every setting under
// it on every chart already carrying the script. These three must match the
// block of the same name in the other two files - CFGSUM covers inputs only, so
// nothing checks them for you.
DC_ON   = true
DC_BACK = 4
DC_REF  = 'Close'

// --- MODULE: flat filter ----------------------------------------------------
// Stand aside while price is walking a long way and arriving nowhere. Measured
// as an efficiency ratio - net displacement over the path actually walked, over
// flatLen bars - with two thresholds so the verdict cannot flicker, and a tail
// of flatWait bars after it clears so the first breakout candle is skipped.
//
// It suppresses the DETECTED signal, exactly where the hours filter does, so an
// open window keeps its entries and its rung and simply stands still until the
// flat ends: the ladder is DEFERRED, never abandoned, and the streak does not
// move while it is paused.
//
// The state machine lives inside pairState, in the shared block, because the
// verdict belongs to the PAIR - it reads no moving average at all. That is what
// makes it affordable here: ONE verdict per pair, shared by all fifteen lengths,
// rather than fifteen verdicts about one stretch of price.
//
// AND IT HAS TO BE ON WHILE SWEEPING if it is on while trading. This file
// decides which MA lengths are best; measure them without the filter and the
// lengths it recommends are the best ones for a strategy you are not running.
//
// THE FIVE INPUTS THEMSELVES ARE DECLARED AT THE FOOT of the file, beside
// chainWithin and deepAt and for the identical reason - see THE LINKS COLUMN
// there. Fifty pair slots sit between here and there, and TradingView maps a
// saved setting onto an input by POSITION: declared here they would rotate the
// entire watchlist, declared at the foot they cost nobody a reset. The MACHINE
// that reads them still sits below their declaration either way, because
// pairState() - which is where flatPath below lives - is itself declared after
// the foot of the input list.
//
// What the module does, and why it is an efficiency ratio rather than any width
// test, is written out in full in maRejection.pine. The five values must stay
// identical there, here, in mar1Gates.pine, mar1GatesScanner.pine,
// mar1Scanner.pine and maSweep.pine. Nothing checks that for you.
// --- MODULE: flat filter ------------------------------------------------ END

refLen = input.int(200, 'Average window (candles)', minval = 10, maxval = 200, group = gSig, display = display.none)

minPips              = input.float(1.0, 'Min wick length (pips)', minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
cooldown             = input.int(1, 'Min bars between signals', minval = 1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
perDirectionCooldown = input.bool(true, 'Cooldown per direction', group = gFilt, display = display.none)
gateMode             = input.string('Max of both', 'Streak gate', options = ['Off', 'Manual', 'Distribution', 'Max of both'], group = gFilt)

// The most important filter in this file, and not for the usual reason. A sweep
// over the whole day answers "which length is best across hours you do not
// trade". Narrow these to the session you actually sit in first.
startHour       = input.int(4, "Start indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
endHour         = input.int(16, "End indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)

// OFF, and this time it can stay off, because the job that kept forcing it
// on has somewhere else to live. This flag narrows the SAMPLE of the shared
// block - it runs before the streak is counted, so it moves the
// distribution, the gate threshold and every total in the ranking. The
// statistics are all-hours by decision (2026-08-15, final form), so it is
// off and the ranking sees everything.
//
// What used to force it on - the PAIRS table matching mar1Scanner.pine - no
// longer needs it: the table is drawn from the SECOND machine in the tail of
// sweepOne, which applies the 'Trading hours' window to its own signals
// exactly the way the scanner's hours filter does. See the header.
//
// Turning this ON narrows the ranking to the indicator hours on top of
// that, which is a different experiment, not a stricter cockpit. Leave it
// off unless the ranking itself is the thing being narrowed.
restrictToHours = input.bool(false, 'Fire signals only inside those hours', group = gFilt)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Cost
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gScan = 'Cost'

// 20000 rather than 0, and the change is about REPRODUCIBILITY rather than cost.
// It is worth reading before setting it back, because the old default was not
// wrong - it answered a different question.
//
// What 0 buys, and it is real: maRejection.pine builds its streak distribution
// from the ENTIRE loaded history, so a window here means a different sample, a
// different percentile, and therefore a different gate threshold - and the gate
// is what decides whether a signal becomes an ENTRY or merely a warning. A
// shorter history does not just blur the statistic, it changes which candles
// were counted as entries at all. At 0 this file measures exactly what the chart
// measures.
//
// What 0 costs, and it is why the default moved: how much history TradingView
// hands out is not a setting and not a constant. It depends on the plan, the
// timeframe, how far the chart has been scrolled, and what the server felt like
// returning. Two runs of this table an hour apart can therefore disagree with
// each other, and there is no way to tell that from a run where something
// actually changed. A calibration table nobody can re-run and reproduce is a
// table whose numbers cannot be checked - and this file exists precisely to have
// its numbers copied into the headers of mar1Gates.pine.
//
// So the default is now a FIXED window. Every run sees the same 20000 bars per
// pair, the same gate threshold comes out of them, and two runs differ only when
// something real differed. The footer still prints CAPPED AT ... BARS PER PAIR
// on every run, so the window is never silent.
//
// 20000 and not something smaller because it is the maxval - the longest window
// that can be asked for, and past what most plans return anyway, so on a plan
// that hands out less this setting changes nothing at all and the two defaults
// are identical in effect.
//
// SET IT BACK TO 0 when the question is "what does the chart actually do",
// rather than "which of these fifteen lengths is best". Those are different
// questions and the gate threshold is what separates them.
//
// It remains the LAST lever to reach for when this script times out, behind
// unticking pairs and raising the timeframe, because it is still the only one of
// the three that changes the answer rather than the cost.
//
// AND IT IS BACK TO 0 (2026-08-15), which retires the reproducibility argument
// above in favour of a louder one. maRejection.pine builds its distribution
// over the WHOLE loaded history and has no window of its own, so any cap here
// gives the two files a different percentile, a different gate threshold and
// therefore a different pair armed - which is exactly the disagreement the
// hours filter above was just turned on to end. Two runs of this table an hour
// apart can now differ with nothing saying why, and that is the price of the
// cockpit and the chart showing the same machine. Set it back to 20000 when
// the table is being read as a calibration sheet rather than as a cockpit, and
// read the footer, which prints CAPPED AT whenever it is not 0.
scanBars = input.int(0, 'Bars of history per pair (0 = all)', minval = 0, maxval = 20000, group = gScan,
     tooltip = 'A FIXED window, so two runs of this table can be compared against each other. How much history TradingView hands out varies with the plan, the timeframe and how far the chart has been scrolled, so at 0 the same settings can produce different numbers an hour apart with nothing saying why - and this table exists to have its numbers copied into other files.\n\nSet it to 0 when you want what the chart itself sees: maRejection.pine builds its streak distribution from the whole loaded history, so a window here gives a different gate threshold and therefore a different set of entries. That is the honest setting for "what does the chart do" and the wrong one for "which length is best".\n\nThe footer says CAPPED AT whenever this is not 0.', display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The slots
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Twenty-one of the 40 request calls TradingView allows, and there are now
// TWO ceilings to keep in mind rather than one. The variable budget is the old
// one: twenty-one slots at thirty-nine values is 819 names against a limit of
// 1200, which puts the hard maximum near TWENTY-NINE slots - still above the
// twenty-two the old fifty-two-wide tuple allowed.
//
// THE SECOND IS THE TOKEN BUDGET, and it is the one this file has actually
// hit. TradingView caps the COMPILED code at 100256 tokens (CE10117), and at
// twenty-five slots this file crossed it - 102855 - the moment the day-closes
// table arrived (2026-08-16). Four slots were cut the same day to get under
// it, and they came back the day after, because REMOVING THE ROTATION JOURNAL
// bought about 17% - rather more than the four slots had cost. The state after
// both changes is roughly 84000 tokens, which is an estimate extrapolated from
// the one real reading above and not a compiler's answer: source tokens fell
// 18% against the file that measured 102855, and the ratio is assumed to hold.
//
// WHAT ONE SLOT COSTS, so the next decision is arithmetic rather than a guess:
// about 1.5% of the file - its request.security line of thirty-nine names, its
// stashPair, its rotWatch, its rotOne and its pubDigit, each of the last three
// carrying a fifteen-argument pickN call, plus its two inputs and its ternary.
//
// IF IT OVERFLOWS AGAIN the levers are, cheapest first: cut slots (1.5% each),
// or lower SWEEP_N, which is dearer per step and changes the answer. Comments
// are NOT a lever: the compiler strips them before it counts, and a tooltip is
// one string token however long it reads.
//
// THE FOUR EMPTY SLOTS CAME AND WENT IN ONE DAY (added and removed
// 2026-08-16, user decision). At fifty-two values per slot they stopped
// fitting the variable budget, and they were pure runtime the whole time: an
// unticked slot still runs its fifteen machines on the chart's own symbol,
// because a request.security call cannot be made conditional.
//
// WHAT THE REMOVAL COSTS ONCE: EIGHT INPUTS LEFT THE MIDDLE OF THE LIST, and
// TradingView maps saved settings by POSITION - so on a chart that carried
// the twenty-five-slot version, every setting BELOW this block reads the
// value of the one eight places LATER. Reset settings once after pasting -
// the defaults in this file are the working setup.
//
// Blank or unticked means "skip this slot" - it still spends a call, because
// Pine cannot make one conditional, but it is pointed at the chart's own
// symbol, which is already loaded, and its numbers are thrown away.
//
// These tick boxes are the TIME lever and only that. An unticked slot still
// declares its thirty-nine names, so it saves nothing at compile time - it only stops
// that pair's history from being walked. It does leave the rotation too: an
// unticked pair cannot close a win, so it cannot end a round either.
//
// The defaults are the same watchlist mar1Scanner.pine ships with, in the same
// order, so the two files describe the same set of pairs out of the box.
//
// display = display.none on every slot keeps fifty widgets out of the status
// line. The footer of the table says how many pairs were actually scanned.
gList1 = 'Pairs 1-10'
gList2 = 'Pairs 11-20'
gList3 = 'Pairs 21'

use01 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 1',  inline = 'p01', group = gList1, display = display.none,
     tooltip = 'The tick is the off switch, and on this file it is also the main performance lever: each ticked slot is fifteen state machines over that pair\'s whole history.')
sym01 = input.symbol('FX:EURUSD', '', inline = 'p01', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use02 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 2',  inline = 'p02', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym02 = input.symbol('FX:GBPUSD', '', inline = 'p02', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use03 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 3',  inline = 'p03', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym03 = input.symbol('FX:USDCAD', '', inline = 'p03', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use04 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 4',  inline = 'p04', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym04 = input.symbol('FX:USDCHF', '', inline = 'p04', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use05 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 5',  inline = 'p05', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym05 = input.symbol('FX:USDJPY', '', inline = 'p05', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use06 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 6',  inline = 'p06', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym06 = input.symbol('FX:AUDUSD', '', inline = 'p06', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use07 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 7',  inline = 'p07', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym07 = input.symbol('FX:EURGBP', '', inline = 'p07', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use08 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 8',  inline = 'p08', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym08 = input.symbol('FX:EURCHF', '', inline = 'p08', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use09 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 9',  inline = 'p09', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym09 = input.symbol('FX:EURJPY', '', inline = 'p09', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use10 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 10', inline = 'p10', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym10 = input.symbol('FX:EURAUD', '', inline = 'p10', group = gList1, display = display.none)

use11 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 11', inline = 'p11', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym11 = input.symbol('FX:EURCAD', '', inline = 'p11', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use12 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 12', inline = 'p12', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym12 = input.symbol('FX:GBPAUD', '', inline = 'p12', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use13 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 13', inline = 'p13', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym13 = input.symbol('FX:GBPJPY', '', inline = 'p13', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use14 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 14', inline = 'p14', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym14 = input.symbol('FX:GBPCHF', '', inline = 'p14', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use15 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 15', inline = 'p15', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym15 = input.symbol('FX:GBPCAD', '', inline = 'p15', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use16 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 16', inline = 'p16', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym16 = input.symbol('FX:CHFJPY', '', inline = 'p16', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use17 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 17', inline = 'p17', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym17 = input.symbol('FX:CADCHF', '', inline = 'p17', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use18 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 18', inline = 'p18', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym18 = input.symbol('FX:CADJPY', '', inline = 'p18', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use19 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 19', inline = 'p19', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym19 = input.symbol('FX:AUDJPY', '', inline = 'p19', group = gList2, display = display.none)
use20 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 20', inline = 'p20', group = gList2, display = display.none)
sym20 = input.symbol('FX:AUDCHF', '', inline = 'p20', group = gList2, display = display.none)

use21 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 21', inline = 'p21', group = gList3, display = display.none)
sym21 = input.symbol('FX:AUDCAD', '', inline = 'p21', group = gList3, display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The Links column - and why these two sit here, at the very bottom
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// They belong to the 'Sweep output' group and every other member of that group
// is declared four hundred lines above. They are down here anyway, and the
// reason is not taste.
//
// TradingView maps SAVED settings BY POSITION. An input inserted into the middle
// of the list shifts every input after it, and after it here are forty-two
// pair inputs - twenty-one tick boxes and twenty-one symbols. Adding these next to
// redAt, where they read best, would silently rotate the entire watchlist. So
// new inputs go at the END of the file, always. What that costs is only the
// order of two fields inside one dialog group, because the order of fields
// within a group follows the order of first appearance in the FILE. That is a
// property of the platform, and this project has already shipped one input this
// way (mar1GatesScanner.pine's logToPanel sits at the foot of that file for the
// same reason).
//
// They still have to be declared BEFORE sweepOne, which reads them - Pine has no
// use-before-declaration. sym21 is the last input in the file and sweepOne is
// the next thing after this block, so this is the one spot that satisfies both.
//
// display.none on both, like almost everything else here: the legend prints an
// indicator's arguments in declaration order, and three bare numbers is already
// the most this file is willing to put there.

// The threshold this column calls DEEP, and it is deliberately its own setting
// rather than a second reader of redAt - so the Links question (did two deep
// windows on one pair happen close together) can be tuned separately from the
// Red question (how often did a window get close to, or reach, disaster).
//
// The cost of two thresholds, stated plainly: Red and Links describe DIFFERENT
// populations of windows unless these two settings are equal. Set them equal
// and they describe the same one, and Links becomes a strict refinement of Red -
// "of the windows Red counted, how many arrived on the heels of another".
deepAt = input.int(3, 'A window is DEEP at this many entries', minval = 2, maxval = 50, group = gOut, display = display.none,
     tooltip = 'How deep a window has to go before the Links column will pair it with another. Set it equal to the Red threshold to make the two columns describe the same set of windows - then Links reads as a refinement of Red rather than as a separate question.')

// How long after a deep window closes another deep window may open on THAT SAME
// PAIR and still be called a follower.
//
// THE DEFAULT IS 180 AND IT IS NOT THE 2 FROM closed-chains.js. This is the one
// number on this column that cannot be copied across from the reference script,
// and copying it is exactly the mistake that would make the column useless.
//
// The reference reads the whole watchlist on ONE timeline: every pair and every
// gate config together, so a deep window can be followed by a deep window on a
// different pair a minute later. Measured on checks/log-0208.csv, windows arrive
// on that merged timeline about every 15.6 minutes, and a two-minute tolerance
// is a sensible fraction of it.
//
// This column reads ONE pair at a time. On a single machine windows arrive about
// every 1370 minutes - eighty-eight times further apart, because that is how many
// machines were merged. The per-pair equivalent of the reference's two minutes is
// therefore around 2 x 88 = 176 minutes, and 180 is that number rounded. At 2 the
// column is not strict, it is DEAD: on the measured log it reads zero on every
// machine at every threshold, and would go on reading zero up to a tolerance of
// sixteen hours.
//
// So the tooltip has to say, and does, that this number is NOT comparable to
// --within in the script. Comparing them is comparing a merged timeline to a
// single one.
chainWithin = input.int(180, 'A follower may open this many minutes after the close', minval = 1, maxval = 480, group = gOut, display = display.none,
     tooltip = 'The gap the Links column allows between one deep window closing and the next deep window opening ON THE SAME PAIR. Shallow windows in the gap are ignored.\n\nThis is NOT the same number as --within in checks/closed-chains.js and must not be copied from it. That script merges every pair onto one timeline, where windows arrive roughly every 16 minutes and two minutes is a tight tolerance. One pair on its own sees a window every 23 hours or so, so the equivalent tolerance scales by the number of pairs - around 180 minutes on a full watchlist. Set it to 2 and the column reads zero everywhere, which looks like a finding and is not.\n\nminval is 1 rather than 0 because the smallest gap this can ever measure is one bar: an entry taken on the bar that closed a window belongs to the window it closed.')

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Flat filter
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Declared here for the same reason chainWithin and deepAt are: forty-two pair
// slots sit between the top of the file and here, and TradingView maps a saved
// setting onto an input by POSITION, so anything declared above them would
// rotate the whole watchlist on every chart already carrying this script.
// Appending here costs nobody anything. See MODULE: flat filter, back near the
// two-candle module, for what this suppresses and why it is an efficiency
// ratio.
//
// These five must hold the same values in maRejection.pine, mar1Gates.pine,
// mar1GatesScanner.pine, mar1Scanner.pine, maSweep.pine and maSweepPairs.pine.
// Nothing checks that for you.
gFlat = 'Flat filter'

// Left VISIBLE, like the streak gate and the hours filter: with it on, a
// stretch of candles carrying no markers means 'nothing was allowed to fire'
// rather than 'nothing triggered', and reading the chart without knowing which
// is reading fiction.
flatOn = input.bool(true, 'Stand aside while flat', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'Suppresses the DETECTED signal, in exactly the place the hours filter does. An open window keeps its entries and its rung and stands still until the flat ends - the ladder is deferred, never abandoned - and the streak does not move while it is paused. Untick to get the behaviour this file had before the filter existed.')
flatLen = input.int(8, 'Bars measured', minval = 2, maxval = 500, group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'How far back the efficiency ratio looks: net displacement over these bars divided by the path actually walked. Shorter reacts sooner and calls more stretches flat; longer only notices the long ones.', display = display.none)
flatLo = input.float(0.2, 'Enter flat below  /  leave above', minval = 0.01, maxval = 1.0, step = 0.01, inline = 'fl', group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'Two thresholds, and the second must be the higher of the two. The state enters flat below the first and leaves only above the second - with a single threshold the verdict flickers bar by bar around it and windows end up half blocked, which is worse than no filter at all. On the ratio itself, 1.0 is a straight line and 0.0 is a walk that ended where it started.', display = display.none)
flatHi = input.float(0.45, '', minval = 0.01, maxval = 1.0, step = 0.01, inline = 'fl', group = gFlat, display = display.none)
flatWait = input.int(0, 'Bars to keep standing aside after it ends', minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = gFlat,
     tooltip = 'The candle that breaks a range is exactly the wick-rejection candle this strategy likes, and it is also the one most often taken straight back. 0 resumes on the breakout candle itself.', display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Rotation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The inputs this file adds over the sweeper core it was cloned from.
// Declared at the foot of the input list for the same reason the flat filter
// is - TradingView maps saved settings by POSITION, so everything this file
// ever adds lands BELOW this line, never between older inputs - and above
// pairState(), which reads it.
//
// THERE IS NO 'ACTIVE MA LENGTH' HERE ANY MORE, and its absence is the whole
// point of the file's second version. The traded length used to be typed in
// beside the ranking that judged it, with nothing checking the two agreed;
// now it IS the ranking's answer, recomputed every time a series closes on a
// win. What stays a human's job is the ONE step Pine cannot do: copying that
// number into maRejection.pine, which is where real entries are taken.
gRot = 'Rotation'

// Which position of the RANKING becomes the traded length. 1 since
// 2026-08-15 (user request) - the ranking's own top row, which is also the
// calibration answer; 2 was the standing choice while the two were kept
// deliberately apart. The ► prefix in the table marks it, the ROTATION row
// names it; when fewer rows than it asks for have enough wins there is no
// pick at all, the row says so, and the pairs table stays empty rather than
// trading a length nobody vouched for.
pickPos = input.int(1, 'Ranking position to trade', minval = 1, maxval = 15, group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'The row of the ranking that becomes the traded MA - 1 is the top row, 2 the second. It is re-read at every round boundary, so this is the only place the traded length is chosen. If fewer rows than this have enough wins, there is no pick, and the pairs table below stays empty until there is.')

// The space switch. Ticked, the twenty-row ranking - header, columns, footer
// and verdict line included - is not drawn, and the table shrinks to the
// PICK ROW (the one ranked position the rotation is read from, kept with its
// own column headers), the ROTATION row, the PAIRS table and the LAST WIN
// line, because unwritten table cells take no space. Everything is still
// COMPUTED: the pick and the active row all come from the same ranking
// whether it is drawn or not, so hiding it costs nothing but the ink - and
// saves none of the runtime, for the same reason.
// Ticked by DEFAULT since 2026-08-15 (user request): the cockpit is the
// working view, the full ranking is the occasional one. Declared at the very
// end of the input list for the position rule every other foot-of-file input
// already follows.
hideSweep = input.bool(true, 'Hide the ranking table (cockpit only)', group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'Draws only the pick row of the ranking, the ROTATION row, the PAIRS table and the LAST WIN line - the compact cockpit. The ranking is still computed, so the pick stays live; the other fourteen rows are just not drawn. Untick to get the full table back.', display = display.none)

// WHEN A ROUND ENDS, and there is now exactly one way that it can.
//
// A window that cost MORE than this many entries recomputes the ranking on the
// spot. Any watched pair, on the traded length, inside the trading window. At
// 3 the ordinary band is one to three entries and the fourth is the alarm; the
// cheap closes that came before it are counted, reported, and change nothing.
//
// IT WAS 4 FOR ONE DAY (2026-08-15 to 2026-08-16, user request). Three rotates
// on a shallower run, so the traded length is re-read more often and the number
// has to be retyped into maRejection.pine more often - which is the whole of
// what lowering it costs.
//
// THE COUNT-OF-PAIRS TRIGGER IS GONE (user decision, 2026-08-15, the same day
// it arrived). It ended a round once three distinct pairs of a snapshotted set
// had closed a win, and the effect was a rotation every few minutes on a
// watchlist doing nothing wrong. The whole set machinery went with it: no
// snapshot, no per-slot "has this one paid yet", no deadlock guard for a set
// smaller than the target.
//
// Set it to 1 to rotate on any window that took two entries; set it to 50 to
// rotate almost never. There is no value that switches the rotation off - the
// traded length IS the ranking's answer, and a file that stopped re-reading it
// would just be showing a stale one.
//
// EVERY SETTING BELOW THIS LINE MOVED UP ONE when the count input was deleted,
// and TradingView maps saved settings by POSITION - so check the Rotation and
// Trading hours groups once, after pasting this version over the previous one.
// Nothing else in the dialog is affected: the pair slots are all above.
rotDeepAt = input.int(3, 'Recompute the MA after a win costing more than', minval = 1, maxval = 50, group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'A single window that cost MORE than this many entries ends the round and re-reads the ranking, on the spot. It is the ONLY thing that ends a round - cheap closes are counted and reported and move nothing. At 3 the ordinary band is one to three entries and a four-entry window rotates immediately. Independent of the Red and Links thresholds in Sweep output: those describe the table, this one moves the traded length.')

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Trading hours
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE HOURS THE SECOND MACHINE TRADES IN, and the one group in this file
// that reaches no ranking total anywhere. That is its whole reason for
// existing.
//
// 'Fire signals only inside those hours', up in Filters, narrows the shared
// block and therefore the RANKING; it is off, and the header says why. THESE
// two inputs gate the signals of the SECOND machine in the tail of sweepOne
// instead - the one the PAIRS table, the proposal and the armed/held counts
// are drawn from - exactly the way the scanner's own hours filter gates its
// machine. The table's streaks, gates and windows are therefore measured
// inside these hours BY CONSTRUCTION, and there is no row filter anywhere:
// what is on screen is a machine, not a censored view of one.
//
// Three consequences, stated so they read as design rather than as bugs:
//
//   a window can only OPEN inside these hours, so the 'Open since' stamp of
//   every armed, held and running row lands inside them. A pre-armed stamp
//   can land at any hour - it is the bar the pair reached the white step, a
//   reading of the streak rather than a signal - and the scanner prints the
//   same thing;
//   an ARMED pair reads HELD while the CLOCK is outside the hours - visible,
//   counted, never proposed. On an evening or a weekend the last trading
//   day's states stay on screen, because machine state persists on its own;
//   a close outside the hours ends no round - see rotWatch. The totals are
//   all-hours, but the round is a trading event and only fires on closes
//   the window let through. A RUNNING window rides through the end of the
//   session: the hours stop entries, they do not close positions.
//
// IT IS A SECOND MACHINE, and that sentence used to say the opposite. While
// this group could only re-label rows of the all-hours machine, no filter
// could make the table match the scanner - a filter decides which rows are
// drawn and never what the numbers in them were measured over, and both
// attempts to pretend otherwise were reverted the day they were made. The
// hours now reach the machine at the SIGNAL, before the streak is counted,
// which is the only place a time window changes what the numbers mean.
//
// UTC+1, the same frame as the filter above and as every timestamp in this
// project. From is inclusive and To is exclusive, so 4 and 16 is 04:00 up to
// but not including 16:00, exactly as the other pair reads.
//
// Declared here, at the foot of the input list, for the position rule every
// other late input in this file follows - and above pairState(), which reads
// them.
gTrade = 'Trading hours'

tradeHoursOn = input.bool(true, 'Enter only inside these hours', group = gTrade,
     tooltip = 'The hours the SECOND state machine - the one the PAIRS table, the proposal and the armed/held counts are drawn from - may find signals in, exactly like the hours filter in the MAR1 scanner. The ranking above is always counted over the whole clock, whatever this says. Outside these hours an armed pair reads HELD and a close does not end a round. Untick to let that machine trade around the clock.')
tradeStart = input.int(4, 'From  /  to', minval = 0, maxval = 24, inline = 'th', group = gTrade,
     tooltip = 'UTC+1. From is inclusive, To is exclusive - 4 and 16 means 04:00 up to but not including 16:00.\n\nThese hours gate the SIGNALS of the machine behind the PAIRS table, so a window can only open inside them and every Open since stamp on an armed or running row lands inside them by construction. A pre-armed stamp can land at any hour - it is a reading of the streak, and the scanner prints the same thing. On a weekend the table shows the last trading day, because machine state persists on its own.')
tradeEnd = input.int(16, '', minval = 0, maxval = 24, inline = 'th', group = gTrade)

// WHICH DAY the DAY CLOSES table is a record of. Off, and then it is the LAST
// trading day the loaded history reached, which is what a cockpit wants - the
// table clears at every window open and the latest one wins.
//
// TWO INPUTS RATHER THAN ONE, and that is what a date picker costs. input.time
// draws a calendar, which is the widget this deserves, and a calendar has no
// way to spell "no day chosen" - every value it can hold is a real date. So the
// tick box carries the off state and the calendar carries nothing else.
//
// It reads as a CALENDAR DAY in UTC+1, and the trading window sits inside one:
// 4 to 16 opens and closes on the same date. Pick any time on the day you want
// - only the date is read - and prefer a time in the middle of it, because a
// pick at 23:30 UTC lands on the next day once the hour of UTC+1 is added.
//
// It works on any day the CHART still holds, and no further: the rotation is
// replayed from the first loaded bar, so a day inside that history reconstructs
// completely, morning included. A date older than the loaded history draws an
// empty table, and the header says so rather than leaving it looking broken.
// The name is jrnDayOn and not clsDayOn because it was the JOURNAL's switch
// first - the journal is gone (2026-08-16) and the day it chose outlived it.
// Renaming it would touch a dozen sites for no behaviour, so the label was
// changed and the identifier was not.
jrnDayOn = input.bool(false, 'Show one chosen day', group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'Off, the day closes table follows the latest trading day in the loaded history and clears when the next one opens. On, it holds the day picked below and stops there.', display = display.none)
// A RAW EPOCH LITERAL rather than a timestamp() call, and it is the same
// answer mar1Scanner.pine, mar1Gates.pine and mar1GatesScanner.pine all
// reached before this file did: input.time demands a CONST int and
// timestamp(y, m, d, h, mi) returns a SIMPLE one, so the readable spelling is
// refused outright (CE10123, reported here 2026-08-16).
//
// 1767265200000 is 2026-01-01 12:00 UTC+1 - midday of the first day of the
// year, in the frame this file reads every date in, so the picker opens on a
// harmless date rather than on 1970. It is a DEFAULT and nothing else: the
// calendar overwrites it the moment a day is chosen.
jrnDayPick = input.time(1767265200000, 'That day', group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'The date is what is read - the time of day is ignored, since the trading window opens and closes inside one calendar date. Pick a time in the middle of the day: the frame is UTC+1, so a pick late in the UTC evening lands on the following date. Only days the chart still has bars for can be shown.', display = display.none)

// THE DAY CLOSES table - every window the day closed, one per line, and since
// 2026-08-16 the ONLY table of the day's record. It began as the unabridged
// half of a rotation journal that kept four closes per row; once it existed
// the journal was four records of what this holds in full, so the journal
// went and this stayed.
//
// Four inputs, and three of them are the "hide and continue" mechanism the
// table needs to be readable at all. A busy day closes more windows than any
// table can be tall, so the list runs DOWN a block and then WRAPS SIDEWAYS
// into the next one - the same layout the ranking uses - and what still does
// not fit goes onto a page. Nothing is ever dropped silently: the title always
// says which records are on screen and how many there are in total.
//
// Declared at the very end of the input list, where every late input in this
// file goes: TradingView maps saved settings by POSITION, and appending after
// the last one shifts nothing above it.
hideCloses = input.bool(true, 'Hide the day closes', group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'The table of every window that closed on the traded length today - time, pair, entries, the MA it closed on, and whether it was the one that ended the round. Only CLOSED windows can be in it: the counter that carries a window\'s cost moves on the close and nowhere else, so a pair sitting on entries right now is invisible until it pays.', display = display.none)

// Only the left and the two centres: the cockpit sits on the middle right by
// default, and offering this table a seat there would be offering to stack the
// two. Two tables in the same position overlap, and nothing in Pine negotiates
// that.
//
// BOTTOM CENTRE by default since 2026-08-16, and it was MIDDLE LEFT for a
// few hours that same day. The three defaults below are lifted off a working
// chart rather than chosen here, which is why they are not the round numbers
// they used to be - and why they move whenever the chart they were lifted
// from does.
clsPos = input.string('Bottom center', 'Day closes position',
     options = ['Top left', 'Bottom left', 'Middle left', 'Top center', 'Bottom center'], group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'Two tables, two positions, independent of each other: the cockpit above and this one. Two tables in the same position overlap, and nothing in Pine negotiates that.', display = display.none)

// How tall one column of the list is before it wraps into the next. Four
// blocks stand side by side, so this times four is what fits on one page -
// twelve gives forty-eight, which is what a chart at the working text size
// holds along the bottom without the table climbing into the price.
// Twenty-five was the first default and it is taller than a screen; fifteen
// was the second and it fits a table standing at the middle left rather than
// at the foot.
clsPerBlock = input.int(12, 'Closes per block (wraps sideways past this)', minval = 5, maxval = 45, group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'The list fills a block downwards, oldest at the top, then starts the next block to its right. Up to four blocks stand side by side, so this is a quarter of what one page holds. Raise it for a taller, narrower table; lower it for a shorter, wider one.', display = display.none)

// What follows the four blocks. Page 1 is the oldest records of the day, and
// the title says which page of how many is on screen, so the tail is never
// invisible.
//
// THREE by DEFAULT, and that is a deliberate oddity rather than an
// off-by-one: at twelve per block a page holds forty-eight closes, so page 3
// is the LATE part of a busy day - the slice worth looking at while trading.
// A quiet day, or a chart just loaded, has no page 3, and the table then
// draws the last page it does have and notes the ask beside the page count -
// 'page 1 of 1 (asked 3)'. It was an amber PAGE 3 IS PAST THE END for a few
// hours on 2026-08-16 and stopped being one the same day (user request): a
// permanent alarm about a value the file itself ships with is an alarm
// nobody reads, and on an ordinary day it was the loudest thing on screen.
clsPage = input.int(3, 'Day closes page', minval = 1, maxval = 20, group = gRot,
     tooltip = 'Which slice of the day is drawn, when it closed more windows than four blocks can hold. Page 1 is the morning. A page past the end draws the last one instead and notes the ask beside the page count, so the two can never be confused.', display = display.none)

// request.security refuses an empty symbol, so a blank slot is pointed at the
// chart itself and filtered out later by the same blank test.
//
// Written out as plain ternaries rather than through a helper on purpose. The
// symbol argument must be a SIMPLE string, and Pine infers the qualifier of a
// user function's return value rather than taking it from the arguments - a
// helper here would very likely come back as series and be refused.
s01 = use01 and sym01 != '' ? sym01 : syminfo.tickerid
s02 = use02 and sym02 != '' ? sym02 : syminfo.tickerid
s03 = use03 and sym03 != '' ? sym03 : syminfo.tickerid
s04 = use04 and sym04 != '' ? sym04 : syminfo.tickerid
s05 = use05 and sym05 != '' ? sym05 : syminfo.tickerid
s06 = use06 and sym06 != '' ? sym06 : syminfo.tickerid
s07 = use07 and sym07 != '' ? sym07 : syminfo.tickerid
s08 = use08 and sym08 != '' ? sym08 : syminfo.tickerid
s09 = use09 and sym09 != '' ? sym09 : syminfo.tickerid
s10 = use10 and sym10 != '' ? sym10 : syminfo.tickerid
s11 = use11 and sym11 != '' ? sym11 : syminfo.tickerid
s12 = use12 and sym12 != '' ? sym12 : syminfo.tickerid
s13 = use13 and sym13 != '' ? sym13 : syminfo.tickerid
s14 = use14 and sym14 != '' ? sym14 : syminfo.tickerid
s15 = use15 and sym15 != '' ? sym15 : syminfo.tickerid
s16 = use16 and sym16 != '' ? sym16 : syminfo.tickerid
s17 = use17 and sym17 != '' ? sym17 : syminfo.tickerid
s18 = use18 and sym18 != '' ? sym18 : syminfo.tickerid
s19 = use19 and sym19 != '' ? sym19 : syminfo.tickerid
s20 = use20 and sym20 != '' ? sym20 : syminfo.tickerid
s21 = use21 and sym21 != '' ? sym21 : syminfo.tickerid

// Index at which the cumulative distribution of the streak histogram crosses
// perc. Identical to calculateIndex in maRejection.pine, including the inclusive
// comparison - a strict one slides the answer one bucket to the right.
calcIndex(int[] arr, float perc) =>
    total = 0.0
    for i = 1 to array.size(arr) - 1
        total += array.get(arr, i)
    if total == 0
        -1
    else
        cumSum = 0.0
        index = -1
        for i = 1 to array.size(arr) - 1
            cumSum += array.get(arr, i) / total * 100
            if cumSum >= perc and index == -1
                index := i
                break
        index

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The colour ladder, borrowed whole from mar1Scanner.pine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Both are copies, byte for byte, of the functions of the same names in
// mar1Scanner.pine - which are themselves the table half of streakColor in
// maRejection.pine. They are here because the PAIRS table below is that
// scanner's table, and a row that wore a colour of this file's own invention
// would be describing the same run as the chart in a different language.
//
// ladderStep turns a losing streak, measured against the anchor the run
// started on, into the step of the small diamond the chart drew for it.
// Step 0 is not a step: it means the streak gate is Off, no ladder exists,
// and nothing on the chart is wearing a ladder colour to echo.
//
// windowStep is the floor that keeps white and faded silver out of a table
// row: steps 1 and 7 both sit BELOW the gate, and a window that exists cannot
// honestly be below the gate that opened it. See the long note on both in
// mar1Scanner.pine - the reasoning is there and is not repeated here, but the
// two implementations must not drift apart, and nothing checks that for you.
ladderStep(int streak, int limit) =>
    limit <= 0 ? 0 :
     streak == limit - 1 ? 1 :
     streak == limit ? 2 :
     streak > limit ? (streak - limit < 2 ? 3 : streak - limit < 3 ? 4 : streak - limit < 4 ? 5 :
          streak - limit < 5 ? 6 : 8) :
     7

windowStep(int step) =>
    step <= 0 ? 0 : step == 1 or step == 7 ? 2 : step

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One MA length, on one pair, over that pair's whole loaded history
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MAR1 with the drawing, the statistics tables and the alerting removed. What is
// left is the smallest thing that can answer "how many windows did this length
// close on this pair, and what did they cost".
//
// The block order is load-bearing and mirrors the order the original executes
// in: the gate reads the streak BEFORE this bar is graded, the markers read it
// AFTER. Swapping those is how a gate ends up judging a signal by its own
// result.
//
// Everything MA-independent arrives as an argument rather than being recomputed.
// That is the whole reason forty lengths fit in one budget at all - see the
// header, and read it before raising the count again.
// The raw candle - close, open, high, low - is read directly, because a function
// called inside request.security evaluates in the REQUESTED symbol's context and
// those builtins already carry that pair's prices.
//
// sepVal arrives PRE-MULTIPLIED - atrVal * trendSep, computed once in
// pairState - because the ATR itself was only ever read here to make that
// product, forty times per pair for one answer. The historical [1] read of a
// function parameter is still allowed the same way it always was: a series
// argument keeps its history across the call.
//
// Returns TWO numbers: the five totals packed (see the packing block above),
// and the machine's live state packed the same way - see the ROTATION TAIL at
// the foot of the body. Both, on every length, is what the rotation costs:
// the totals are the ranking's raw material, the live value is the PAIRS
// table's, and which length's live value gets drawn is decided a thousand
// lines below by a ranking that cannot exist until every pair has answered.
sweepOne(simple int len, float srcVal, float sepVal, float bodyRef, float rangeSize,
     float bodySize, float upWick, float dnWick, float tolVal,
     bool isBull, bool isBear, bool wickRejectDown, bool wickRejectUp, bool smallBody,
     bool longWickDown, bool longWickUp, bool counterShort, bool counterLong,
     bool dcShort, bool dcLong, bool hoursOk, bool flatOk, bool tradeOk, bool active) =>
    // SYNC:BEGIN mar1-signal-core
    // From here to the SYNC:END line is the shared MAR1 state machine.
    // checks/block-sync.js compares this region byte-for-byte across the
    // registered files and then asserts the tail below the END marker only
    // READS the machine's variables. Edit the block in one file, run the
    // checker, carry the same bytes to the others.
    // switch, NOT the ?: chain this used to be. Pine evaluates EVERY branch of
    // a ternary, so the old spelling computed all four moving averages per
    // machine per bar and kept one - a thousand machines deep, the dominant
    // cost of the whole file. A switch runs only the selected branch, and
    // because maType is a simple input the SAME branch runs on every bar - the
    // series and its history are identical, only the three dead MAs are gone.
    // The compiler may warn about ta.* inside a conditional; that warning is
    // about conditions that change bar to bar, which this one cannot.
    maValue = switch maType
        'WMA' => ta.wma(srcVal, len)
        'EMA' => ta.ema(srcVal, len)
        'RMA' => ta.rma(srcVal, len)
        => ta.sma(srcVal, len)

    // THE TREND TEST, AS A ROLLING SUM RATHER THAN A LOOP (2026-08-12), and it
    // is the same test to the bit - not an approximation of it.
    //
    // It used to be `for i = 1 to trendBars`, reading atrVal[i], close[i] and
    // maValue[i] on every iteration. Nine history reads at a VARIABLE index per
    // machine per bar, and this file runs ONE MACHINE PER PAIR PER SWEPT
    // LENGTH - several hundred of them, and the exact product is worked out at
    // the head of each file - so that is the single most repeated piece of
    // arithmetic in the script. A variable index is also
    // the expensive kind: Pine can resolve a constant offset at compile time
    // and cannot resolve a loop counter, and it has to keep a deeper buffer for
    // every series read that way.
    //
    // The identity that makes the rewrite exact: the loop's term at i is
    //
    //     close[i] <= maValue[i] - atrVal[i] * trendSep
    //
    // which is the SAME EXPRESSION as below, evaluated i bars ago - every
    // series in it is read at the same offset. So the whole loop is
    // math.sum(term, trendBars) shifted back one bar, and math.sum keeps that
    // running incrementally instead of re-reading the window.
    //
    // The [1] is what makes the range 1..trendBars rather than 0..trendBars-1.
    // Drop it and the test starts including the bar being graded, which is a
    // different strategy.
    //
    // nz() covers the warmup, where math.sum is na until its window fills. The
    // loop reached past the start of history there and compared against na,
    // which is false, so it counted zero - nz gives the same zero and the same
    // false verdict. Nothing changes at the left edge of the chart.
    belowNow = close <= maValue - sepVal ? 1 : 0
    aboveNow = close >= maValue + sepVal ? 1 : 0

    trendDown = nz(math.sum(belowNow, trendBars)[1]) == trendBars
    trendUp   = nz(math.sum(aboveNow, trendBars)[1]) == trendBars

    bodyRejectDown = allowBodyInside and open <= maValue and smallBody and upWick > bodySize
    bodyRejectUp   = allowBodyInside and open >= maValue and smallBody and dnWick > bodySize

    rejectDown = rangeSize > 0 and high >= maValue - tolVal and close <= maValue and (wickRejectDown or bodyRejectDown or longWickDown)
    rejectUp   = rangeSize > 0 and low  <= maValue + tolVal and close >= maValue and (wickRejectUp or bodyRejectUp or longWickUp)

    triggerShort = trendDown and (rejectDown or counterShort or dcShort)
    triggerLong  = trendUp and (rejectUp or counterLong or dcLong)

    var int lastLongBar  = -100000
    var int lastShortBar = -100000
    canFireLong  = bar_index - lastLongBar >= cooldown
    canFireShort = bar_index - lastShortBar >= cooldown

    // flatOk sits beside hoursOk because it is the same kind of veto - "not now"
    // - and it lands in the same place: on the DETECTED signal, so a suppressed
    // one never reaches the streak histogram below. It arrives as an argument
    // for the same reason hoursOk does: it belongs to the PAIR, and pairState
    // measures it once for all forty lengths.
    buyFound  = triggerLong[1] and canFireLong and hoursOk and flatOk
    sellFound = triggerShort[1] and not triggerLong[1] and canFireShort and hoursOk and flatOk

    var int[] failHist    = array.new_int(16, 0)
    var int   curFailRun  = 0
    var int   streak      = 0
    var int   distCache   = -1
    var int   lockedLimit = 0

    gateThreshold = gateMode == 'Off' ? 0 :
         gateMode == 'Manual' ? minEntriesToStart :
         gateMode == 'Distribution' ? (distCache > 0 ? distCache : minEntriesToStart) :
         math.max(distCache, minEntriesToStart)

    gateOk = streak >= gateThreshold

    if streak == 0
        lockedLimit := gateThreshold
    colorLimit = gateMode == 'Off' ? 0 : lockedLimit

    buyTaken     = buyFound and gateOk
    sellTaken    = sellFound and gateOk
    gateArmedNow = gateMode != 'Off' and streak == gateThreshold - 1
    buyWarn      = buyFound and gateArmedNow
    sellWarn     = sellFound and gateArmedNow

    var int  entriesInWindow = 0
    var bool windowOpen      = false

    var int totClosures = 0
    var int totEntries  = 0
    var int worstWindow = 0
    var int totRed      = 0

    // The Links column's whole memory: when the current window opened, and when
    // the last DEEP window closed. Both are bar-opening stamps, and that
    // symmetry is a condition of correctness rather than a coincidence - the
    // difference between them is only a true gap while both ends sit on the same
    // grid. Swap either one for a bar-closing stamp and every gap moves by a bar
    // with nothing complaining.
    //
    // lastDeepMs starts at -1 rather than na so the "no deep window yet" test is
    // an integer comparison and not a na check - the first deep window of a run
    // has no predecessor and must not count as a follower.
    var int winOpenMs  = 0
    var int lastDeepMs = -1
    var int totLinks   = 0

    if barstate.isconfirmed and active
        if buyFound
            lastLongBar := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastShortBar := bar_index
        if sellFound
            lastShortBar := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastLongBar := bar_index

        // Doji is a miss on both sides, matching the original exactly.
        if not na(bodyRef) and (buyFound or sellFound)
            hit = (buyFound and isBull) or (sellFound and isBear)
            histChanged = false

            if hit
                curFailRun := 0
            else
                curFailRun += 1
                // ONCE A STREAK REACHES THE CATCH-ALL, NOTHING MOVES ANY MORE - and this
                // used to keep moving it, which is the bug this guard fixes rather than a
                // style choice.
                //
                // The transfer is what keeps a LIVE streak in exactly one bucket: as it grows
                // past n it is added to bucket n and taken back out of n-1. Bucket 15 is a
                // catch-all, so a streak that reaches 15 has ARRIVED and never leaves. With the
                // index clamped by math.min, the sixteenth failure ran the transfer AGAIN -
                // adding a second copy of one live streak to bucket 15 and taking a real one
                // OUT of bucket 14, which went NEGATIVE by one per extra failure.
                //
                // THE TOTAL WAS NEVER AFFECTED, and that is what made it silent for as long as
                // it was: the +1 and the -1 both sit inside the summed range 1..15, so the one
                // number anybody could have checked by hand stayed exactly right while the
                // shape underneath it did not.
                //
                // What it cost: calcIndex walks the cumulative distribution and takes the FIRST
                // crossing of the percentage. A negative bucket 14 drags that cumulative back
                // DOWN, so a crossing that belonged at 14 was missed and picked up at 15
                // instead - one rung too high on the gate threshold, in Distribution and in Max
                // of both. The gate then opens one loss LATER than the distribution says it
                // should. The error is one-sided and bounded: never earlier, and never any
                // bucket other than 14 -> 15.
                //
                // Worked example, 20 consecutive misses against 19 shorter streaks at 95%:
                // bucket 14 reads -4 and bucket 15 reads 6 where they should read 1 and 1, the
                // cumulative falls from 90% to 70% at bucket 14, and the answer comes back 15
                // instead of 14.
                //
                // The same five lines live in SIX files. maRejection.pine is the one where this
                // feeds the gate that decides real entries.
                if curFailRun <= 15
                    array.set(failHist, curFailRun, array.get(failHist, curFailRun) + 1)
                    array.set(failHist, curFailRun - 1, array.get(failHist, curFailRun - 1) - 1)
                    histChanged := true

            streak := hit ? 0 : streak + 1
            // Recomputed only when the histogram itself moved - on a hit, and
            // on a miss already past the catch-all bucket, calcIndex would
            // return exactly what distCache already holds.
            if histChanged
                distCache := calcIndex(failHist, basePercentage)

        buyFailed    = (buyTaken or buyWarn) and not isBull
        sellFailed   = (sellTaken or sellWarn) and not isBear
        greenDiamond = (buyFailed or sellFailed) and colorLimit > 0 and streak == colorLimit

        // Which successes close a window, exactly as the scanner reads it.
        buySet = successScope == 'All found signals' ? buyFound :
             successScope == 'Entries and warnings' ? (buyTaken or buyWarn) :
             buyTaken
        sellSet = successScope == 'All found signals' ? sellFound :
             successScope == 'Entries and warnings' ? (sellTaken or sellWarn) :
             sellTaken

        win        = (buySet and isBull) or (sellSet and isBear)
        entryTaken = buyTaken or sellTaken

        // Entries are counted before either reset can fire, so an entry taken on
        // the bar that closes a window belongs to the window it closed.
        if entryTaken
            entriesInWindow += 1
            // When this window OPENED, for the Links column.
            //
            // The guard is entriesInWindow == 1, tested AFTER the increment
            // above - deliberately NOT the `if not windowOpen` sitting on the
            // next line, which looks like the same test and is the smaller
            // diff. windowOpen is only ever set back to false in the `win`
            // branch, so after the first green diamond abandons a window it
            // stays true for the rest of the history and a guard hung on it
            // would never fire again. The entry counter is the one that
            // genuinely restarts, and the diamond restarts it too.
            //
            // Getting this wrong does not fail to compile. It makes every gap
            // read from a stale opening, which only ever makes gaps LOOK
            // longer, so the column silently under-counts - and its expected
            // value is already low.
            if entriesInWindow == 1
                winOpenMs := time
            if not windowOpen
                windowOpen := true

        if win
            totClosures += 1
            totEntries  += entriesInWindow
            worstWindow := math.max(worstWindow, entriesInWindow)

            // The tail, counted on the same event as everything else. A window
            // that reached the red threshold is one red window however much
            // further it ran - how far is the Worst column's job.
            //
            // It is counted HERE, on the close, and not the moment the fifth
            // entry is taken, and that is not an oversight: entries and closures
            // are both counted on the close too, so a window abandoned by a
            // green diamond or still open at the last bar contributes to none of
            // the three. Counting red on the entry instead would make it the one
            // number on the row describing a different set of windows from the
            // average standing next to it.
            if entriesInWindow >= redAt
                totRed += 1

            // The Links column, counted on the same event as everything else.
            //
            // The rule is closed-chains.js's WITHIN reading, narrowed to one
            // pair: this window is deep, and it OPENED no later than
            // chainWithin minutes after the last deep window CLOSED. Shallow
            // windows in between are ignored, and they are ignored for free -
            // lastDeepMs is only ever written here, on a deep close, so
            // anything cheaper in the gap simply never touches it.
            //
            // What this cannot see, and the tooltip says so: a deep window on
            // ANOTHER pair. The reference script counts those, this cannot -
            // one packed number crosses request.security per pair-and-length
            // and there is no shared timeline on the other side. The result is
            // a strict lower bound on the cross-pair count, because the next
            // deep window anywhere always opens no later than the next deep
            // window on this pair.
            if entriesInWindow >= deepAt
                if lastDeepMs >= 0 and winOpenMs - lastDeepMs <= chainWithin * 60000
                    totLinks += 1
                lastDeepMs := time

            windowOpen      := false
            entriesInWindow := 0
        else if greenDiamond
            windowOpen      := true
            entriesInWindow := 0
    // SYNC:END mar1-signal-core

    // -1 rather than a packed number the moment any field could corrupt its
    // neighbours. See the packing block: a total that cannot be trusted must not
    // be handed over as one that can.
    //
    // Worst and links are not in that test because they do not overflow - they
    // clamp. Both clamps are here rather than at the top so the two columns keep
    // their real values inside this function, where math.max and += are still
    // comparing and counting rather than saturating.
    //
    // Why links clamps and red does not, when both are counts of windows: red is
    // ranked on, links is not. See the packing block - a sentinel is a sentinel
    // for the WHOLE row, so a field nobody acts on must never be able to delete
    // the fields everybody acts on.
    packed = totEntries >= PACK_E or totClosures >= PACK_C or totRed >= PACK_R ? -1.0 :
         1.0 * totEntries * POS_E + totClosures * POS_C + totRed * POS_R +
         (totLinks < PACK_D ? totLinks : PACK_D - 1) * POS_D +
         (worstWindow < PACK_W ? worstWindow : PACK_W - 1)

    // --- ROTATION TAIL - this file only. It READS the shared machine and
    // never writes it: everything above this comment is the shared MAR1
    // block and must stay identical to the other six files, and the checker
    // asserts no write to the block's own names lands below the END marker.
    //
    // THE SECOND MACHINE (2026-08-15, the design that ended the day). The
    // shared block above runs ALL HOURS and feeds the totals - that is the
    // statistics, and nothing narrows it. The PAIRS table needs the states a
    // 4-16 trader actually faces, and no filter on rows or digits can make
    // those out of an all-hours machine, because the hours gate runs BEFORE
    // the streak is counted. So the tail runs a SECOND copy of the grading
    // with exactly one difference: a signal outside the Trading hours window
    // (tradeOk) is never found - which is what mar1Scanner.pine's machine
    // does with its own hours filter on.
    //
    // What is shared and what is owned: the GEOMETRY - triggerLong,
    // triggerShort, isBull, isBear, bodyRef - is read from the block, since
    // the MA does not change with the clock and both machines see the same
    // candidate signals. Every piece of STATE is this machine's own copy,
    // suffixed T2: the SYNC fence forbids writing the shared names, and the
    // whole point is a second, different history of the same candles.
    // flatOk is shared because the flat verdict belongs to the PAIR;
    // hoursOk is machine 1's business alone and is not read here.
    var int   lastLongT2  = -100000
    var int   lastShortT2 = -100000
    var int[] failHistT2  = array.new_int(16, 0)
    var int   curFailT2   = 0
    var int   streakT2    = 0
    var int   distT2      = -1
    var int   lockedT2    = 0
    var int   entriesT2   = 0
    var bool  windowT2    = false

    // THE TRADING MACHINE'S OWN CLOSURE COUNTERS (2026-08-16, user decision:
    // the day-closes record follows the machine being TRADED, not the
    // all-hours statistics). Cumulative, like the shared block's totals, and
    // for the same reason: request.security repeats its last value on a chart
    // bar the pair had no bar for, and a counter that merely repeats reads as
    // a delta of zero. Only their LOW DIGITS cross the wire - see t2v below.
    var int totClsT2 = 0
    var int totEntT2 = 0

    // HOW OLD THE WINDOW WAS WHEN IT CLOSED (2026-08-16, user request: the
    // day-closes record shows the end of a series and had no way to show its
    // START). Minutes between the bar the window opened on and the bar it
    // closed on, +1, with 0 meaning "no open window" - the same
    // zero-means-nothing convention rotStamp uses, so the reader can tell an
    // absent stamp from a window that opened and closed on one candle.
    //
    // NOT cumulative, unlike the two counters above, and it does not have to
    // be: it is only ever READ on a bar where the closure counter actually
    // moved, and it is written in the same block that moves it - so a
    // repeated value from request.security is never mistaken for a fresh one.
    // Clamped at 998, which is 16.6 hours: past that the record prints a dash
    // rather than a wrong time, and a window open longer than that spans a
    // night the DAY CLOSES table is not a record of anyway.
    var int lastAgeT2 = 0

    // AND WHEN THE WINDOW WENT PAST THE ROUND THRESHOLD (2026-08-16, user
    // request). rotDeepAt says a window that cost MORE than N entries ends
    // the round, so the entry that crosses it is the (N+1)th - and THAT is
    // the critical event the cockpit rotates on. It happens INSIDE the
    // window, minutes or hours before the close, and nothing on the wire
    // carried it: the closure counter only moves when the window pays, so
    // the chart could only ever see the boundary at the close.
    //
    // deepSinceT is the bar the crossing entry was taken on, held until the
    // window ends; lastDeepT2 is its age at the close, in the same
    // minutes-plus-one shape as lastAgeT2 above, so one reader serves both.
    // 0 means this window never crossed - which is most of them.
    var int deepSinceT = -1
    var int lastDeepT2 = 0

    canFireLongT2  = bar_index - lastLongT2 >= cooldown
    canFireShortT2 = bar_index - lastShortT2 >= cooldown

    // tradeOk stands in the exact position hoursOk holds in the shared
    // block: on the DETECTED signal, before the streak is counted. This one
    // line is the whole difference between the two machines.
    buyFoundT2  = triggerLong[1] and canFireLongT2 and tradeOk and flatOk
    sellFoundT2 = triggerShort[1] and not triggerLong[1] and canFireShortT2 and tradeOk and flatOk

    gateT2 = gateMode == 'Off' ? 0 :
         gateMode == 'Manual' ? minEntriesToStart :
         gateMode == 'Distribution' ? (distT2 > 0 ? distT2 : minEntriesToStart) :
         math.max(distT2, minEntriesToStart)

    gateOkT2 = streakT2 >= gateT2

    if streakT2 == 0
        lockedT2 := gateT2
    climT2 = gateMode == 'Off' ? 0 : lockedT2

    buyTakenT2  = buyFoundT2 and gateOkT2
    sellTakenT2 = sellFoundT2 and gateOkT2
    armedNowT2  = gateMode != 'Off' and streakT2 == gateT2 - 1
    buyWarnT2   = buyFoundT2 and armedNowT2
    sellWarnT2  = sellFoundT2 and armedNowT2

    // The stamps and the side, the same ones the scanner keeps: when the
    // open window's gate opened, when the pair arrived on the white step,
    // and the side of the last entry inside the open window.
    var int openSinceT  = -1
    var int whiteSinceT = -1
    var int lastSideT   = 0

    if barstate.isconfirmed and active
        if buyFoundT2
            lastLongT2 := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastShortT2 := bar_index
        if sellFoundT2
            lastShortT2 := bar_index
            if not perDirectionCooldown
                lastLongT2 := bar_index

        // The grading, the histogram transfer with its catch-all guard and
        // the streak - line for line the shared block's, over this machine's
        // own state. The guard's full essay lives in the SYNC region above
        // and is not repeated.
        if not na(bodyRef) and (buyFoundT2 or sellFoundT2)
            hitT2 = (buyFoundT2 and isBull) or (sellFoundT2 and isBear)
            histChangedT2 = false
            if hitT2
                curFailT2 := 0
            else
                curFailT2 += 1
                if curFailT2 <= 15
                    array.set(failHistT2, curFailT2, array.get(failHistT2, curFailT2) + 1)
                    array.set(failHistT2, curFailT2 - 1, array.get(failHistT2, curFailT2 - 1) - 1)
                    histChangedT2 := true
            streakT2 := hitT2 ? 0 : streakT2 + 1
            if histChangedT2
                distT2 := calcIndex(failHistT2, basePercentage)

        buyFailT2  = (buyTakenT2 or buyWarnT2) and not isBull
        sellFailT2 = (sellTakenT2 or sellWarnT2) and not isBear
        greenT2    = (buyFailT2 or sellFailT2) and climT2 > 0 and streakT2 == climT2

        buySetT2 = successScope == 'All found signals' ? buyFoundT2 :
             successScope == 'Entries and warnings' ? (buyTakenT2 or buyWarnT2) :
             buyTakenT2
        sellSetT2 = successScope == 'All found signals' ? sellFoundT2 :
             successScope == 'Entries and warnings' ? (sellTakenT2 or sellWarnT2) :
             sellTakenT2

        winT2   = (buySetT2 and isBull) or (sellSetT2 and isBear)
        entryT2 = buyTakenT2 or sellTakenT2

        // The window, exactly as the scanner runs it: opened by an entry or
        // a green diamond, closed by a win and nothing else, side and stamp
        // reset with it.
        if entryT2
            entriesT2 += 1
            lastSideT := buyTakenT2 ? 1 : -1
            // The crossing entry, stamped once - == and not >=, so a window
            // that runs on to six entries still reports the FOURTH, which is
            // the one the round rule fired on.
            if entriesT2 == rotDeepAt + 1
                deepSinceT := time
            if not windowT2
                windowT2   := true
                openSinceT := time

        if winT2
            // Counted BEFORE the reset wipes entriesT2 - the cost belongs to
            // the window that just ended, exactly as in the shared block.
            totClsT2 += 1
            totEntT2 += entriesT2
            // Read BEFORE the reset below wipes openSinceT, for the same
            // reason entriesT2 is: the stamp belongs to the window that just
            // ended.
            lastAgeT2  := openSinceT >= 0 ? math.min(int((time - openSinceT) / 60000), 998) + 1 : 0
            lastDeepT2 := deepSinceT >= 0 ? math.min(int((time - deepSinceT) / 60000), 998) + 1 : 0
            windowT2   := false
            entriesT2  := 0
            lastSideT  := 0
            openSinceT := -1
            deepSinceT := -1
        else if greenT2
            windowT2   := true
            entriesT2  := 0
            openSinceT := time
            deepSinceT := -1

        // Set on arrival at the white step and HELD - the second bar of the
        // same wait must not restamp it, or every pale row reads "just now".
        whiteSinceT := not windowT2 and ladderStep(streakT2, climT2) == 1 ?
             (whiteSinceT < 0 ? time : whiteSinceT) : -1

    // The eight packed fields, every one describing the SECOND machine. The
    // layout, its ~1e15 ceiling (exact in a double) and the rv* readers on
    // the other side are unchanged:
    //
    //     live  =  stamp * 1e10   minutes since the row started waiting, +1
    //           +  side * 1e9     0 none, 1 long, 2 short
    //           +  market * 1e8   1 while a position is live on this candle
    //           +  step * 1e7     the colour ladder step, 0..8
    //           +  open * 1e5     entries in the OPEN window, clamped at 99
    //           +  gate * 1e3     the gate threshold, clamped at 99
    //           +  streak * 1e1   the miss streak, clamped at 99
    //           +  digit          the machine's current state
    //
    // The digit alphabet is CLOSED at 0..4: 0 idle, 1 pre-armed (the white
    // step), 2 armed (window open, or the gate is), 3 running, 4 held -
    // armed while the flat filter or the trading hours veto signals, so it
    // cannot fire and must never be proposed. With the gate Off the
    // threshold is 0 and every idle machine legitimately reads armed.
    //
    // Every var above moves only under barstate.isconfirmed, so no field
    // can flicker while a candle forms. -1 for the WHOLE value whenever the
    // totals row overflowed - one sentinel, decided in one place, so a pair
    // can never be excluded from the ranking and present in the table at
    // the same time.
    rotStep = windowT2 ? windowStep(ladderStep(streakT2, climT2)) : 0
    rotMkt  = buyTakenT2 or sellTakenT2 ? 1 : 0

    rotStampT = windowT2 ? openSinceT : whiteSinceT
    rotStamp  = rotStampT < 0 ? 0 : math.min(int((time - rotStampT) / 60000), 99998) + 1

    rotDigit = entriesT2 > 0 ? 3 :
         windowT2 or streakT2 >= gateT2 ? (tradeOk and flatOk ? 2 : 4) :
         ladderStep(streakT2, climT2) == 1 ? 1 : 0

    live = packed < 0 ? -1.0 :
         1.0 * rotStamp * 10000000000 +
         (lastSideT == 1 ? 1 : lastSideT == -1 ? 2 : 0) * 1000000000 +
         rotMkt * 100000000 + rotStep * 10000000 +
         (entriesT2 < 99 ? entriesT2 : 99) * 100000 +
         (gateT2 < 99 ? gateT2 : 99) * 1000 +
         (streakT2 < 99 ? streakT2 : 99) * 10 + rotDigit

    // THREE DIGITS FOR THE WIRE: entries mod 100 over closes mod 10. The
    // rotation reads DELTAS bar to bar, so the counters do not need their
    // full width - only enough range that one chart bar's movement cannot
    // wrap. One bar's delta is bounded by the cooldown: more than 9 closes
    // or 99 entries landing on a single chart bar would need the chart to
    // have swallowed twenty-plus of the pair's minutes, which is a data
    // outage and not a trading day. pairState packs five of these per float.
    t2v = (totEntT2 % 100) * 10 + totClsT2 % 10

    // THREE MORE FOR THE WIRE, in the same shape and read by the same
    // unpacker: the age of the window that closed last, 0..999. pairState
    // packs five of these per float too.
    a2v = lastAgeT2

    // AND THREE MORE, the same shape again: how long before the close the
    // window crossed the round threshold. 0 on every window that never did.
    d2v = lastDeepT2

    [packed, live, t2v, a2v, d2v]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One pair, all fifteen lengths
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The shared block first - everything a moving average does not enter into -
// then fifteen call sites of the machine above, one per swept length. A
// separate call site is what buys each machine its own state - a `var` inside
// a function belongs to the call site, so a loop
// would give one machine run fifteen times.
//
// Called once per slot through request.security, so each of the twenty-one
// calls gets its own copy of all fifteen.
pairState() =>
    srcVal = srcName == 'close' ? close :
         srcName == 'open' ? open :
         srcName == 'high' ? high :
         srcName == 'low' ? low :
         srcName == 'hl2' ? hl2 :
         srcName == 'hlc3' ? hlc3 :
         ohlc4

    isBull = open < close
    isBear = open > close

    bodySize  = math.abs(close - open)
    atrVal    = ta.atr(atrLen)
    bodyRef   = ta.sma(bodySize, refLen)[1]
    rangeSize = high - low
    upWick    = high - math.max(open, close)
    dnWick    = math.min(open, close) - low
    upWickRef = ta.sma(upWick, refLen)[1]
    dnWickRef = ta.sma(dnWick, refLen)[1]

    // Resolved inside the requested context, so a JPY cross gets its own pip.
    pipSizeSym = syminfo.mintick * (syminfo.type == 'forex' ? 10.0 : 1.0)
    tolVal     = atrVal * touchTol
    // The trend separation, once per pair instead of once per machine - the
    // ATR was only ever read inside sweepOne to make this product.
    sepVal     = atrVal * trendSep

    wickRejectDown = upWick >= wickRatio * rangeSize and upWick >= minPips * pipSizeSym
    wickRejectUp   = dnWick >= wickRatio * rangeSize and dnWick >= minPips * pipSizeSym
    smallBody      = not na(bodyRef) and bodySize < bodyRef
    longWickDown   = allowLongWick and not na(upWickRef) and upWick > upWickRef
    longWickUp     = allowLongWick and not na(dnWickRef) and dnWick > dnWickRef

    counterShort = allowCounterCandle and isBull and isBear[1]
    counterLong  = allowCounterCandle and isBear and isBull[1]

    dcRef   = DC_REF == 'Open' ? open[DC_BACK] : close[DC_BACK]
    dcReady = DC_ON and not na(dcRef)
    dcShort = dcReady and isBull and isBull[1] and isBear[2] and close <= dcRef
    dcLong  = dcReady and isBear and isBear[1] and isBull[2] and close >= dcRef

    // UTC+1, the frame the hours filter and every timestamp in this project use.
    localHour = (hour + 1) % 24
    inHours   = localHour >= startHour and localHour < endHour
    hoursOk   = not restrictToHours or inHours

    // THE TRADING WINDOW, and it is a SECOND verdict rather than a widening of
    // the one above. hoursOk feeds the shared state machine and therefore
    // every total in the ranking; this one never gets that far - it is carried
    // into sweepOne only to be read by the rotation tail, where it can turn an
    // ARMED row into a HELD one and nothing else. That is what lets the
    // statistics be all-hours while the entries are not. See the Trading hours
    // group for the rest of it.
    inTrade = localHour >= tradeStart and localHour < tradeEnd
    tradeOk = not tradeHoursOn or inTrade

    // THE FLAT VERDICT FOR THIS PAIR - one per pair, shared by all fifteen
    // lengths. See MODULE: flat filter at the top of the file.
    //
    // na for the first flatLen bars, because math.sum propagates na - and na
    // fails BOTH comparisons below, so the state stays where it is during warmup
    // rather than reading a zero ratio as a flat.
    flatPath = math.sum(math.abs(close - close[1]), flatLen)
    flatEr   = nz(flatPath) > 0 ? math.abs(close - close[flatLen]) / flatPath : float(na)

    var bool flatNow  = false
    var int  flatLeft = -100000

    // Confirmed bars only. The verdict has to hold still for the whole of a
    // forming candle, or the signal read from that candle changes under the
    // reader.
    enteredThisBar = false
    if barstate.isconfirmed
        if flatNow
            if flatEr > flatHi
                flatNow  := false
                flatLeft := bar_index
        else if flatEr < flatLo
            flatNow := true
            enteredThisBar := true

    // The bar flat turns on does not stand aside for its OWN close - only
    // from the next bar. A signal graded this bar reads triggerLong[1], a
    // pattern that completed on the PREVIOUS bar, and the live preview held
    // the not-yet-flat verdict stable through this whole bar's formation
    // (flatNow never changes mid-candle, only right here at confirmation) -
    // so grading this same bar against the flat state its own close just
    // produced contradicted what the preview had been showing the whole
    // time it formed (user report, 2026-08-10, in mar1Gates.pine,
    // propagated here since these five inputs and this same block must
    // stay identical across all six files). Leaving flat is unaffected -
    // flatWait already owns how soon trading resumes after that, and 0 has
    // always meant the breakout candle itself; only entering is deferred.
    flatHold = (flatNow and not enteredThisBar) or bar_index - flatLeft < flatWait
    flatOk   = not (flatOn and flatHold)

    // Off by default. Note what it does NOT protect: the averages, the ATR and
    // the twenty moving averages all run on every bar whatever this says. Only
    // the counter bookkeeping sits behind it.
    active = scanBars <= 0 or bar_index > last_bar_index - scanBars

    // GENERATED - fifteen call sites and the thirty-nine-wide tuple they feed.
    // Every length hands back FIVE numbers now: the packed totals the
    // ranking is built from, the packed live state the pairs table is drawn
    // from, its trading-machine counter digits, the age of the window those
    // counters last closed, and the age of the entry that took that window
    // past the round threshold - the last three five lengths to a float, see
    // the h, g and f blocks below. There is no extra machine for the traded
    // length - it is one of these fifteen, chosen by the ranking, so a machine of
    // its own would be a second copy of one of them.
    // Regenerate, never hand-edit.
    [q1, r1, t1, a1, b1] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 0 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q2, r2, t2, a2, b2] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 1 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q3, r3, t3, a3, b3] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 2 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q4, r4, t4, a4, b4] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 3 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q5, r5, t5, a5, b5] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 4 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q6, r6, t6, a6, b6] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 5 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q7, r7, t7, a7, b7] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 6 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q8, r8, t8, a8, b8] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 7 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q9, r9, t9, a9, b9] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 8 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q10, r10, t10, a10, b10] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 9 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q11, r11, t11, a11, b11] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 10 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q12, r12, t12, a12, b12] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 11 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q13, r13, t13, a13, b13] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 12 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q14, r14, t14, a14, b14] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 13 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)
    [q15, r15, t15, a15, b15] = sweepOne(sweepFrom + 14 * sweepStep, srcVal, sepVal, bodyRef, rangeSize, bodySize, upWick, dnWick, tolVal, isBull, isBear, wickRejectDown, wickRejectUp, smallBody, longWickDown, longWickUp, counterShort, counterLong, dcShort, dcLong, hoursOk, flatOk, tradeOk, active)

    // THE TRADING MACHINE'S COUNTERS, five lengths to a float. Each t is
    // three digits - entries mod 100 over closes mod 10, see t2v at the
    // tail of sweepOne - so five of them are fifteen digits, exact in a
    // double. Three floats instead of fifteen values is what keeps the
    // tuple inside the variable budget: the rotation reads DELTAS bar to
    // bar, so the low digits are all it ever needs.
    h1 = 1.0 * t1 + 1.0 * t2 * 1000 + 1.0 * t3 * 1000000 + 1.0 * t4 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * t5 * 1000000000000
    h2 = 1.0 * t6 + 1.0 * t7 * 1000 + 1.0 * t8 * 1000000 + 1.0 * t9 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * t10 * 1000000000000
    h3 = 1.0 * t11 + 1.0 * t12 * 1000 + 1.0 * t13 * 1000000 + 1.0 * t14 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * t15 * 1000000000000

    // THE WINDOW AGES, five lengths to a float, the identical layout the
    // counters use above and read back by the identical t2At - three digits
    // each, fifteen to a float, exact in a double.
    g1 = 1.0 * a1 + 1.0 * a2 * 1000 + 1.0 * a3 * 1000000 + 1.0 * a4 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * a5 * 1000000000000
    g2 = 1.0 * a6 + 1.0 * a7 * 1000 + 1.0 * a8 * 1000000 + 1.0 * a9 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * a10 * 1000000000000
    g3 = 1.0 * a11 + 1.0 * a12 * 1000 + 1.0 * a13 * 1000000 + 1.0 * a14 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * a15 * 1000000000000

    // THE CROSSING AGES, the third family and the last, packed exactly like
    // the two above and read by the same t2At.
    f1 = 1.0 * b1 + 1.0 * b2 * 1000 + 1.0 * b3 * 1000000 + 1.0 * b4 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * b5 * 1000000000000
    f2 = 1.0 * b6 + 1.0 * b7 * 1000 + 1.0 * b8 * 1000000 + 1.0 * b9 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * b10 * 1000000000000
    f3 = 1.0 * b11 + 1.0 * b12 * 1000 + 1.0 * b13 * 1000000 + 1.0 * b14 * 1000000000 + 1.0 * b15 * 1000000000000

    [q1, q2, q3, q4, q5, q6, q7, q8, q9, q10, q11, q12, q13, q14, q15, r1, r2, r3, r4, r5, r6, r7, r8, r9, r10, r11, r12, r13, r14, r15, h1, h2, h3, g1, g2, g3, f1, f2, f3]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The scan
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ignore_invalid_symbol keeps one mistyped ticker from killing the whole script;
// that slot comes back na and is counted as unresolved in the footer.
//
// No lookahead argument: the default is barmerge.lookahead_off, which is the
// only honest setting here.
//
// GENERATED - twenty-one destructurings of thirty-nine values each: fifteen
// packed totals in 1..15, the fifteen packed live states in 16..30, one per
// swept length and in the same order, the three trading-machine counter floats
// in 31..33, the three window-age floats in 34..36 and the three
// threshold-crossing floats in 37..39 - five lengths to a float, in the same
// order again.
[v01_1, v01_2, v01_3, v01_4, v01_5, v01_6, v01_7, v01_8, v01_9, v01_10, v01_11, v01_12, v01_13, v01_14, v01_15, v01_16, v01_17, v01_18, v01_19, v01_20, v01_21, v01_22, v01_23, v01_24, v01_25, v01_26, v01_27, v01_28, v01_29, v01_30, v01_31, v01_32, v01_33, v01_34, v01_35, v01_36, v01_37, v01_38, v01_39] = request.security(s01, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[v02_1, v02_2, v02_3, v02_4, v02_5, v02_6, v02_7, v02_8, v02_9, v02_10, v02_11, v02_12, v02_13, v02_14, v02_15, v02_16, v02_17, v02_18, v02_19, v02_20, v02_21, v02_22, v02_23, v02_24, v02_25, v02_26, v02_27, v02_28, v02_29, v02_30, v02_31, v02_32, v02_33, v02_34, v02_35, v02_36, v02_37, v02_38, v02_39] = request.security(s02, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[v03_1, v03_2, v03_3, v03_4, v03_5, v03_6, v03_7, v03_8, v03_9, v03_10, v03_11, v03_12, v03_13, v03_14, v03_15, v03_16, v03_17, v03_18, v03_19, v03_20, v03_21, v03_22, v03_23, v03_24, v03_25, v03_26, v03_27, v03_28, v03_29, v03_30, v03_31, v03_32, v03_33, v03_34, v03_35, v03_36, v03_37, v03_38, v03_39] = request.security(s03, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[v04_1, v04_2, v04_3, v04_4, v04_5, v04_6, v04_7, v04_8, v04_9, v04_10, v04_11, v04_12, v04_13, v04_14, v04_15, v04_16, v04_17, v04_18, v04_19, v04_20, v04_21, v04_22, v04_23, v04_24, v04_25, v04_26, v04_27, v04_28, v04_29, v04_30, v04_31, v04_32, v04_33, v04_34, v04_35, v04_36, v04_37, v04_38, v04_39] = request.security(s04, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
