[v19_1, v19_2, v19_3, v19_4, v19_5, v19_6, v19_7, v19_8, v19_9, v19_10, v19_11, v19_12, v19_13, v19_14, v19_15, v19_16, v19_17, v19_18, v19_19, v19_20, v19_21, v19_22, v19_23, v19_24, v19_25, v19_26, v19_27, v19_28, v19_29, v19_30, v19_31, v19_32, v19_33, v19_34, v19_35, v19_36, v19_37, v19_38, v19_39] = request.security(s19, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[v20_1, v20_2, v20_3, v20_4, v20_5, v20_6, v20_7, v20_8, v20_9, v20_10, v20_11, v20_12, v20_13, v20_14, v20_15, v20_16, v20_17, v20_18, v20_19, v20_20, v20_21, v20_22, v20_23, v20_24, v20_25, v20_26, v20_27, v20_28, v20_29, v20_30, v20_31, v20_32, v20_33, v20_34, v20_35, v20_36, v20_37, v20_38, v20_39] = request.security(s20, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
[v21_1, v21_2, v21_3, v21_4, v21_5, v21_6, v21_7, v21_8, v21_9, v21_10, v21_11, v21_12, v21_13, v21_14, v21_15, v21_16, v21_17, v21_18, v21_19, v21_20, v21_21, v21_22, v21_23, v21_24, v21_25, v21_26, v21_27, v21_28, v21_29, v21_30, v21_31, v21_32, v21_33, v21_34, v21_35, v21_36, v21_37, v21_38, v21_39] = request.security(s21, timeframe.period, pairState(), ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The totals
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Parallel arrays rather than one array of records, for the same reason the
// scanner's log uses them: it is the shape the table renderer wants, and Pine
// has no tuple array.
//
//   agC    closed windows, summed over every ticked pair
//   agE    entries spent, summed the same way
//   agR    closed windows that cost redAt entries or more, summed the same way
//   agD    deep windows that opened on the heels of another deep window on the
//          same pair, summed the same way - the Links column
//   agW    the worst SINGLE window any pair had at this length
//   agWho  the pair that worst window happened on
//   agP    how many pairs contributed at least one closure
//   agX    how many pairs came back overflowed and were left out entirely
//
// agR is a sum and agW is a maximum, and that is the whole difference between
// them: one says how often this length went deep across the watchlist, the other
// says how deep the single worst run got and where. A length can be quiet on one
// and alarming on the other, which is exactly when it is worth knowing both.
//
// agP is the column that catches a length which looks good because ONE pair
// carried it. An average of 1.8 over twenty pairs and an average of 1.8 over
// three are not the same finding, and nothing else on the row says which it is.
//
// agWho exists because the worst window is the number a recovery ladder dies on,
// and a number with no pair attached cannot be acted on: eleven entries is a
// warning about SOMETHING, and until it names the pair there is nothing to go
// and look at. It is the pair that set the current maximum, so a later tie does
// not steal the credit.
var int[]    agC   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var int[]    agE   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var int[]    agR   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var int[]    agD   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var int[]    agW   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var string[] agWho = array.new_string(SWEEP_N, '')
var int[]    agP   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var int[]    agX   = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)

// The order the rows are DRAWN in, rebuilt every last-bar pass.
//
// FOUR BANDS, and the offsets between them are what keeps a row from climbing
// out of the one it belongs to. Within every band the rows are ordered on the
// same column the table is sorted by, so there is no block anywhere that looks
// unsorted:
//
//     0     cleared the win threshold - the ranking proper
//   1e6     has closed windows, but fewer than the threshold asks for
//   1e8     has closed nothing at all: no column exists to sort it on, so
//           length order is the only order left, and it is the honest one
//   2e9     outside the requested range, never touched by either pass
//
// The bands are wide enough that no key can cross one, and this is worth
// re-deriving rather than assuming, because the Links ranking broke the old
// justification. Four of the five keys are an average or a share - single
// digits at worst. The fifth is links · 4 + nL · 2 + score, and links is a SUM
// over the slots: twenty-one pairs, each clamped at 99, is 2079, so that key
// tops out at 2079 · 4 + 2 + 1 = 8319. Band 1 starts at 1e6, which leaves a
// factor of about a hundred. Safe - but it is arithmetic now, not an obvious
// truth, so widen the bands before widening PACK_D, the slot count, or either
// multiplier in the Links key.
var float[] sortKey = array.new_float(SWEEP_N, 0.0)

// The Balanced score per length, 0 best to 1 worst, na where the row does not
// qualify. It is held in an array rather than recomputed in the drawing loop for
// one reason: the number that is SHOWN in the Score column and the number the
// table was SORTED on have to be the same number, and the only way to guarantee
// that is for there to be one of them.
var float[] agScore = array.new_float(SWEEP_N, float(na))

// Two counters in an array rather than two plain variables, because a Pine
// function cannot assign to a global - an array is a reference, so mutating its
// contents is the one way to count something from inside one.
//
//   0  slots actually scanned, blanks and unticked excluded
//   1  slots that came back na, which means the ticker did not resolve
var int[] tally = array.new_int(2, 0)

// Unpacking, the exact inverse of the expression at the end of sweepOne - and
// exact in the arithmetic sense as well, which took some care.
//
// The obvious way to write this is divide, then floor. It survives this layout,
// and only just. v reaches 9e15, so v / 100 reaches 9e13, where the gap between
// one representable double and the next is 0.015625 - and rounding can move a
// true quotient of q + 0.99 by half of that, 0.0078, against the 0.01 that
// separates it from q + 1. The margin is 0.0022. Nothing is wrong with the
// answer today; what is wrong is that it depends on a hair's width of a field
// width nobody would think to re-derive before widening one.
//
// So every division here is applied to a number the modulo has already made an
// exact multiple of the divisor. IEEE division of two exactly-representable
// values whose true quotient is representable returns that quotient exactly, so
// each step is exact by construction and the margin above stops mattering.
//
// int() first, not math.floor: Pine's int is 64-bit and holds 9e15 without loss,
// and integer % is exact where a float one would not have to be.
//
// Five readers now, and the one thing that can go wrong here is invisible.
// unpD is a copy of unpR's SHAPE, and the two differ only in which constants
// they name. Copy unpR and forget to swap PACK_R for PACK_D and the result is
// not an error - it is red's two low digits leaking into the Links column, so
// a packed 300020000370406 (300 entries, 200 closures, 37 red, 4 links, 6
// worst) reads 3704 instead of 4. Small, plausible, and nothing on screen says
// otherwise. Both constants change in both readers, or neither does.
unpW(float v) => int(v) % PACK_W
unpD(float v) => int((v - int(v) % POS_D) / POS_D) % PACK_D
unpR(float v) => int((v - int(v) % POS_R) / POS_R) % PACK_R
unpC(float v) => int((v - int(v) % POS_C) / POS_C) % PACK_C
unpE(float v) => int((v - int(v) % POS_E) / POS_E)

// The red share read pessimistically, and it is the exact analogue of Avg +1σ:
// what this length's tail would look like if the run had been one error bar
// lucky. Ranking on the raw share instead would let a small sample win by not
// having lived long enough to go deep.
//
// The share is a proportion, so its error bar is the binomial sqrt(p(1-p)/n) -
// and at p = 0 that is exactly zero, which would hand a length that closed forty
// windows without a deep one the same flawless tail as one that closed four
// thousand. So the error is taken at a SMOOTHED proportion, (red + 0.5) /
// (wins + 1), which is never zero and shrinks as the sample grows: no deep runs
// in forty windows is charged about 1.7%, no deep runs in four hundred about
// 0.2%. The centre stays the real share, so what the Red column prints is still
// what happened - only the error bar is smoothed.
redHi(int red, int wins) =>
    if wins <= 0
        float(na)
    else
        p = (red + 0.5) / (wins + 1.0)
        1.0 * red / wins + math.sqrt(p * (1.0 - p) / wins)

// The exchange prefix is width and nothing else: every row in a one-broker list
// carries the same one.
shortName(string raw) =>
    parts = str.split(raw, ':')
    array.size(parts) > 1 ? array.get(parts, array.size(parts) - 1) : raw

// One length of one pair, folded into the totals. A negative value is the
// overflow sentinel and contributes nothing at all - counted, and left out of
// every sum, so no average is quietly built on a corrupted number.
//
// The worst window is the one field that is a MAXIMUM rather than a sum, so it
// carries the pair that set it. Strictly greater, not greater-or-equal: a later
// pair that merely ties keeps the earlier one's name, which makes the column
// stable across a redraw instead of showing whichever slot happened to be last.
accum(int k, float v, string pair) =>
    if v < 0
        array.set(agX, k, array.get(agX, k) + 1)
    else
        c = unpC(v)
        w = unpW(v)
        array.set(agC, k, array.get(agC, k) + c)
        array.set(agE, k, array.get(agE, k) + unpE(v))
        array.set(agR, k, array.get(agR, k) + unpR(v))
        array.set(agD, k, array.get(agD, k) + unpD(v))
        if w > array.get(agW, k)
            array.set(agW, k, w)
            array.set(agWho, k, pair)
        if c > 0
            array.set(agP, k, array.get(agP, k) + 1)

// THE TOTALS, PARKED IN ONE FLAT ARRAY - and this is not tidiness, it is the
// only shape the compiler accepts.
//
// A user function may reference at most 254 EXTERNAL elements - values from
// the enclosing scope - and every one of the hundreds of names the
// request.security calls destructure counts as one. computeRanking() reads
// all of them through its twenty-one addPair calls, so with the totals as
// plain arguments it came to about eleven hundred and was refused outright:
//
//   The "computeRanking" function uses 1100 external elements. The limit is
//   254 ... You can reduce the number of external elements by using arrays or
//   objects (CE10116)
//
// An ARRAY is ONE external element however many numbers are inside it, so the
// totals are stashed into a single flat array - slot * SWEEP_N + length - and
// addPair reads them back out by slot. Same numbers, same order, one name
// crossing the function boundary instead of hundreds.
//
// THE LIVE HALF IS NOT IN HERE and does not need to be: it never enters the
// ranking. It reaches rotWatch and rotOne through pickN, which takes
// its fifteen values as ordinary arguments at the CALL SITE, in the main scope
// where no such limit applies.
//
// Filled once per confirmed bar in a top-level block of its own, below - not
// at the two places the ranking is computed, because a block of twenty-one
// more generated lines inside either of those risks the other compiled-size
// ceiling this file has already hit once (CE10205, "the if statement is too
// long").
var float[] packAll = array.new_float(SWEEP_N * 21, float(na))

// GENERATED - one slot's fifteen totals into the flat array. Regenerate,
// never hand-edit.
stashPair(int slot, float v1, float v2, float v3, float v4,
     float v5, float v6, float v7, float v8, float v9, float v10,
     float v11, float v12, float v13, float v14, float v15) =>
    b = slot * SWEEP_N
    array.set(packAll, b + 0, v1)
    array.set(packAll, b + 1, v2)
    array.set(packAll, b + 2, v3)
    array.set(packAll, b + 3, v4)
    array.set(packAll, b + 4, v5)
    array.set(packAll, b + 5, v6)
    array.set(packAll, b + 6, v7)
    array.set(packAll, b + 7, v8)
    array.set(packAll, b + 8, v9)
    array.set(packAll, b + 9, v10)
    array.set(packAll, b + 10, v11)
    array.set(packAll, b + 11, v12)
    array.set(packAll, b + 12, v13)
    array.set(packAll, b + 13, v14)
    array.set(packAll, b + 14, v15)

// One pair, all fifteen lengths. 'on' is the slot's tick box and 'raw' its
// symbol: both have to hold, because an unticked or blank slot was pointed at
// the chart's own symbol and its answer is about a pair nobody asked for.
//
// na is tested through the first value alone: ignore_invalid_symbol makes the
// whole tuple na at once, so one test settles all fifteen.
//
// GENERATED no longer - the fifteen values arrive through packAll, so the
// signature is three arguments and the body is a loop. accum keeps every
// counter it touches in an array rather than in a `var`, which is what makes
// a loop legal here where the machines themselves needed separate call sites.
addPair(bool on, string raw, int slot) =>
    live = on and raw != ''
    if live
        b = slot * SWEEP_N
        array.set(tally, 0, array.get(tally, 0) + 1)
        if na(array.get(packAll, b))
            array.set(tally, 1, array.get(tally, 1) + 1)
        else
            nm = shortName(raw)
            for k = 0 to SWEEP_N - 1
                accum(k, array.get(packAll, b + k), nm)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The rotation readers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The other half of the ROTATION TAIL contract in sweepOne: the tail packs
// stamp * 1e10 + side * 1e9 + market * 1e8 + step * 1e7 + open * 1e5 +
// gate * 1e3 + streak * 1e1 + digit, and these eight unpack it with the same
// integer discipline as the unp* family above - int() first, and every
// division applied to a number the modulo has already made an exact multiple
// of the divisor. The alphabet (0..4), the ~1e15 ceiling and the monotonic
// -1 sentinel are documented at the tail; the two sides move together or
// neither does.
//
// rvStamp is minutes + 1, never a clock: zero means the row has no stamp at
// all. Turning it back into a time is the drawing's job, because only the
// drawing knows what bar it is standing on.
rvDig(float v)   => int(v) % 10
rvStk(float v)   => int((int(v) - int(v) % 10) / 10) % 100
rvThr(float v)   => int((int(v) - int(v) % 1000) / 1000) % 100
rvOpn(float v)   => int((int(v) - int(v) % 100000) / 100000) % 100
rvStep(float v)  => int((int(v) - int(v) % 10000000) / 10000000) % 10
rvMkt(float v)   => int((int(v) - int(v) % 100000000) / 100000000) % 10
rvSide(float v)  => int((int(v) - int(v) % 1000000000) / 1000000000) % 10
rvStamp(float v) => int((int(v) - int(v) % 10000000000) / 10000000000)

// WHICH OF THE FIFTEEN, and the one function this whole redesign is built on.
//
// The traded length is not an input any more, so nothing about it is known
// until the ranking has been computed - which cannot happen until every pair
// has answered, which is long after the call sites are fixed. So all fifteen
// answer, and exactly one of the fifteen values is chosen HERE, on this side of
// request.security, by an index that is an ordinary series int.
//
// A ternary chain rather than an array: an array would have to be built from
// fifteen pushes per pair per bar, and this is called on every confirmed bar.
// Pine evaluates every branch of a ternary, which costs nothing when every
// branch is a bare name.
//
// na for an index outside 0..14 - which is what "no pick yet" looks like, and
// every caller already tests the value for na.
pickN(int k, float a1, float a2, float a3, float a4, float a5,
     float a6, float a7, float a8, float a9, float a10,
     float a11, float a12, float a13, float a14, float a15) =>
    k == 0 ? a1 : k == 1 ? a2 : k == 2 ? a3 : k == 3 ? a4 : k == 4 ? a5 :
     k == 5 ? a6 : k == 6 ? a7 : k == 7 ? a8 : k == 8 ? a9 : k == 9 ? a10 :
     k == 10 ? a11 : k == 11 ? a12 : k == 12 ? a13 : k == 13 ? a14 : k == 14 ? a15 :
     float(na)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The rotation state - which length is being traded, and when that changes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE THING THIS FILE DECIDES BY ITSELF. activeIdx is a slot of the swept
// range, not a length: 0 is sweepFrom, 14 is sweepFrom + 14·Step. -1 means
// nothing is being traded, which is the honest state until some length has
// closed enough windows for the ranking to trust it.
//
// It moves on ROUND BOUNDARIES and never between them, and a boundary is ONE
// event: a window closed costing MORE than rotDeepAt entries, on any watched
// pair, on the traded length, inside the trading window. Nothing else ends a
// round - not a count of wins, not the clock, not a pair arming or drifting
// away.
//
// THAT IS THE WHOLE RULE, and it is deliberately the smallest one that still
// reacts to the thing worth reacting to. A length that keeps closing inside
// the band is a length doing its job, and interrupting it to re-read a ranking
// that has barely moved is churn - churn a human pays for, since the new
// number has to be retyped into maRejection.pine by hand. A run past the band
// is the one event a recovery ladder cannot absorb.
//
// WHAT WENT WITH THE OLD RULE, so the absence is not read as an oversight: the
// round used to own a SET - the pairs that already had a window open when it
// started, snapshotted, with pre-armed pairs excluded so the round could not
// deadlock on a pair that owed a close it had no way to pay. None of that
// exists any more. There is no membership test in rotWatch, no snapshot pass,
// and a pair that armed a minute ago can end a round exactly like one that has
// been open for an hour.
//
// On a round boundary the ranking is recomputed AS OF THAT BAR - no lookahead,
// the totals are what they were then - and the length holding 'Ranking position
// to trade' becomes the traded one. It very often is the one already traded,
// and then nothing moves but the round.
//
// activeSince is the bar time of the last change, activeSwitches counts them,
// and both exist for the ROTATION row: a cockpit that says which length is
// traded and refuses to say since when is a cockpit that cannot be checked
// against the chart.
var int activeIdx      = -1
var int activeLen      = 0
var int activeSince    = -1
var int activeSwitches = 0

// How many wins have been counted since the last recompute. NOTHING hangs on
// it - the boundary is the deep window and only that - but a cockpit that says
// "recompute at >4 entries" and refuses to say how much has closed since the
// last one has no sense of time in it. Counted on the same closes the round
// ignores, which is every close the trading window let through.
var int winsSinceRot = 0

// Per-slot baselines for the trading machine's counters of the TRADED length
// - their low digits, exactly as they cross the wire - and the reason they
// are per slot rather than one aggregate sum: the deep-window test needs a
// pair's closures and its entries to be the same pair's. Summed first, two
// pairs closing on one bar would look like one expensive window.
//
// -1 means "no baseline" - a slot that is unticked, blank, unresolved or
// overflowed, and every slot for one bar after the traded length changes,
// because the counters of the new length have nothing to do with the counters
// of the old one. A missing baseline produces no delta and therefore no
// event, which is the safe direction to fail in.
//
// Named base* rather than prev*: sweepOne's tail owns a family of state
// names of its own (the T2 machine), and a global array shadowed by a local
// inside the most-edited function in the file is a trap set for later rather
// than a name saved now.
var int[] baseCls = array.new_int(21, -1)
var int[] baseEnt = array.new_int(21, -1)

// This bar's one event, in an array because a Pine function cannot assign to a
// global: set when some pair closed a window costing more than rotDeepAt
// entries. One slot, because there is one boundary condition left.
var int[] rotEvt = array.new_int(1, 0)

// WHAT ELSE THE EVENT USED TO CARRY, and this is the note left where it was:
// beside the flag stood the pair that fired it and what the window cost,
// written by rotWatch and read by nobody but the ROTATION JOURNAL. The journal
// is gone (2026-08-16) and they went with it - the same facts are in the DAY
// CLOSES table below, on the record itself, where the ► marks the window that
// ended the round. Nothing decides anything on them anywhere, so there is
// nothing to restore if the journal ever comes back except the journal.

// When the day's record starts, printed in the DAY CLOSES title.
var int jrnDayAt = -1

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DAY CLOSES - every window the day closed, and not a summary of them
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One entry per closed window, in the order they closed, and since 2026-08-16
// the only record of the day this file keeps. Since the same day it records
// the TRADING machine's closes - the same closes mar1Scanner.pine's log
// holds - and not the all-hours statistics': see rotWatch.
//
//   clsSt    when the series STARTED - the opening minute of the candle its
//            first entry was placed on, 0 when the pair sent no stamp
//   clsDp    when the window CROSSED the round threshold - the minute of the
//            entry that made a recompute due, 0 when it never crossed
//   clsAt    the bar time the close was DETECTED on this chart
//   clsWho   the pair
//   clsEnt   what it cost in entries
//   clsCnt   how many windows that entry covers - 1 almost always, 2 or more
//            only when the chart had no bar of its own for each of the pair's
//            and two closes arrived together, their costs added
//   clsLen   the MA length it closed ON, which is what makes a rotation
//            visible in the list: the column simply changes value partway down
//
// ONE DAY, gated by jrnDayNow: cleared when the trading window opens - the
// same tradeStart the entries are gated by, so "today" here means the trading
// day and not the calendar one. With the hours filter off there is no window
// to open on, and the calendar day in UTC+1 is used instead.
//
// WHAT A RECORD CANNOT SHOW, and it is a property of the wire rather than of
// this code: only CLOSED windows are visible. The trading machine's entries
// counter moves ONLY when a window closes, so a pair sitting on three
// entries right now is in no record until it closes. Nothing else crosses
// request.security, so there is no version of this that sees open windows.
//
// FIVE HUNDRED, and it is a memory cap rather than a display one: the drawing
// pages through whatever is here. Past it the OLDEST record falls off and the
// title says how many - a table frozen at the morning is worse than one that
// says it lost the morning.
// A day that closes five hundred windows on a twenty-one pair watchlist is not
// a day this file was built for, so the cap is a backstop and not a setting.
CLS_MAX = 500
var int[]    clsAt  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    clsSt  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    clsDp  = array.new_int(0)
var string[] clsWho = array.new_string(0)
var int[]    clsEnt = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    clsCnt = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    clsLen = array.new_int(0)

// How many records fell off the front, in an ARRAY rather than in a plain int
// - a Pine function cannot assign to a global, and the two helpers below are
// functions for a reason of their own: the confirmed-bar block they are called
// from is the one this file has already had to split once for compiled size
// (CE10205), so what goes into it is one call and never a dozen lines.
var int[] clsMeta = array.new_int(1, 0)

clearCloses() =>
    array.clear(clsSt)
    array.clear(clsDp)
    array.clear(clsAt)
    array.clear(clsWho)
    array.clear(clsEnt)
    array.clear(clsCnt)
    array.clear(clsLen)
    array.set(clsMeta, 0, 0)

pushClose(int st, int dp, int at, string who, int ent, int cnt, int len) =>
    if array.size(clsAt) >= CLS_MAX
        array.shift(clsSt)
        array.shift(clsDp)
        array.shift(clsAt)
        array.shift(clsWho)
        array.shift(clsEnt)
        array.shift(clsCnt)
        array.shift(clsLen)
        array.set(clsMeta, 0, array.get(clsMeta, 0) + 1)
    array.push(clsSt,  st)
    array.push(clsDp,  dp)
    array.push(clsAt,  at)
    array.push(clsWho, who)
    array.push(clsEnt, ent)
    array.push(clsCnt, cnt)
    array.push(clsLen, len)

// The rotation bookkeeping, rebuilt on every redraw exactly like the totals:
//
//   rotArmedNames  pairs whose traded-length machine is ARMED, in slot order.
//                  The FIRST entry is the proposal - the tie rule is slot
//                  order, fixed by the verdict, deliberately nothing cleverer
//   rotWinNames    pairs that closed a WIN on the traded length on this bar
//   rotWinEnts     what those windows COST, in entries, one per name
//   rotWinCls      how many windows that one record covers - 1 almost always,
//                  2 or more only when the chart had no bar of its own for
//                  each of the pair's and two closes arrived together
//   rotWinStart    when the series STARTED - the opening time of the candle
//                  its first entry was placed on, 0 when the pair sent no
//                  stamp. On a two-in-one-bar record it is the LAST of the
//                  two windows, which is the same tolerance the cost carries
//   rotWinDeep     when the window CROSSED the round threshold - the time of
//                  the entry after which a recompute was owed, 0 on every
//                  window that stayed inside the band
//   rotTally       0 armed, 1 held, 2 running, 3 excluded (na or -1 overflow)
//
// rotWinEnts, rotWinCls, rotWinStart and rotWinDeep are the DAY CLOSES
// table's, and they are pushed in lockstep with the names: five arrays read
// by index, so a record can never name one pair and carry another one's cost.
var string[] rotArmedNames = array.new_string(0)
var string[] rotWinNames   = array.new_string(0)
var int[]    rotWinEnts    = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotWinCls     = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotWinStart   = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotWinDeep    = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotTally      = array.new_int(4, 0)

// The PAIRS table's raw material, one entry per pair that is not idle, in slot
// order. Every column of mar1Scanner.pine's table is in here: the state digit,
// the miss streak and the gate it is measured against, the entries inside the
// open window, the ladder step the row is coloured by, whether a position is
// live, the side of the last entry, and the stamp the 'Open since' column
// prints. The display ORDER is built later, by the scanner's own sort key.
var string[] rotPairNames = array.new_string(0)
var int[]    rotPairDigs  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairStks  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairThrs  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairOpns  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairSteps = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairMkts  = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairSides = array.new_int(0)
var int[]    rotPairStamp = array.new_int(0)
var float[]  rotPairKeys  = array.new_float(0)

// The most recent WIN, kept until the next one replaces it. rotWinNames only
// holds wins detected on the CURRENT confirmed bar, so without this a win is
// on screen for exactly one bar and then gone - fine for an alert subscriber,
// useless on a plan that has no alerts. '-' until the first win after the
// chart loads. The pair name, the length and the time all live INSIDE the
// stored text, so the line cannot drift from what it describes (changing any
// input reruns the script and resets it anyway - a var does not survive a
// settings change).
var string lastWinTxt = '-'

// The length the last win closed ON, which is not always the length being
// traded a moment later: a win can be the third of its round, and then the
// rotation moves activeLen on the very same bar. Captured before the move, so
// the LAST WIN line and the alert name the machine that actually won.
var int lastWinLen = 0

// THE TRADING WINDOW, on the CHART's clock - the same UTC+1 frame pairState
// measures it in, and the same answer while the pair and the chart share a
// timeframe and a session.
//
// It is read again here rather than carried inside the packed live value
// because the ROUND has to be gated at the moment a close is DETECTED, and
// that moment is a chart bar: the totals value the detection reads carries no
// clock of its own, only counters. The two readings can disagree by a bar at
// the edge of the window when the pair's last bar is older than the chart's,
// which is the same tolerance the 'Open since' column already carries.
chartHour = (hour + 1) % 24
tradeNow  = not tradeHoursOn or (chartHour >= tradeStart and chartHour < tradeEnd)

// THE WORKING DAY, for the DAY CLOSES table and nothing else. With the hours filter
// on it is the window itself - the day begins on the first bar inside it, which
// is what "since the start of the working day" means when the day is defined by
// two inputs. With the filter off tradeNow is always true and can never open,
// so the calendar day in UTC+1 stands in: time is milliseconds UTC, one hour
// forward and floored by a day gives an index that changes at midnight UTC+1.
chartDay  = math.floor((time + 3600000) / 86400000)
dayOpens  = tradeHoursOn ? (tradeNow and not tradeNow[1]) : chartDay != nz(chartDay[1], chartDay)

// THE CHOSEN DAY, as the same index the bar carries, so the test is one integer
// comparison and no calendar arithmetic. jrnDayNow gates both writes into the
// record - the clear and the close records - which is what makes a chosen day
// FREEZE once it is over rather than being cleared by the next window that
// opens. With the switch off it is always true and the table behaves exactly
// as it did before the picker existed.
pickDay   = math.floor((jrnDayPick + 3600000) / 86400000)
jrnDayNow = not jrnDayOn or chartDay == pickDay

// One three-digit group of the trading machine's counter float: entries mod
// 100 over closes mod 10, the inverse of t2v at the tail of sweepOne. Same
// integer discipline as the unp* family - int() first, subtract the modulo
// before dividing - so every step is exact by construction.
t2At(float hv, int g) =>
    iv = int(hv)
    d  = g == 0 ? 1 : g == 1 ? 1000 : g == 2 ? 1000000 : g == 3 ? 1000000000 : 1000000000000
    int((iv - iv % d) / d) % 1000

// One slot's TRADING-MACHINE counters for the traded length, read on every
// confirmed bar: it counts this bar's wins, flags a deep one, names the pairs
// that closed, and leaves the baseline ready for the next bar.
//
// SINCE 2026-08-16 THIS READS THE SECOND MACHINE, not the all-hours totals
// (user decision: the day-closes record, the LAST WIN line, the alert and the
// round itself follow the machine being TRADED - the same machine the PAIRS
// table and mar1Scanner.pine's log describe. The RANKING alone stays
// all-hours - and since 2026-08-16 the two grids sit one step apart, the
// sweeper reading 16..44 to this file's 15..43, so the sweeper's first
// place can differ). The three floats per family are the
// pair's fifteen lengths at three digits each - see t2v in the tail - and the
// traded length's group is picked in here rather than at the call site.
//
// The win trigger is the closures counter's delta and not a pulse, for the
// reason written at the tail. The counters arrive MODULAR - only their low
// digits cross the wire - so a delta is read modulo its field width: a
// repeated value on a chart bar the pair had no bar for still reads zero, and
// a baseline of -1 still means "count nothing on this bar". No tradeNow gate
// any more: this machine finds no signals outside the trading window BY
// CONSTRUCTION, so every movement of its counters is a trading close, and
// gating detection by the chart's clock would only drop a close that landed a
// bar past the boundary.
//
// TWO WINS ON ONE BAR are possible when the chart has no bar of its own for
// each of the pair's, and then the two costs arrive added together. The round
// test is therefore written as a SUFFICIENT condition rather than an exact
// one: with dC closures sharing dE entries, no single window can have cost
// more than dE - (dC - 1), so when even that lower bound clears the threshold
// at least one window really did. It under-reports in that rare corner and
// never over-reports, which is the right way round for a trigger that ends a
// round.
rotWatch(int slot, bool on, string raw, float ha, float hb, float hc,
     float ga, float gb, float gc, float fa, float fb, float fc) =>
    k  = activeIdx
    hv = k < 0 ? float(na) : k < 5 ? ha : k < 10 ? hb : hc
    gv = k < 0 ? float(na) : k < 5 ? ga : k < 10 ? gb : gc
    fv = k < 0 ? float(na) : k < 5 ? fa : k < 10 ? fb : fc
    if on and raw != '' and not na(hv) and hv >= 0
        t = t2At(hv, k % 5)
        c = t % 10
        e = int((t - t % 10) / 10)
        a  = na(gv) or gv < 0 ? 0 : t2At(gv, k % 5)
        b  = na(fv) or fv < 0 ? 0 : t2At(fv, k % 5)
        pc = array.get(baseCls, slot)
        pe = array.get(baseEnt, slot)
        if pc >= 0
            dC = (c - pc + 10) % 10
            dE = (e - pe + 100) % 100
            if dC > 0
                // Every close is reported - the LAST WIN line, the alert and
                // the wins-since counter read the watchlist, not the round.
                //
                // The cost and the count ride along for the DAY CLOSES
                // table. dE is what this pair spent on everything that closed
                // here, so on the rare two-in-one-bar record it is the pair
                // of windows together - which is why the count is carried
                // beside it rather than left to be guessed from a number that
                // would read as one very deep window.
                array.push(rotWinNames, shortName(raw))
                array.push(rotWinEnts, dE)
                array.push(rotWinCls, dC)
                // WHEN THE SERIES STARTED, rebuilt from the age that rode
                // over with it: the pair measured it from the bar its window
                // opened on to the bar it closed on, and this bar is that
                // close. So the chart's own bar OPENING time, minus those
                // minutes, is the opening minute of the candle the first
                // entry was placed on - which is what an entry time means.
                //
                // Not time_close, which the Time column uses: that column
                // prints when a candle SETTLED and this one prints when a
                // candle was ENTERED, and those are the two ends of the same
                // trade. 0 stays 0 - no stamp, and the table prints a dash.
                //
                // The usual tolerance applies, the one 'Open since' already
                // carries: a minute or two out when the pair's last bar is
                // older than the chart's.
                array.push(rotWinStart, a > 0 ? time - (a - 1) * 60000 : 0)
                // AND WHEN IT CROSSED THE ROUND THRESHOLD - the same
                // arithmetic on the same kind of stamp. 0 on every window
                // that stayed inside the band, which is most of them, and
                // the presence of this one is exactly what makes a record a
                // round boundary.
                array.push(rotWinDeep, b > 0 ? time - (b - 1) * 60000 : 0)
                // THE ONE BOUNDARY, and no membership test in front of it:
                // any watched pair can end a round.
                if dE - (dC - 1) > rotDeepAt
                    array.set(rotEvt, 0, 1)
        array.set(baseCls, slot, c)
        array.set(baseEnt, slot, e)
    else
        array.set(baseCls, slot, -1)
        array.set(baseEnt, slot, -1)

// One slot's LIVE value for the traded length, read on redraw bars only: the
// digit feeds the counts and the proposal, the other seven fields are the
// table's row.
//
// The sort key is mar1Scanner.pine's, and it is built here rather than in the
// drawing so the two files can be diffed against each other: state · 1000,
// pre-armed lifted half a state above idle, entries · 10 to order the deep
// windows first, and the slot index as a thousandth so the order is stable
// where everything else ties. The digits map onto the scanner's three states
// - 3 running is its 2, 2 and 4 (armed, held) are its 1, 1 pre-armed is its 0
// with the half-state bonus.
rotOne(bool on, string raw, int slot, float v) =>
    if on and raw != ''
        if na(v) or v < 0
            array.set(rotTally, 3, array.get(rotTally, 3) + 1)
        else
            d = rvDig(v)

            // NO ROW FILTER, and none is needed any more: the live value is
            // packed from the SECOND, hours-gated machine, so the row that
            // used to have to be filtered out - a window opened at ten at
            // night - cannot exist in the first place. A pre-armed stamp can
            // still land at any hour, exactly as in the scanner, and is
            // shown. A row is drawn whenever that machine says the pair is
            // not idle, which is precisely addRow in mar1Scanner.pine.
            //
            // The stamp is minutes-ago +1 against the chart's current bar -
            // see rotStamp at the tail of sweepOne - with the usual
            // tolerance: a minute or two out when the pair's last bar is
            // older than the chart's. Zero means the machine has no stamp at
            // all, which is what a window opened before the loaded history
            // looks like.
            stampMin = rvStamp(v)

            if d != 0
                st = d == 3 ? 2 : d == 1 ? 0 : 1
                array.push(rotPairNames, shortName(raw))
                array.push(rotPairDigs, d)
                array.push(rotPairStks, rvStk(v))
                array.push(rotPairThrs, rvThr(v))
                array.push(rotPairOpns, rvOpn(v))
                array.push(rotPairSteps, rvStep(v))
                array.push(rotPairMkts, rvMkt(v))
                array.push(rotPairSides, rvSide(v))
                array.push(rotPairStamp, stampMin)
                array.push(rotPairKeys, st * 1000.0 + (d == 1 ? 500.0 : 0.0) +
                     math.min(rvOpn(v), 99) * 10.0 - slot * 0.01)
                if d == 2
                    array.push(rotArmedNames, shortName(raw))
                    array.set(rotTally, 0, array.get(rotTally, 0) + 1)
                else if d == 4
                    array.set(rotTally, 1, array.get(rotTally, 1) + 1)
                else if d == 3
                    array.set(rotTally, 2, array.get(rotTally, 2) + 1)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The ranking, as a function - and why it had to become one
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every line of this was inline in the redraw block, which ran once, on the
// last bar, because that was the only bar anything was drawn on. The rotation
// changed that: the traded length is decided at round boundaries scattered
// through the history, and each one needs the ranking AS IT STOOD on its own
// bar. So the pass moved in here and is called from two places - the round
// boundary and the redraw - with nothing else about it altered.
//
// It writes its answers into arrays because a Pine function cannot assign to
// a global, and it needs to hand back rather more than one value:
//
//   rankOrder  the lengths, best first - array.sort_indices' answer, copied
//              out because the array it returns is the function's own
//   rankI      0 trusted, 1 overflowed, 2 redFree, 3 bestLen, 4 best2Len,
//              5 worstLen, 6 worst2Len, 7 bestCls
//   rankF      0 spread, 1 bestAvg, 2 bestHi, 3 bestScore
//
// The two passes, the four bands of sortKey and the five ranking keys are
// unchanged and their reasoning is where it always was - at sortKey, at
// redHi, and at the 'Rank and sort by' input.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS, because it is no longer a once-per-chart luxury: twenty-one
// pairs by fifteen lengths of unpacking, two passes over fifteen lengths, and a
// sort of fifteen. It runs on a round boundary - a few hundred times over a
// long history, against the twenty thousand bars the machines themselves are
// walked over - and once more per redraw bar. That is small beside the
// machines, and it is the only new arithmetic the rotation added.
var int[]   rankOrder = array.new_int(SWEEP_N, 0)
var int[]   rankI     = array.new_int(8, 0)
var float[] rankF     = array.new_float(4, float(na))

computeRanking() =>
    for k = 0 to SWEEP_N - 1
        array.set(agC, k, 0)
        array.set(agE, k, 0)
        array.set(agR, k, 0)
        array.set(agD, k, 0)
        array.set(agW, k, 0)
        array.set(agWho, k, '')
        array.set(agP, k, 0)
        array.set(agX, k, 0)
        // Parked above even the unrankable rows, so a length outside the
        // requested range can never surface at the top of a sorted table on
        // the strength of a key nobody set.
        array.set(sortKey, k, 2000000000.0 + k)
    array.set(tally, 0, 0)
    array.set(tally, 1, 0)

    // GENERATED - twenty-one addPair calls, each naming a slot of packAll.
    addPair(use01, sym01, 0)
    addPair(use02, sym02, 1)
    addPair(use03, sym03, 2)
    addPair(use04, sym04, 3)
    addPair(use05, sym05, 4)
    addPair(use06, sym06, 5)
    addPair(use07, sym07, 6)
    addPair(use08, sym08, 7)
    addPair(use09, sym09, 8)
    addPair(use10, sym10, 9)
    addPair(use11, sym11, 10)
    addPair(use12, sym12, 11)
    addPair(use13, sym13, 12)
    addPair(use14, sym14, 13)
    addPair(use15, sym15, 14)
    addPair(use16, sym16, 15)
    addPair(use17, sym17, 16)
    addPair(use18, sym18, 17)
    addPair(use19, sym19, 18)
    addPair(use20, sym20, 19)
    addPair(use21, sym21, 20)

    float bestKey    = na
    float best2Key   = na
    float worstKey   = na
    float worst2Key  = na
    int   bestLen    = 0
    int   best2Len   = 0
    int   worstLen   = 0
    int   worst2Len  = 0
    float bestAvg    = na
    float bestHi     = na
    float bestScore  = na
    int   bestCls    = 0
    float avgMin     = na
    float avgMax     = na
    float hiMin      = na
    float hiMax      = na
    float rMin       = na
    float rMax       = na
    float lMin       = na
    float lMax       = na
    int   trusted    = 0
    int   overflowed = 0
    int   redFree    = 0

    // FIRST PASS - the extremes, and nothing else.
    //
    // The Balanced score mixes two quantities that share no unit: entries per
    // window and a proportion. There is no honest constant that converts one
    // into the other, so each is stretched onto 0..1 across the rows that
    // qualify - and a stretch cannot be applied until both ends of it are
    // known. Hence two passes over the same lengths rather than one.
    for k = 0 to usedN - 1
        overflowed += array.get(agX, k)
        c = array.get(agC, k)
        if c >= minClosures
            a  = 1.0 * array.get(agE, k) / c
            h  = a + math.sqrt(math.max(a * a - a, 0.0)) / math.sqrt(c)
            rh = redHi(array.get(agR, k), c)
            // redHi reused rather than reinvented: links, like red windows,
            // is a count of closures, and "a length with a bigger sample
            // earns more trust in an equally clean record" is exactly the
            // question redHi already answers for Red.
            lh = redHi(array.get(agD, k), c)
            trusted += 1
            if array.get(agR, k) == 0
                redFree += 1

            avgMin := na(avgMin) or a < avgMin ? a : avgMin
            avgMax := na(avgMax) or a > avgMax ? a : avgMax
            hiMin  := na(hiMin) or h < hiMin ? h : hiMin
            hiMax  := na(hiMax) or h > hiMax ? h : hiMax
            rMin   := na(rMin) or rh < rMin ? rh : rMin
            rMax   := na(rMax) or rh > rMax ? rh : rMax
            lMin   := na(lMin) or lh < lMin ? lh : lMin
            lMax   := na(lMax) or lh > lMax ? lh : lMax

    tailW = tailWeight / 100.0

    // SECOND PASS - the key every row is ranked and ordered on.
    for k = 0 to usedN - 1
        c = array.get(agC, k)
        array.set(agScore, k, float(na))
        if c <= 0
            // Nothing closed at this length, so there is no column to sort it
            // on and length order is the only order left. Above the untrusted
            // band and below the out-of-range one.
            array.set(sortKey, k, 100000000.0 + k)
        else
            a  = 1.0 * array.get(agE, k) / c
            h  = a + math.sqrt(math.max(a * a - a, 0.0)) / math.sqrt(c)
            rh = redHi(array.get(agR, k), c)
            lh = redHi(array.get(agD, k), c)
            d  = array.get(agD, k)
            l  = sweepFrom + k * sweepStep

            nH = na(hiMax) or hiMax <= hiMin ? 0.0 : (h - hiMin) / (hiMax - hiMin)
            nR = na(rMax) or rMax <= rMin ? 0.0 : (rh - rMin) / (rMax - rMin)
            nL = na(lMax) or lMax <= lMin ? 0.0 : (lh - lMin) / (lMax - lMin)
            score = (1.0 - tailW) * nH + tailW * nR

            qual = c >= minClosures
            if qual
                array.set(agScore, k, score)

            key = rankBy == 'Avg' ? a :
                 rankBy == 'Avg +1σ' ? h :
                 rankBy == 'Red share' ? rh + h / 1000000.0 :
                 rankBy == 'Links, then Score' ? (qual ? d * 4.0 + nL * 2.0 + score : h) :
                 qual ? score : h
            array.set(sortKey, k, qual ? key : 1000000.0 + key)

            // Best and worst are the qualifying rows' business alone. An
            // untrusted row has a place in the order, and a place is not a
            // verdict - it must never wear a star.
            if qual
                if na(bestKey) or key < bestKey
                    best2Key := bestKey
                    best2Len := bestLen
                    bestKey := key
                    bestLen := l
                    bestAvg := a
                    bestHi  := h
                    bestScore := score
                    bestCls := c
                else if na(best2Key) or key < best2Key
                    best2Key := key
                    best2Len := l

                if na(worstKey) or key > worstKey
                    worst2Key := worstKey
                    worst2Len := worstLen
                    worstKey := key
                    worstLen := l
                else if na(worst2Key) or key > worst2Key
                    worst2Key := key
                    worst2Len := l

    // Best first. Not named 'order': that is a Pine namespace, and
    // order.ascending would stop resolving the moment a local shadowed it.
    ord = array.sort_indices(sortKey, order.ascending)
    for k = 0 to SWEEP_N - 1
        array.set(rankOrder, k, array.get(ord, k))

    array.set(rankI, 0, trusted)
    array.set(rankI, 1, overflowed)
    array.set(rankI, 2, redFree)
    array.set(rankI, 3, bestLen)
    array.set(rankI, 4, best2Len)
    array.set(rankI, 5, worstLen)
    array.set(rankI, 6, worst2Len)
    array.set(rankI, 7, bestCls)
    array.set(rankF, 0, na(avgMin) or na(avgMax) ? 0.0 : avgMax - avgMin)
    array.set(rankF, 1, bestAvg)
    array.set(rankF, 2, bestHi)
    array.set(rankF, 3, bestScore)

// The length holding 'Ranking position to trade' in the order just computed,
// as a SLOT of the swept range. -1 when fewer rows than the position asks for
// have cleared the win threshold - trusted rows occupy ranked positions
// 0..trusted-1 contiguously, the same band arithmetic the middle pair rides
// on, so this is an index and not a search.
pickSlot() =>
    array.get(rankI, 0) > pickPos - 1 ? array.get(rankOrder, pickPos - 1) : -1

posFor = tablePos == 'Middle left' ? position.middle_left :
     tablePos == 'Middle center' ? position.middle_center :
     tablePos == 'Top right' ? position.top_right :
     tablePos == 'Bottom right' ? position.bottom_right :
     tablePos == 'Top center' ? position.top_center :
     tablePos == 'Bottom center' ? position.bottom_center :
     position.middle_right

txtSize = txtSizeName == 'Small' ? size.small : txtSizeName == 'Normal' ? size.normal : size.tiny

// How much history this actually saw. The answer to "is it using everything" is
// not a setting - it is how many bars TradingView loaded, which depends on the
// plan and the timeframe, and it is printed in the footer so it can be checked
// rather than assumed.
var int firstBarTime = na
if na(firstBarTime)
    firstBarTime := time

// Four blocks of ten columns with a blank between them, and the tallest a
// single block can be plus the header rows and the footer stack - footer,
// verdict, ROTATION, the PAIRS table and the LAST WIN line. The declared size
// has to exceed every index ever written, or the write is an out-of-bounds
// error that kills the script rather than a cell quietly going missing.
//
// EIGHTY rows, and the arithmetic behind the number: rowsPerBlock reaches 40,
// the ROTATION row sits at rowsPerBlock + 4, the worst-window line one under
// it, the pairs header one under that, and the pairs table is now as tall as
// the watchlist has non-idle pairs - twenty-one at the very most, where it
// used to be a fixed five. 40 + 7 + 26 is 73 for the LAST WIN line, and eighty
// leaves the margin. It was sixty while the block was five rows tall; the
// clear below moves with it.
//
// The stride is 11 and it appears in five places - this declaration, the clear,
// hdrSpan, and the two placements. They have to move together. It was 9 until
// the Score column, 10 until the Links column, and both times all five moved at
// once; a half-applied change here draws a table that looks almost right.
//
// Nothing else carries the literal. merge_cells, the footer and the verdict row
// all ride on hdrSpan and follow it by themselves - checked, not assumed.
var table swTable = table.new(posFor, MAX_BLOCKS * 11 - 1, 80, border_width = 1)

// THE DAY CLOSES table, and its second position on the chart. Four blocks of
// six columns with a blank between them - 4 x 7 - 1 is 27 - and forty-seven
// rows, which is the largest 'Closes per block' plus the title and the column
// headers. The declared size has to exceed every index ever written, or a
// write is an out-of-bounds error that kills the script rather than a cell
// going quietly missing.
CLS_BLOCKS = 4
clsPosFor = clsPos == 'Bottom left' ? position.bottom_left :
     clsPos == 'Middle left' ? position.middle_left :
     clsPos == 'Bottom center' ? position.bottom_center :
     clsPos == 'Top center' ? position.top_center :
     position.top_left

var table clsTable = table.new(clsPosFor, CLS_BLOCKS * 7 - 1, 47, border_width = 1)

// The ladder's colours, the other half of ladderStep - copied from
// mar1Scanner.pine for the same reason the two step functions were: the PAIRS
// table is that table, and a row here has to be the same colour as the diamond
// on the chart and the row in the scanner. The transparency is an argument
// because the scanner's two tables want different weights; this file uses the
// open table's 55.
stepColor(int step, int transp) =>
    step == 1 ? color.new(color.white, transp) :
     step == 2 ? color.new(#22C55E, transp) :
     step == 3 ? color.new(#15803D, transp) :
     step == 4 ? color.new(#166534, transp) :
     step == 5 ? color.new(#EA580C, transp) :
     step == 6 ? color.new(#DC2626, transp > 25 ? transp - 25 : 0) :
     step == 8 ? color.new(#450A0A, transp > 25 ? transp - 25 : 0) :
     step == 7 ? color.new(color.silver, 80) :
     color.new(#333333, 60)

stepText(int step) =>
    step == 1 ? color.black : color.white

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The drawing, as functions - and why they are functions
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pine puts a ceiling on the compiled size of a single `if` statement -
// CE10205, "The if statement is too long" - and the redraw block crossed it
// when the cockpit grew its pick row and the pairs columns. A function body
// compiles as its own unit (sweepOne above is far larger than any of these),
// so the three drawing stages live here and the redraw block below calls
// them: same code, same order, one call site each. A useful side effect:
// their locals leave the #main variable budget.
//
// They READ globals - inputs, the palette, the ag* totals, the rot*
// bookkeeping, swTable - and only ever WRITE table cells. The one drawing
// line that must write a GLOBAL, the LAST WIN line with its lastWinTxt,
// stays in the redraw block: Pine forbids a function assigning a global.

// The full ranking - sweep header, column headers, the fifteen rows, the
// footer and the verdict line. Layout, break-even, the middle pair and the
// pick index are derived here from the arguments and the inputs.
drawRanking(int[] rank, int scanned, int unresolved, int trusted, int overflowed, int redFree, float span,
     int bestLen, int best2Len, int worstLen, int worst2Len, float bestAvg, float bestHi, float bestScore, int bestCls) =>
    // The layout is settled BEFORE the rows are drawn, and the order matters:
    // clipping afterwards could leave the header naming a best length whose row
    // was never drawn, which is the one way a highlight can lie.
    blocks = int(math.ceil(usedN / math.max(rowsPerBlock, 1)))
    blocks := math.min(math.max(blocks, 1), MAX_BLOCKS)
    shown  = math.min(usedN, blocks * rowsPerBlock)

    hdrSpan = blocks * 11 - 2
    footRow = rowsPerBlock + 2
    verdRow = rowsPerBlock + 3

    breakEven = 1.0 + payoutPct / 100.0

    // THE TWO MIDDLE ROWS, as DRAWN POSITIONS rather than as lengths, and the
    // reason that works is the band arithmetic up in sortKey: every qualifying
    // row keeps its raw key, every unqualifying one is pushed past 1e6. So the
    // qualifying rows occupy ranked positions 0..trusted-1 CONTIGUOUSLY, and the
    // middle of the ranking is an index rather than a search.
    //
    // Middle of the SORTED ORDER, not the arithmetic mean of the key. On a
    // skewed column those are different rows, and the positional reading is the
    // one that belongs beside ★ ☆ ▽ ▼ - all four of those are positions too.
    // What this pair says is "half the table is better than this and half is
    // worse", which is a fact about the ranking; nearest-to-the-mean would be a
    // fact about the key's distribution and would be free to land anywhere.
    //
    // An EVEN count has a genuine middle pair - at ten qualifying rows it is
    // positions 4 and 5, which straddle the midpoint. An ODD count has a single median row,
    // and the second mark then goes to the row directly after it: two marks were
    // asked for, and there is no symmetric second on an odd list.
    //
    // Below three qualifying rows there is no middle worth naming - with one or
    // two rows every row is already an extreme - so nothing is marked.
    int midA = -1
    int midB = -1
    if trusted >= 3
        midA := int((trusted - 1) / 2)
        midB := int(trusted / 2)
        if midB <= midA
            midB := midA + 1

    pickIdx = pickPos - 1

    // The winner, named in words at the top. The highlighted row answers it too,
    // but a highlight has to be found and a sentence does not.
    // The range that was actually swept, and why it is not the one that was
    // typed when it is not. First, Step and Last are all settings now and
    // nothing reconciles them, so the two ways they can fall short each get
    // named - one has a fix and the other does not.
    rangeMissed = sweepLast != sweepTo
    rangeText = ' MA ' + str.tostring(sweepFrom) + '-' + str.tostring(sweepLast) +
         ' step ' + str.tostring(sweepStep) +
         (not rangeMissed ? '' :
          slotsShort ? '  ·  ASKED ' + str.tostring(sweepTo) + ', ONLY ' + str.tostring(SWEEP_N) + ' SLOTS - raise Step to reach it' :
          '  ·  asked ' + str.tostring(sweepTo) + ', the step does not land on it')

    // Three verdicts rather than two, because the +1σ column earns one. A length
    // whose average pays and whose pessimistic average does not is a real state
    // and the most common one worth knowing about: it means the edge is there in
    // this sample and has not yet been shown to survive another.
    bestPays = bestLen > 0 and bestHi < breakEven
    bestThin = bestLen > 0 and not bestPays and bestAvg < breakEven

    headBg = #0E2A1B
    table.cell(swTable, 0, 0,
         ' MA SWEEP · PAIRS  ' + timeframe.period + '  ·  ' + str.tostring(scanned) + ' pairs  · ' + rangeText +
         (restrictToHours ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(startHour) + '-' + str.tostring(endHour) + ' UTC+1' : '  ·  all hours') +
         '  ·  break-even ' + str.tostring(breakEven, '#.00') +
         (bestLen > 0 ?
              '  ·  ★ BEST ON ' + rankBy + ': MA ' + str.tostring(bestLen) + ' at ' + str.tostring(bestAvg, '#.00000') +
              ' avg, ' + str.tostring(bestHi, '#.00000') + ' +1σ' +
              (na(bestScore) ? '' : ', score ' + str.tostring(bestScore, '#.000')) +
              (bestPays ? ' - pays even at +1σ' :
               bestThin ? ' - pays on avg, NOT at +1σ' :
               ' - ABOVE break-even, none of these pay') :
              '  ·  NO LENGTH HAS ' + str.tostring(minClosures) + '+ WINS YET') + ' ',
         bgcolor = headBg, text_color = bestPays and not rangeMissed ? #6EF0A5 : #FFC163, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, 0, '', bgcolor = headBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, 0, hdrSpan, 0)

    // One header row per block that has something under it. The labels and
    // tooltips are built ONCE, above the loop - nothing in them depends on b,
    // and the Score and Links tooltips are the longest string concatenations
    // in the file, so rebuilding them per block was up to four copies of the
    // same work per redraw.
    hdrRedTxt   = ' Red ' + str.tostring(redAt) + '+ '
    // UNPADDED, like the End column of the day closes and for the same reason
    // (user request, 2026-08-16): this is the LAST column of a block, so
    // whatever slack the merged rows below leave over ends up here, and its
    // own content is one or two digits. Every other cell in this project is
    // ' text ' so the columns cannot touch; this one is narrow enough that a
    // space each side is a third of it.
    hdrLinksTxt = 'Links' + str.tostring(deepAt) + '+'
    tipMA    = 'Rows are ordered best first, on ' + rankBy + ' - change that in Sweep output. ★ best   ☆ second best   ≈ the middle pair   ▽ second worst   ▼ worst, over the rows that clear the win threshold.\n\nThe two blue ≈ rows are the middle of the ORDER, not the rows nearest the average of the column: half the qualifying rows rank better than them and half rank worse. On an odd count there is one true median row and the mark beside it goes to the row directly below. Blue because the middle of a ranking is not a verdict - green means take it and red means avoid it, and this pair means neither.\n\nThe rows under the threshold follow, greyed and unmarked, in the same order on the same column - they are placed but not trusted. Last of all come the lengths that closed nothing, which have no column to be placed on.'
    tipWins  = 'Windows closed at this length, summed over every scanned pair. The same number the scanner prints after CLOSED.'
    tipAvg   = 'Entries per closed window, which is 1 / per-entry win rate. Green below break-even, red above.'
    tipHi    = 'avg + sqrt(avg² - avg) / sqrt(wins) - the average plus one standard error, and the column the table is SORTED and RANKED on. Entries-per-window is a geometric count, whose variance is avg² - avg, so this is the same sample read pessimistically: what the average would look like if this run had been one error bar unlucky. A length with forty wins cannot outrank one with four hundred on luck alone, because this column has already charged it for the small sample.'
    tipRed   = 'How many closed windows cost ' + str.tostring(redAt) + ' entries or more, and what share of this length\'s wins that was. ' + str.tostring(redAt) + ' is where the diamond ladder turns red, so this is the count of runs that went red at least once.\n\nThis is the column the average cannot give you. Two lengths can average the same and be nothing alike - one closes almost everything on the second entry, the other pays for cheap wins with runs that go five and six deep - and only this column tells them apart.\n\nThe share is coloured against what a geometric run of that average would produce on its own: green means this length goes deep LESS often than its own average implies, red means the tail is fatter than the mean is admitting. Grey means the average is at or below 1 and the comparison has nothing to say.'
    tipScore = 'The two columns to the left, combined into one number: 0 is the best row in the table on both, 1 is the worst on both.\n\nHow it is built. Avg +1σ and the Red share are stretched separately onto 0..1 across the rows that clear the win threshold, then mixed - ' + str.tostring(100 - tailWeight) + '% average, ' + str.tostring(tailWeight) + '% tail, set by Tail weight. They cannot simply be added: one is entries per window and the other is a proportion, and no honest constant converts one into the other.\n\nThe Red side is read pessimistically, the same way Avg +1σ is: a length with no deep runs in forty windows has not proved as much as one with no deep runs in four hundred, and the score charges it for that.\n\nWhat it is NOT: an absolute measure. 0 and 1 are the best and worst rows CURRENTLY on screen, so changing the range or the win threshold changes what the number means. Compare lengths to each other with it, never to a fixed standard - the four columns to the left are the absolute ones.\n\nIt is blank on a row under the win threshold, because that row is not part of the population the 0..1 was stretched across. Those rows are ordered on Avg +1σ instead.' + (rankBy == 'Balanced (+1σ & Red)' ? '\n\nThe table is sorted and ranked on this column.' : rankBy == 'Links, then Score' ? '\n\nThe table is sorted on Links first and settles the ties on THIS column. Since Links reads zero on most rows, this is usually the column actually deciding the order.' : '\n\nThe table is currently sorted on ' + rankBy + ', so this column is shown but not acted on.')
    tipWorst = 'The most entries ONE window cost at this length, and the pair it happened on. This is a count of entries, not an identifier - 11 means a single series took eleven attempts before it closed on a win. Where Red says how OFTEN this length goes deep, this says how deep the single worst run got, and which pair to go and look at. 99 is the ceiling the packed format clamps to, and means 99 or more.'
    tipPairs = 'How many pairs closed at least one window at this length. A good average carried by three pairs is a different finding from the same average across twenty.'
    tipLinks = 'How many times a window of ' + str.tostring(deepAt) + '+ entries opened within ' + str.tostring(chainWithin) + ' minutes of another ' + str.tostring(deepAt) + '+ window CLOSING ON THE SAME PAIR, summed over every scanned pair. Shallow windows in the gap are ignored.\n\nRed says how often this length goes deep and Worst says how deep the single worst run got. Neither says whether the deep ones arrived TOGETHER, and that is the one that kills a recovery ladder: evenly spaced deep windows let the stake return to base between them, two in a row do not.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS NUMBER IS NOT.\n\nIt is not the CHAINS figure from checks/closed-chains.js. That script counts across the WHOLE watchlist - a deep window on one pair followed by a deep window on another - and this cannot see that at all: one packed number crosses request.security per pair-and-length, and there is no shared timeline on this side to lay them on. What you get here is a strict lower bound on that figure. It is also LINKS rather than runs: a chain of three deep windows is two links here and one chain there.\n\nAnd the tolerance is not the script\'s --within either. Merging every pair onto one timeline puts windows about sixteen minutes apart, so two minutes is tight there. One pair on its own sees a window roughly every twenty-three hours, so the equivalent tolerance scales by the number of pairs - which is why the default here is 180 and not 2.\n\nExpect zero on most rows. This is a tripwire, not a scale: it is meant to be blank until a length is one you should not be trading.\n\nThe table is SORTED on it by default - "Links, then Score" under Rank and sort by. A real difference in the raw count always wins first; among rows that tie on the count (usually every row, at zero), a sample-size-aware reading of it decides - the same caution behind Avg +1σ, now applied here - and Score settles anything still tied. No amount of Score or sample size can move a row past one with a genuinely different link count.'
    for b = 0 to blocks - 1
        h = b * 11
        table.cell(swTable, h + 0, 1, ' MA ',   bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipMA)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 1, 1, ' Wins ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipWins)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 2, 1, ' Entries ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 3, 1, ' Avg ',  bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipAvg)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 4, 1, ' Avg +1σ ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipHi)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 5, 1, hdrRedTxt, bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipRed)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 6, 1, ' Score ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipScore)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 7, 1, ' Worst · pair ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipWorst)
        table.cell(swTable, h + 8, 1, ' Pairs ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipPairs)
        // Last in the row on purpose, and not next to Red where it reads best.
        // The Score tooltip says "the two columns to the left", and Score sits
        // at h + 6 with Avg +1σ and Red at h + 4 and h + 5. Inserting anything
        // between them turns that sentence into a lie with nothing to catch it.
        // Append; do not insert.
        table.cell(swTable, h + 9, 1, hdrLinksTxt, bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize, tooltip = tipLinks)

    // i is where the row is DRAWN, k is which LENGTH it is. They were the same
    // number until the table learned to sort, and keeping them apart is what
    // stops the two from being quietly confused again: every array read below
    // takes k, and every placement takes i.
    for i = 0 to shown - 1
        k   = array.get(rank, i)
        len = sweepFrom + k * sweepStep
        cls = array.get(agC, k)
        ent = array.get(agE, k)
        red = array.get(agR, k)
        lnk = array.get(agD, k)
        wst = array.get(agW, k)
        who = array.get(agWho, k)
        prs = array.get(agP, k)
        scr = array.get(agScore, k)
        avg = cls > 0 ? 1.0 * ent / cls : float(na)
        ok  = cls >= minClosures

        // One standard error on the mean of a geometric count, whose variance is
        // avg² - avg. The clamp at zero covers the one case that can drive that
        // negative: a widened success scope can close a window with no entry in
        // it at all, which pulls the average under 1 and makes the variance term
        // meaningless rather than merely small.
        se = na(avg) or cls <= 0 ? float(na) : math.sqrt(math.max(avg * avg - avg, 0.0)) / math.sqrt(cls)
        hi = na(se) ? float(na) : avg + se

        // The deep runs, as a count and as a share of this length's own wins.
        // The share is what makes two lengths comparable - 40 red windows out of
        // 200 and out of 2000 are not the same finding, and the raw count alone
        // would rank a length well simply for having closed less.
        rsh = cls > 0 ? 100.0 * red / cls : float(na)

        // What that share WOULD be if windows failed independently at this
        // length's own rate. Entries per window is a geometric count with
        // p = 1 / avg, so the chance a window needs redAt entries or more is
        // (1 - p) ^ (redAt - 1), and comparing against it is what turns the
        // column from a number into a verdict: above the line the tail is
        // fatter than the mean is admitting, which is precisely the shape that
        // survives a backtest on averages and then breaks a recovery ladder.
        //
        // na below an average of 1, where the model has nothing to say - a
        // widened success scope can close windows that cost no entry at all.
        expShare = na(avg) or avg <= 1.0 ? float(na) : 100.0 * math.pow((avg - 1.0) / avg, redAt - 1)

        // The four ranked rows, each excluding the ones above it, so a table with
        // two or three qualifying rows cannot paint one row twice. With exactly
        // one qualifying row it is best and nothing else, which is correct - it
        // has nothing to be better or worse than.
        isBest  = ok and bestLen   > 0 and len == bestLen
        isWorst = ok and worstLen  > 0 and len == worstLen  and not isBest
        isBest2 = ok and best2Len  > 0 and len == best2Len  and not isBest and not isWorst
        isWors2 = ok and worst2Len > 0 and len == worst2Len and not isBest and not isWorst and not isBest2

        // The middle pair, LAST in the precedence chain. On a short table the
        // middle can land on a row that is already an extreme - with five
        // qualifying rows the second middle position IS the second worst - and
        // an extreme is the louder fact: a row that is both the worst in the
        // table and the middle of it should read as the worst. One mark then
        // simply does not appear, which is correct rather than a gap.
        //
        // Tested against the DRAWN position i, not against the length, because
        // that is what midA and midB are.
        isMid = ok and (i == midA or i == midB) and
             not isBest and not isWorst and not isBest2 and not isWors2

        // The stripe follows the DRAWN position, not the length - sorted rows
        // striped by length would alternate in a pattern with no relation to
        // what is on screen, which is worse than no stripe at all.
        rowBg = isBest ? C_BEST :
             isWorst ? C_WORST :
             isBest2 ? C_BEST2 :
             isWors2 ? C_WORST2 :
             isMid ? C_MID :
             not ok ? C_ROW_NO :
             i % 2 == 0 ? C_ROW_A : C_ROW_B
        rowFg = isBest ? C_BEST_T :
             isWorst ? C_WORST_T :
             isBest2 ? C_BEST2_T :
             isWors2 ? C_WORST2_T :
             isMid ? C_MID_T :
             not ok ? C_TXT_NO :
             C_TXT

        // Both average columns keep the break-even verdict even on a ranked row,
        // except on the two solid ones - dark text on lime or on red is the only
        // pairing here that cannot also carry a hue and stay readable.
        avgFg = isBest or isWorst ? rowFg :
             not ok ? C_TXT_NO :
             avg < breakEven ? #6EF0A5 : #FF7B7B
        hiFg = isBest or isWorst ? rowFg :
             not ok or na(hi) ? C_TXT_NO :
             hi < breakEven ? #6EF0A5 : #FF7B7B
        redFg = isBest or isWorst ? rowFg :
             not ok or na(rsh) ? C_TXT_NO :
             na(expShare) ? C_TXT :
             rsh <= expShare ? #6EF0A5 : #FF7B7B
        // A gradient rather than two colours, because this one is a POSITION on
        // a scale rather than a pass or a fail: 0.31 and 0.34 are not two
        // different verdicts, and painting them as if they were would invent a
        // boundary the number does not have.
        scrFg = isBest or isWorst ? rowFg :
             not ok or na(scr) ? C_TXT_NO :
             color.from_gradient(scr, 0.0, 1.0, #6EF0A5, #FF7B7B)

        // ≈ rather than a fifth arrow: the four existing glyphs all point at one
        // end of the table or the other, and this row points at neither.
        //
        // ► is the PICK - the ranking position the Rotation group's pickPos
        // names, the row the user actually trades. A prefix rather than a
        // sixth colour, so it can land on a row that already wears a mark (at
        // the default pick of 2 it sits on ☆ permanently) without stealing
        // the ranking's colour language.
        isPick = ok and i == pickIdx and i < trusted
        tag = isBest ? ' ★ ' : isBest2 ? ' ☆ ' : isWorst ? ' ▼ ' : isWors2 ? ' ▽ ' : isMid ? ' ≈ ' : ' '
        if isPick
            tag := '►' + tag

        blk = int(math.floor(i / math.max(rowsPerBlock, 1)))
        c   = blk * 11
        r   = i - blk * rowsPerBlock + 2

        table.cell(swTable, c + 0, r, tag + str.tostring(len) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, c + 1, r, ' ' + str.tostring(cls) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, c + 2, r, ' ' + str.tostring(ent) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, c + 3, r, na(avg) ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(avg, '#.00000') + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = avgFg, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, c + 4, r, na(hi)  ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(hi,  '#.00000') + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = hiFg,  text_size = txtSize)
        // The deep runs. A real zero is worth printing here - unlike Worst, where
        // a zero would read as "the worst window cost nothing", zero red windows
        // is the best thing this column can say and has to be visible as such.
        table.cell(swTable, c + 5, r, na(rsh) ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(red) + ' · ' + str.tostring(rsh, '#.0') + '% ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = redFg, text_size = txtSize)
        // The combined score, read straight out of the array the sort used, so
        // the number on screen and the number the row was placed by cannot drift
        // apart.
        table.cell(swTable, c + 6, r, na(scr) ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(scr, '#.000') + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = scrFg, text_size = txtSize)
        // The count and the pair in one cell. Blank rather than '0' when nothing
        // closed at this length, because a zero here would read as "the worst
        // window cost nothing" rather than "there was no window".
        table.cell(swTable, c + 7, r, wst > 0 ? ' ' + str.tostring(wst) + ' · ' + who + ' ' : ' - ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, c + 8, r, ' ' + str.tostring(prs) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
        // The Links column. A real zero PRINTS - it is the answer on most rows
        // and the good one, exactly like the Red count and unlike Worst, where
        // a zero would read as "the worst window cost nothing". A dash here
        // would hide the difference between "no deep window followed another"
        // and "nothing closed at all", so the dash is kept for the second case
        // only, which is what cls == 0 is.
        //
        // Coloured only when it fires. Grey at zero rather than green: this
        // column is not a score to be read down, it is a flag to be noticed,
        // and a wall of green zeros would make the one amber cell harder to
        // find rather than easier.
        //
        // Unpadded, like its header - see the note there.
        table.cell(swTable, c + 9, r, cls == 0 ? '-' : str.tostring(lnk), bgcolor = rowBg,
             text_color = isBest or isWorst ? rowFg : lnk > 0 ? #FFC163 : C_TXT_NO, text_size = txtSize)

    // How much history this saw. Nothing in this file windows the history unless
    // 'Bars of history per pair' is set - this is simply how many bars
    // TradingView handed the script, which is a property of the plan and the
    // timeframe. If the number looks small, scroll left until the chart stops
    // loading more, then let it recompute.
    //
    // Six things are shouted rather than mentioned, because each of them means
    // a number above is not what it appears to be: a capped history, a ticker
    // that did not resolve, a total that overflowed the packed format, rows the
    // block layout could not fit, a Red column that is empty everywhere - which
    // looks like good news and is usually the threshold set too high or a
    // history too short to have grown a tail - and a Links tolerance narrower
    // than one bar. All six are silent everywhere else.
    //
    // The sixth is the newest and the quietest failure of the lot. The smallest
    // gap this script can ever measure is ONE BAR: an entry taken on the bar
    // that closed a window belongs to the window it closed, so a follower opens
    // on the next bar at the earliest. Set the tolerance below the bar length -
    // two minutes on a five-minute chart, say - and the Links column is
    // identically zero by construction, on every row, forever. Zero reads as
    // "no length does this", which is the opposite of what happened. The header
    // of this file recommends raising the timeframe as the second-best lever
    // against a timeout, so this is a foot-gun the file itself hands over.
    tolBars = chainWithin * 60 < timeframe.in_seconds()
    footBg  = C_FOOT
    table.cell(swTable, 0, footRow,
         ' history ' + str.tostring(bar_index + 1) + ' bars, from ' +
         (na(firstBarTime) ? '-' : str.format_time(firstBarTime, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1')) +
         (scanBars > 0 ? '  ·  CAPPED AT ' + str.tostring(scanBars) + ' BARS PER PAIR' : '') +
         (unresolved > 0 ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(unresolved) + ' UNRESOLVED' : '') +
         (overflowed > 0 ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(overflowed) + ' TOTALS OVERFLOWED AND WERE DROPPED' : '') +
         (shown < usedN ? '  ·  ' + str.tostring(usedN - shown) + ' ROWS NOT DRAWN - raise Rows per block' : '') +
         (trusted > 0 and redFree == trusted ? '  ·  NO LENGTH HAS A SINGLE ' + str.tostring(redAt) + '+ WINDOW - lower the red threshold, or there is too little history to see a tail' : '') +
         (tolBars ? '  ·  LINKS TOLERANCE ' + str.tostring(chainWithin) + ' MIN IS NARROWER THAN ONE ' + timeframe.period + ' BAR - that column can only read zero, raise it or drop the timeframe' : '') +
         '  ·  spread ' + (span > 0 ? str.tostring(span, '#.000') : '-') +
         '  ·  ' + str.tostring(trusted) + ' of ' + str.tostring(shown) + ' rows have ' + str.tostring(minClosures) + '+ wins ',
         bgcolor = footBg, text_color = unresolved > 0 or overflowed > 0 or shown < usedN or tolBars or (trusted > 0 and redFree == trusted) ? #FFC163 : #C9D1DA, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, footRow, '', bgcolor = footBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, footRow, hdrSpan, footRow)

    // The honesty line. The spread between best and worst is the whole of what
    // the table found, and it means nothing until it is read against the error
    // bar of a single average - which the Avg +1σ column now prints per row, and
    // which is summarised here for the winner. Under three of those, the ranking
    // above is noise in five decimals.
    errBar = na(bestAvg) or bestCls <= 0 ? float(na) : math.sqrt(math.max(bestAvg * bestAvg - bestAvg, 0.0)) / math.sqrt(bestCls)
    noisy  = span > 0 and not na(errBar) and errBar > 0 and span < errBar * 3
    verdBg = noisy ? #5C3A00 : #0E2A1B

    table.cell(swTable, 0, verdRow,
         (na(errBar) ? ' not enough data to judge anything yet ' :
          noisy ? ' SPREAD ' + str.tostring(span, '#.000') + ' IS INSIDE THE NOISE (±' + str.tostring(errBar, '#.000') + ' on the winner) - every length here is the same ' :
          ' spread ' + str.tostring(span, '#.000') + ' clears the noise (±' + str.tostring(errBar, '#.000') + ' on the winner) - MA ' + str.tostring(bestLen) + ' is a real winner '),
         bgcolor = verdBg, text_color = noisy or na(errBar) ? #FFC163 : #6EF0A5, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, verdRow, '', bgcolor = verdBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, verdRow, hdrSpan, verdRow)

// THE DAY CLOSES - every window the day closed, one per line, oldest at the
// top. It is the whole record of the day and not a summary of one: there was a
// ROTATION JOURNAL beside it for one revision, one row per recompute with four
// closes shown as a taste, and once this table existed that was four records of
// what this holds in full. It went (user decision, 2026-08-16) and nothing was
// lost with it - a recompute is visible here as the line where the MA column
// changes, and the window that caused it as the ► on the line above.
//
// The list fills a block downwards and wraps into the next block to its right,
// exactly as the ranking does, so a day of a hundred closes is one screen. Past
// four blocks it pages, and the title always says which records are drawn out
// of how many - a table that quietly ends is the one failure this file will not
// have.
//
// Two things are marked rather than merely printed, and both are round
// boundaries seen from a different side: a record that cost MORE than the
// recompute threshold carries ► in the last column and is the reason the round
// ended, and an MA that differs from the record above it is printed with an
// arrow, which is where the length actually moved. They are usually one line
// apart - the deep window ends the round, the next close is on the new length.
drawCloses() =>
    n   = array.size(clsAt)
    per = clsPerBlock
    cap = per * CLS_BLOCKS

    // The day's arithmetic, over every record rather than over the page. A
    // page is a window onto the day; a total that changed when the page did
    // would be a total of nothing.
    totEnt = 0
    totWin = 0
    deep   = 0
    fired  = 0
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            eT = array.get(clsEnt, i)
            cT = array.get(clsCnt, i)
            totEnt += eT
            totWin += cT
            // The lower bound on one window's cost, and the same expression
            // rotWatch ends a round on: with cT closes sharing eT entries no
            // single window can have cost more than eT - (cT - 1). On the
            // ordinary record, where cT is 1, it is simply eT.
            oneT = eT - (cT - 1)
            if oneT > deep
                deep := oneT
            if oneT > rotDeepAt
                fired += 1

    // int() around every division: Pine's / on two ints is not reliably an
    // integer division, and every quotient here is over positive numbers, so a
    // truncation and a floor are the same thing.
    pages  = n <= 0 ? 1 : int((n + cap - 1) / cap)
    pg     = int(math.min(math.max(clsPage, 1), pages))
    from   = (pg - 1) * cap
    upto   = int(math.min(n, from + cap)) - 1
    shown  = upto - from + 1
    blocks = int(math.min(math.max(int((shown + per - 1) / per), 1), CLS_BLOCKS))
    hdrSpan = blocks * 7 - 2
    dropped = array.get(clsMeta, 0)
    past    = clsPage > pages

    // THREE LINES, NOT ONE (user request, 2026-08-16 - two the same day, then
    // three), and for the reason the ROTATION row was broken up: this cell is
    // merged across every column of the table, so its longest LINE is a FLOOR
    // under the whole width. One line of a hundred and forty characters
    // stretched the blocks to fit it and the slack landed on the last one,
    // whose own columns need almost nothing.
    //
    // THREE rather than two because of the ONE-BLOCK day. A block of this
    // table is about forty characters wide, so on a quiet day - one block, or
    // two - a seventy-character line is still stretching it, and only the
    // busy day where four blocks stand side by side was actually fixed. Three
    // lines put the longest at about fifty, which is as near the block's own
    // width as this much text gets without reading as a paragraph.
    //
    // The split is by kind: what day it is on top, what the day did in the
    // middle, where the table is standing in it underneath.
    //
    // AND THE PAGE NOTE IS NO LONGER A SHOUT. It read 'PAGE 3 IS PAST THE END'
    // in amber, which is a true sentence about a setting the file itself
    // ships with - at twelve per block a page holds forty-eight closes, so the
    // default page 3 exists only on a day that closed ninety-seven of them.
    // A permanent alarm about a default is an alarm nobody reads, so it is now
    // a quiet '(asked 3)' beside the page count, and the amber is kept for the
    // two things that are genuinely wrong: records dropped off the front, and
    // a chosen day with nothing in it.
    pgTxt = pages > 1 or past ?
         ' · page ' + str.tostring(pg) + ' of ' + str.tostring(pages) +
         (past ? ' (asked ' + str.tostring(clsPage) + ')' : '') : ''
    ttl = ' DAY CLOSES' +
         (jrnDayOn ? ' · ' + str.format_time(jrnDayPick, 'yyyy-MM-dd', 'UTC+1') :
          jrnDayAt > 0 ? ' · from ' + str.format_time(jrnDayAt, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') : '') +
         (n == 0 ? (jrnDayOn ? ' · NOTHING RECORDED - outside the loaded history? ' : ' · nothing has closed yet ') :
          '\n ' + str.tostring(totWin) + ' closes · ' + str.tostring(totEnt) + ' entries · avg ' +
          str.tostring(1.0 * totEnt / totWin, '#.00') +
          ' · deepest ' + str.tostring(deep) + ' ' +
          '\n ' + str.tostring(fired) + ' ended a round' +
          ' · showing ' + str.tostring(from + 1) + '-' + str.tostring(upto + 1) + ' of ' + str.tostring(n) +
          pgTxt +
          (dropped > 0 ? ' · ' + str.tostring(dropped) + ' OLDEST DROPPED' : '') + ' ')
    table.cell(clsTable, 0, 0, ttl, bgcolor = C_FOOT,
         text_color = dropped > 0 or n == 0 ? #FFC163 : C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    for kT = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(clsTable, kT, 0, '', bgcolor = C_FOOT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(clsTable, 0, 0, hdrSpan, 0)

    if n == 0
        table.cell(clsTable, 0, 2, jrnDayOn ? ' nothing closed on that day ' : ' nothing has closed on the traded length yet today ',
             bgcolor = C_ROW_NO, text_color = C_TXT_NO, text_size = txtSize)
        for kE = 1 to hdrSpan
            table.cell(clsTable, kE, 2, '', bgcolor = C_ROW_NO, text_size = txtSize)
        table.merge_cells(clsTable, 0, 2, hdrSpan, 2)
    else
        for b = 0 to blocks - 1
            h = b * 7
            // FIRST in the row (user request, 2026-08-16), so a record reads
            // left to right as start -> end. It is the opening minute of the
            // candle the series' FIRST entry was placed on - the other end of
            // the trade the Time column closes.
            table.cell(clsTable, h + 0, 1, ' Start ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
                 tooltip = 'When the series STARTED: the opening minute of the candle its first entry was placed on, UTC+1 - the same reading the scanner\'s Open since column gives a window that is still open. The Time column beside it is the other end, the closing minute of the winning candle.\n\nA dash means the pair sent no stamp: a window older than 16.6 hours, which is past what the wire carries, or a close with no window behind it at all. On the rare record where two windows closed on one chart bar it is the start of the LATER one.')
            table.cell(clsTable, h + 1, 1, ' Time ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
                 tooltip = 'Closing time of the candle the close was detected on, UTC+1 - the same minute the scanner\'s Closed column prints. On a pair bar the chart has no bar of its own for, it is a bar later than the pair\'s own close.')
            table.cell(clsTable, h + 2, 1, ' Pair ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(clsTable, h + 3, 1, ' Ent ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
                 tooltip = 'What the window cost in entries. (2w) marks the rare record where two windows of one pair closed on the same chart bar - the number is then what the two cost together, because that is how it crosses request.security.')
            table.cell(clsTable, h + 4, 1, ' MA ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
                 tooltip = 'The length this window closed on. An arrow means it differs from the record above - that is where the rotation actually moved.')
            // IT WAS 'End' AND A BARE ► until 2026-08-16, unpadded because a
            // single glyph in a padded cell is mostly padding. The glyph said
            // WHICH record ended the round and left the WHEN to be read off
            // the Time column beside it - which was the same minute, since
            // detection and close are one bar. The user asked for the hour of
            // the critical event itself, and that is a different minute: the
            // entry that pushed the window past the threshold, taken while
            // the window was still open. It now prints that, and a non-empty
            // cell is the marker the ► used to be.
            table.cell(clsTable, h + 5, 1, ' Recalc ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
                 tooltip = 'When this window went PAST ' + str.tostring(rotDeepAt) + ' entries - the minute the entry after which a recompute was owed was taken, UTC+1. That is the critical event the rotation fires on, and it happens while the window is still open: the ranking is only re-read later, on the close, because the cost of a window is not known until it pays.\n\nBlank on every window that stayed inside the band, so a filled cell is exactly a round boundary. A dash means the window crossed but the pair sent no stamp.')

        for i = from to upto
            j     = i - from
            blk   = int(j / per)
            c     = blk * 7
            r     = j - blk * per + 2
            e     = array.get(clsEnt, i)
            cnt   = array.get(clsCnt, i)
            one   = e - (cnt - 1)
            lenI  = array.get(clsLen, i)
            moved = i > 0 and lenI != array.get(clsLen, i - 1)
            hot   = one > rotDeepAt
            stI   = array.get(clsSt, i)
            dpI   = array.get(clsDp, i)
            // Striped on the ROW rather than on the record index, so the
            // stripes of two blocks standing side by side line up whatever
            // the block height is.
            bg = r % 2 == 0 ? C_ROW_A : C_ROW_B
            // Dimmer than the close beside it, and deliberately: the close is
            // what the record is OF, the start is context for it.
            table.cell(clsTable, c + 0, r, stI > 0 ? ' ' + str.format_time(stI, 'HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + ' ' : ' - ',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = C_TXT_NO, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(clsTable, c + 1, r, ' ' + str.format_time(array.get(clsAt, i), 'HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + ' ',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(clsTable, c + 2, r, ' ' + array.get(clsWho, i) + ' ',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(clsTable, c + 3, r, ' ' + str.tostring(e) + (cnt > 1 ? ' (' + str.tostring(cnt) + 'w)' : '') + ' ',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = hot ? #FFC163 : #6EF0A5, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(clsTable, c + 4, r, (moved ? ' -> ' : ' ') + str.tostring(lenI) + ' ',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = moved ? #FFC163 : C_TXT_NO, text_size = txtSize)
            // Filled only on a window that crossed the threshold, which is
            // what makes the column its own marker. 'hot' and the stamp agree
            // on the ordinary record - both are "cost more than rotDeepAt" -
            // and can disagree only on the rare two-in-one-bar one, where the
            // cost is a lower bound and the stamp belongs to the later window.
            // The dash names that case rather than hiding it.
            table.cell(clsTable, c + 5, r, dpI > 0 ? ' ' + str.format_time(dpI, 'HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + ' ' : hot ? ' - ' : '',
                 bgcolor = bg, text_color = #FFC163, text_size = txtSize)

// The cockpit's traded row - the one length kept on screen when the ranking
// is hidden, with its own column headers.
drawPickRow(int trusted) =>
    breakEven = 1.0 + payoutPct / 100.0
    // THE TRADED ROW - the part of the ranking that hiding must not hide.
    // It is the row of the length being TRADED, which is the one the last
    // round boundary picked, and NOT whatever holds the picked position at
    // this instant: those are the same row most of the time and differ
    // exactly when a rotation is pending, which is precisely the moment a
    // cockpit must not quietly redraw itself as though it had already
    // happened. The pending pick is named in the ROTATION row underneath,
    // where it reads as news instead of as fact.
    //
    // Same arrays, same formulas as the full table - a shorter drawing of
    // the same facts, never a second computation. Headers carry no
    // tooltips here: the full table owns the essays, this is the pocket
    // edition.
    table.cell(swTable, 0, 0, ' ► traded ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 1, 0, ' Wins ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 2, 0, ' Entries ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 3, 0, ' Avg ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 4, 0, ' Avg +1σ ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 5, 0, ' Red ' + str.tostring(redAt) + '+ ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 6, 0, ' Score ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 7, 0, ' Worst · pair ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.cell(swTable, 8, 0, ' Pairs ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    // Unpadded, like the same column in the full ranking - see the note there.
    table.cell(swTable, 9, 0, 'Links' + str.tostring(deepAt) + '+', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    if activeIdx >= 0
        kP   = activeIdx
        clsP = array.get(agC, kP)
        entP = array.get(agE, kP)
        redP = array.get(agR, kP)
        lnkP = array.get(agD, kP)
        wstP = array.get(agW, kP)
        whoP = array.get(agWho, kP)
        prsP = array.get(agP, kP)
        scrP = array.get(agScore, kP)
        avgP = clsP > 0 ? 1.0 * entP / clsP : float(na)
        seP  = na(avgP) or clsP <= 0 ? float(na) : math.sqrt(math.max(avgP * avgP - avgP, 0.0)) / math.sqrt(clsP)
        hiP  = na(seP) ? float(na) : avgP + seP
        rshP = clsP > 0 ? 100.0 * redP / clsP : float(na)
        expP = na(avgP) or avgP <= 1.0 ? float(na) : 100.0 * math.pow((avgP - 1.0) / avgP, redAt - 1)
        avgFgP = na(avgP) ? C_TXT_NO : avgP < breakEven ? #6EF0A5 : #FF7B7B
        hiFgP  = na(hiP) ? C_TXT_NO : hiP < breakEven ? #6EF0A5 : #FF7B7B
        redFgP = na(rshP) ? C_TXT_NO : na(expP) ? C_TXT : rshP <= expP ? #6EF0A5 : #FF7B7B
        scrFgP = na(scrP) ? C_TXT_NO : color.from_gradient(scrP, 0.0, 1.0, #6EF0A5, #FF7B7B)
        table.cell(swTable, 0, 1, ' ► MA ' + str.tostring(activeLen) + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 1, 1, ' ' + str.tostring(clsP) + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 2, 1, ' ' + str.tostring(entP) + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 3, 1, na(avgP) ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(avgP, '#.00000') + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = avgFgP, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 4, 1, na(hiP)  ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(hiP,  '#.00000') + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = hiFgP,  text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 5, 1, na(rshP) ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(redP) + ' · ' + str.tostring(rshP, '#.0') + '% ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = redFgP, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 6, 1, na(scrP) ? ' - ' : ' ' + str.tostring(scrP, '#.000') + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = scrFgP, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 7, 1, wstP > 0 ? ' ' + str.tostring(wstP) + ' · ' + whoP + ' ' : ' - ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 8, 1, ' ' + str.tostring(prsP) + ' ', bgcolor = C_ROW_A, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 9, 1, clsP == 0 ? '-' : str.tostring(lnkP), bgcolor = C_ROW_A,
             text_color = lnkP > 0 ? #FFC163 : C_TXT_NO, text_size = txtSize)
    else
        // No traded length is a fact worth a row of its own: silence here
        // would read as "the cockpit forgot the ranking", which is the
        // opposite of what happened.
        table.cell(swTable, 0, 1, ' nothing traded yet - only ' + str.tostring(trusted) + ' rows with ' + str.tostring(minClosures) + '+ wins ',
             bgcolor = C_ROW_NO, text_color = #FFC163, text_size = txtSize)
        for k2 = 1 to 9
            table.cell(swTable, k2, 1, '', bgcolor = C_ROW_NO, text_size = txtSize)
        table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, 1, 9, 1)

// The cockpit's live half - the ROTATION row and the PAIRS table. Returns how
// many pair rows it drew, because the LAST WIN line below has to land under
// them and the table is no longer a fixed five rows tall.
drawCockpit(int trusted, int nextLen) =>
    blocks = int(math.ceil(usedN / math.max(rowsPerBlock, 1)))
    blocks := math.min(math.max(blocks, 1), MAX_BLOCKS)
    hdrSpan = blocks * 11 - 2

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE ROTATION ROW - the one line this file exists for, and every number
    // in it was computed on this same confirmed bar.
    //
    // It says four things in order: which length is being TRADED and since
    // when, what the ranking would pick if a round ended right now, which pair
    // to enter, and how the watchlist is distributed across the three states.
    //
    // The pending pick is the row's news. Traded and pending agree most of the
    // time and the clause simply disappears; when they differ, a rotation is
    // one round boundary away and the row turns amber - which is the cue to be
    // ready to retype the number in maRejection.pine, not to retype it yet.
    //
    // The proposal is the FIRST armed pair in slot order - the tie rule fixed
    // by the verdict, deliberately nothing cleverer - and a pair that cannot
    // fire (held by flat or hours) is counted, named in the count, and never
    // proposed. An excluded pair (na, or the monotonic -1 overflow sentinel)
    // is shouted rather than silently dropped: an invisible exclusion reads as
    // "nothing on this pair", which is the opposite of what happened.
    rotArmedN = array.get(rotTally, 0)
    rotHeldN  = array.get(rotTally, 1)
    rotRunN   = array.get(rotTally, 2)
    rotExclN  = array.get(rotTally, 3)
    proposal  = array.size(rotArmedNames) > 0 ? array.get(rotArmedNames, 0) : ''
    rotDue    = activeIdx >= 0 and nextLen > 0 and nextLen != activeLen
    rotRow    = rowsPerBlock + 4
    rotWarn   = rotDue or rotExclN > 0 or activeIdx < 0
    rotBg     = rotWarn ? #5C3A00 : #0E2A1B
    // THREE LINES RATHER THAN ONE (user request, 2026-08-16), and the reason
    // is the WIDTH of a table this row does not belong to. It is merged across
    // every column of the ranking above, so its length is a FLOOR under the
    // whole table: one line of a hundred and sixty characters stretched ten
    // columns that between them need ninety, and the slack landed on the
    // narrowest of them - Links, on the right-hand end.
    //
    // Broken up, the cell is as wide as its longest LINE and simply grows
    // downwards, which is a direction a chart has more of. The three are the
    // three questions the row answers: what is being traded, what would end
    // the round, and what to enter.
    //
    // CENTRED, like every other merged line in this table (user request,
    // 2026-08-16). It was left-aligned for exactly one revision, on the
    // argument that centred lines of different lengths do not read as a
    // block - which is true of a stacked LIST and turned out not to be true
    // of three sentences: they read as a paragraph either way, and one line
    // in the stack aligned differently from the WORST WINDOW and PAIRS lines
    // above and below it was the louder problem. Nothing is spelled here -
    // table.cell centres by default.
    rotTxt    = ' ROTATION  ·  ' +
         (activeIdx < 0 ? 'nothing traded yet - only ' + str.tostring(trusted) + ' rows with ' + str.tostring(minClosures) + '+ wins' :
          'trading MA ' + str.tostring(activeLen) + ' (pick #' + str.tostring(pickPos) + ')' +
          (activeSince > 0 ? ' since ' + str.format_time(activeSince, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') : '') +
          ' · ' + str.tostring(activeSwitches) + ' switches' +
          '\n ' + str.tostring(winsSinceRot) + ' wins since, recompute at >' + str.tostring(rotDeepAt) + ' entries' +
          (rotDue ? '  ·  NEXT: MA ' + str.tostring(nextLen) : '') +
          '\n enter: ' +
          (proposal != '' ? proposal + (rotArmedN > 1 ? ' (+' + str.tostring(rotArmedN - 1) + ' more armed)' : '') :
           rotHeldN > 0 ? 'nothing armed (' + str.tostring(rotHeldN) + ' held by flat/hours)' : 'nothing armed') +
          '  ·  armed ' + str.tostring(rotArmedN) + ' · held ' + str.tostring(rotHeldN) + ' · running ' + str.tostring(rotRunN)) +
         (rotExclN > 0 ? '  ·  EXCLUDED ' + str.tostring(rotExclN) : '') + ' '
    table.cell(swTable, 0, rotRow, rotTxt, bgcolor = rotBg, text_color = rotWarn ? #FFC163 : #6EF0A5, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, rotRow, '', bgcolor = rotBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, rotRow, hdrSpan, rotRow)

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE WORST-WINDOW LINE - statistics, and nothing else acts on it
    // (user request, 2026-08-15).
    //
    // IT AVERAGES THE WORST COLUMN, down the swept lengths - the numbers the
    // ranking prints beside a pair name, 7, 12, 11, 9, 15 and so on (user
    // decision, 2026-08-15, after the first version averaged the wrong
    // population).
    //
    // What it answers: the deepest single window at ONE length says nothing
    // about whether that length was lucky. The mean of the same figure over
    // every swept length is the watchlist's own scale - "a run on this list
    // costs about eleven entries at worst, and the length being traded is
    // seven" - and it is what makes the traded row's number readable as good
    // or bad rather than merely as a number.
    //
    // WHAT IT IS NOT is the per-pair reading, which this row carried for one
    // revision: the average of every pair's own deepest window inside the
    // traded length. That number was right and answered a question nobody had
    // asked, and it could not be checked against anything on screen - its
    // twenty-five inputs are nowhere in the table. This one averages exactly
    // what is printed above it, so it can be verified by hand.
    //
    // A ROW rather than a column, for the reason a column could not carry it:
    // it is one number for the whole table, not one per length. It also buys
    // the highlight, since a Pine table cell has one text colour and part of a
    // cell cannot be coloured on its own.
    //
    // A length that closed nothing prints '-' in that column and is left OUT
    // of both the sum and the count - there is no worst window to average, and
    // counting it as a zero would report a watchlist that is calmer than it is.
    // The count is printed beside the average so a short history says so.
    //
    // It takes no part in choosing the length. When that changes it becomes an
    // option in 'Rank and sort by' and a key in computeRanking, and nothing
    // here has to move.
    statRow  = rotRow + 1
    statSum  = 0
    statN    = 0
    statMax  = 0
    statMaxK = -1
    for k = 0 to usedN - 1
        w = array.get(agW, k)
        if w > 0
            statSum += w
            statN += 1
            if w > statMax
                statMax  := w
                statMaxK := k
    statAvg = statN > 0 ? 1.0 * statSum / statN : float(na)
    // Clamped for the reason every index in this file is: Pine evaluates BOTH
    // branches of a ternary, so a -1 inside one that is never chosen still
    // reads element -1 and kills the script.
    statMaxLen = sweepFrom + math.max(statMaxK, 0) * sweepStep
    statMaxWho = array.get(agWho, math.max(statMaxK, 0))
    statTxt = statN == 0 ? ' WORST WINDOW  ·  no length has closed a window yet ' :
         ' WORST WINDOW  ·  avg ' + str.tostring(statAvg, '#.00') + ' entries across ' +
         str.tostring(statN) + ' MA lengths' +
         (statMaxK < 0 ? '' : '  ·  deepest ' + str.tostring(statMax) + ' on MA ' +
              str.tostring(statMaxLen) + (statMaxWho == '' ? '' : ' · ' + statMaxWho)) +
         (activeIdx < 0 ? '' : '  ·  traded MA ' + str.tostring(activeLen) + ': ' +
              str.tostring(array.get(agW, math.max(activeIdx, 0)))) + ' '
    statBg = statN == 0 ? #5C3A00 : #0E2A1B
    table.cell(swTable, 0, statRow, statTxt, bgcolor = statBg,
         text_color = statN == 0 ? #FFC163 : #6EF0A5, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, statRow, '', bgcolor = statBg, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, statRow, hdrSpan, statRow)

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE PAIRS TABLE - mar1Scanner.pine's open-pairs table, on the length
    // this file chose, drawn as nearly like the original as a shared table
    // grid allows: the same seven columns in the same order, the same words,
    // the same sort key, and rows wearing the colour of the last small diamond
    // on that pair rather than a scale of this table's own.
    //
    // Every pair that is not idle is on it - pre-armed, armed, held, running -
    // and idle ones are left off, which is the scanner with 'Show idle pairs'
    // unticked. Twenty-one rows at the very most, so nothing is ever
    // truncated and there is no "showing 10 of 17" to read past.
    //
    // WHERE IT DIFFERS FROM THE SCANNER, all of it stated rather than
    // discovered:
    //
    //   HELD is a state of its own here, printed where the scanner prints
    //   ARMED. The scanner has no reason to split them; this file does, since
    //   the row above proposes a pair to enter and must not offer one the
    //   flat filter or the trading hours are currently vetoing.
    //
    //   Market is one bar coarse. The scanner redraws on every tick, this
    //   table once per closed bar - see rotMkt at the tail of sweepOne.
    //
    //   Open since is rebuilt from a minutes-ago count rather than carried as
    //   a timestamp, so it can be a minute or two out when the pair's last bar
    //   is older than the chart's. See rotStamp at the same place.
    //
    // WHAT THIS CANNOT FILTER, asked for and stated plainly: the broker's
    // payout per pair. That number lives on the broker's site and Pine has no
    // way to read it - the pair tick boxes are the filter. Untick a pair whose
    // payout has dropped and it leaves this table, the proposal and the totals
    // at the same time.
    ordN = array.size(rotPairNames)
    // Two under the ROTATION row now, not one - the worst-window line sits
    // between them. Everything below follows from this, including the LAST WIN
    // line, which is placed by the caller from the count this returns.
    pairsHdrRow = rotRow + 2
    // The header names the hours the machine behind the table trades in, so
    // a screenshot carries its own context - the same reason the scanner's
    // header names its counts.
    pairsHdrTxt = ' PAIRS ON MA ' + (activeIdx < 0 ? '-' : str.tostring(activeLen)) +
         (activeIdx < 0 ? '  ·  nothing traded yet' :
          ordN == 0 ? '  ·  all idle' :
          '  ·  ' + str.tostring(rotRunN) + ' running · ' + str.tostring(rotArmedN) + ' armed · ' +
          str.tostring(rotHeldN) + ' held') +
         (activeIdx < 0 ? '' : tradeHoursOn ? '  ·  hours ' + str.tostring(tradeStart) + '-' + str.tostring(tradeEnd) + ' UTC+1' : '  ·  all hours') + ' '
    table.cell(swTable, 0, pairsHdrRow, pairsHdrTxt, bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to hdrSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, pairsHdrRow, '', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, pairsHdrRow, hdrSpan, pairsHdrRow)

    // The column headings, and they are the scanner's words rather than
    // paraphrases of them - the two tables are meant to be read the same way
    // by the same eye.
    int drawn = 0
    if ordN > 0
        colRow = pairsHdrRow + 1
        table.cell(swTable, 0, colRow, ' Pair ',          bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize)
        table.cell(swTable, 1, colRow, ' State ',         bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
             tooltip = 'RUNNING - entries taken, none has paid yet. ARMED - the gate is open, no entry yet. HELD - armed, but the flat filter or the trading hours are vetoing signals, so it cannot fire and is never proposed. pre-armed - the pair is on the white step of the colour ladder, so the next large diamond on it would come out white.\n\nOutside the trading hours every armed pair reads HELD and a running one keeps running - the hours stop entries, they do not close positions.')
        table.cell(swTable, 2, colRow, ' Market ',        bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
             tooltip = 'Whether a position was live on the last closed candle. An entry grades exactly one candle - taken at its open, settled at its close - so IN lasts one candle while RUNNING can stand for an hour across candles you were flat for.')
        table.cell(swTable, 3, colRow, ' Entries ',       bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
             tooltip = 'Entries taken since this window opened, none of which has come out right yet.')
        table.cell(swTable, 4, colRow, ' Open since ',    bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
             tooltip = 'On an open window: when the gate opened. On a pre-armed row: when the pair reached the white step of the ladder. UTC+1, and rebuilt from a minutes-ago count, so it can be a minute or two out when the pair last traded before the chart did.\n\nWindows can only OPEN inside the trading hours - the machine behind this table finds no signals outside them - so an armed or running stamp always lands inside the window, with nothing filtered. A pre-armed stamp can land at any hour, exactly as in the scanner. On a weekend the table shows the last trading day, because machine state persists on its own.')
        table.cell(swTable, 5, colRow, ' Streak / gate ', bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
             tooltip = 'Current losing streak against the threshold entries resume at.')
        table.cell(swTable, 6, colRow, ' Last ',          bgcolor = C_HEAD, text_color = C_TXT, text_size = txtSize,
             tooltip = 'The side of the last entry inside the window that is open now.')

        // Sorting an empty array is safe; looping over one is not - a Pine for
        // loop whose start exceeds its end counts DOWNWARDS, which here would
        // index off the end of the array and kill the script. ordN > 0 is
        // already guaranteed above.
        ord = array.sort_indices(rotPairKeys, order.descending)
        // The column-heading row counts too: what the caller needs is how far
        // down the table reached, not how many pairs are in it, and off by one
        // here writes the LAST WIN line straight over the last pair.
        drawn := ordN + 1
        for i = 0 to ordN - 1
            k  = array.get(ord, i)
            d  = array.get(rotPairDigs, k)
            ld = array.get(rotPairSteps, k)
            sd = array.get(rotPairSides, k)
            st = array.get(rotPairStamp, k)
            rP = colRow + 1 + i
            pre = d == 1

            // The pre-armed row is deliberately the faintest thing here: a
            // wash rather than a fill, and white because white is already what
            // the ladder calls "one step short of the gate" - the large
            // diamond this row is reporting is literally a white diamond.
            // Everything else wears the step of the last small diamond on that
            // pair, so a purple row and a purple diamond are the same fact.
            rowBg = pre ? color.new(color.white, 88) : ld > 0 ? stepColor(ld, 55) : color.new(#333333, 55)
            rowFg = pre ? color.new(color.white, 30) : ld > 0 ? stepText(ld) : color.white

            // Lower case, like the scanner's idle and unlike RUNNING and
            // ARMED, and for the reason those two are shouted: they are states
            // you can act on. HELD is shouted too - it is a state you would act
            // on if the filter let you, which is worth seeing at a glance.
            stTxt = d == 3 ? 'RUNNING' : d == 2 ? 'ARMED' : d == 4 ? 'HELD' : 'pre-armed'

            // The direction keeps its own colour, but it has to survive a light
            // row: lime on lime and red on white are both unreadable. Same hue,
            // darkened, so a long is still green and a short still red wherever
            // the ladder has put the row. Step 6 and 8 are the alarm steps,
            // where the row already carries the warning and a red arrow on red
            // would vanish.
            light  = ld == 1 or pre
            hot    = ld == 6 or ld == 8
            sideFg = sd == 1 ? (light ? color.new(#006622, 0) : hot ? #B9F6CA : color.lime) :
                 sd == 2 ? (light ? color.new(#991111, 0) : hot ? #FFFFFF : color.red) :
                 (light ? color.new(#444444, 0) : color.silver)

            table.cell(swTable, 0, rP, ' ' + array.get(rotPairNames, k) + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(swTable, 1, rP, ' ' + stTxt + ' ', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(swTable, 2, rP, array.get(rotPairMkts, k) == 1 ? ' ● IN ' : pre ? '' : ' wait ',
                 bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
            // The scanner's three answers, kept apart: a figure on a RUNNING
            // row, a dash on one that is armed or held - there is a window and
            // nothing has been spent in it yet - and blank on a pre-armed row,
            // where there is no window to spend anything in.
            table.cell(swTable, 3, rP, d == 3 ? str.tostring(array.get(rotPairOpns, k)) : pre ? '' : '-',
                 bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
            // Two different answers sharing one column, because they are the
            // same question asked of the two states a row can be in: since
            // when has this pair been asking for attention. A pre-armed row can
            // never carry an open time and a window can never carry a white
            // time, so the two can never collide in one cell. Zero means the
            // machine has no stamp at all, which is what an armed row looks
            // like when the gate opened before the loaded history did.
            table.cell(swTable, 4, rP, st > 0 ? str.format_time(time - (st - 1) * 60000, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') : '',
                 bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(swTable, 5, rP, str.tostring(array.get(rotPairStks, k)) + ' / ' + str.tostring(array.get(rotPairThrs, k)),
                 bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = rowFg, text_size = txtSize)
            table.cell(swTable, 6, rP, sd == 1 ? '▲ long' : sd == 2 ? '▼ short' : '', bgcolor = rowBg, text_color = sideFg, text_size = txtSize)
    drawn

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHEN THE TABLE IS REBUILT - once per closed bar, not once per tick
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This was `barstate.islast`, which is true on EVERY realtime tick of the last
// bar, so everything below - clearing eight aggregate arrays, twenty-one
// addPair calls of twenty accum each, two passes over the lengths, a sort, a
// table clear and some four hundred cell writes - ran several times a second.
//
// And produced the identical table every time. Every counter sweepOne keeps
// moves only inside `if barstate.isconfirmed and active`, so the packed number
// each pair-and-length hands back cannot change between one bar's close and the
// next. Redrawing on a tick was recomputing a number that had not moved.
//
// TWO STATES, and both are needed. islastconfirmedhistory fires exactly once,
// on the last closed bar of history, which is what puts the table on screen the
// moment the chart finishes loading rather than making it wait for the first
// candle to close. After that every confirmed bar redraws it.
//
// What this costs, stated: the bar count in the footer is the count at the last
// CLOSE, so during a forming candle it reads one bar short. It catches up at
// the close, and a calibration table that is one bar behind on its own history
// length is not a table anybody is reading wrong.
redrawNow = barstate.islastconfirmedhistory or (barstate.islast and barstate.isconfirmed)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The totals, into the flat array
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A block of its own, and it has to be one: everything below reads packAll -
// the round boundary and the redraw both compute the ranking out of it - and
// both of those sit inside if statements this file has already had to split
// once for size. See the note at packAll for why the array exists at all.
//
// Confirmed bars only, which is every bar either reader can run on: the
// rotation block below is guarded the same way, and both redraw states -
// islastconfirmedhistory, and islast with isconfirmed - are confirmed bars.
//
// GENERATED - twenty-one stashPair calls. Regenerate, never hand-edit.
if barstate.isconfirmed
    stashPair(0, v01_1, v01_2, v01_3, v01_4, v01_5, v01_6, v01_7, v01_8, v01_9, v01_10, v01_11, v01_12, v01_13, v01_14, v01_15)
    stashPair(1, v02_1, v02_2, v02_3, v02_4, v02_5, v02_6, v02_7, v02_8, v02_9, v02_10, v02_11, v02_12, v02_13, v02_14, v02_15)
    stashPair(2, v03_1, v03_2, v03_3, v03_4, v03_5, v03_6, v03_7, v03_8, v03_9, v03_10, v03_11, v03_12, v03_13, v03_14, v03_15)
    stashPair(3, v04_1, v04_2, v04_3, v04_4, v04_5, v04_6, v04_7, v04_8, v04_9, v04_10, v04_11, v04_12, v04_13, v04_14, v04_15)
    stashPair(4, v05_1, v05_2, v05_3, v05_4, v05_5, v05_6, v05_7, v05_8, v05_9, v05_10, v05_11, v05_12, v05_13, v05_14, v05_15)
    stashPair(5, v06_1, v06_2, v06_3, v06_4, v06_5, v06_6, v06_7, v06_8, v06_9, v06_10, v06_11, v06_12, v06_13, v06_14, v06_15)
    stashPair(6, v07_1, v07_2, v07_3, v07_4, v07_5, v07_6, v07_7, v07_8, v07_9, v07_10, v07_11, v07_12, v07_13, v07_14, v07_15)
    stashPair(7, v08_1, v08_2, v08_3, v08_4, v08_5, v08_6, v08_7, v08_8, v08_9, v08_10, v08_11, v08_12, v08_13, v08_14, v08_15)
    stashPair(8, v09_1, v09_2, v09_3, v09_4, v09_5, v09_6, v09_7, v09_8, v09_9, v09_10, v09_11, v09_12, v09_13, v09_14, v09_15)
    stashPair(9, v10_1, v10_2, v10_3, v10_4, v10_5, v10_6, v10_7, v10_8, v10_9, v10_10, v10_11, v10_12, v10_13, v10_14, v10_15)
    stashPair(10, v11_1, v11_2, v11_3, v11_4, v11_5, v11_6, v11_7, v11_8, v11_9, v11_10, v11_11, v11_12, v11_13, v11_14, v11_15)
    stashPair(11, v12_1, v12_2, v12_3, v12_4, v12_5, v12_6, v12_7, v12_8, v12_9, v12_10, v12_11, v12_12, v12_13, v12_14, v12_15)
    stashPair(12, v13_1, v13_2, v13_3, v13_4, v13_5, v13_6, v13_7, v13_8, v13_9, v13_10, v13_11, v13_12, v13_13, v13_14, v13_15)
    stashPair(13, v14_1, v14_2, v14_3, v14_4, v14_5, v14_6, v14_7, v14_8, v14_9, v14_10, v14_11, v14_12, v14_13, v14_14, v14_15)
    stashPair(14, v15_1, v15_2, v15_3, v15_4, v15_5, v15_6, v15_7, v15_8, v15_9, v15_10, v15_11, v15_12, v15_13, v15_14, v15_15)
    stashPair(15, v16_1, v16_2, v16_3, v16_4, v16_5, v16_6, v16_7, v16_8, v16_9, v16_10, v16_11, v16_12, v16_13, v16_14, v16_15)
    stashPair(16, v17_1, v17_2, v17_3, v17_4, v17_5, v17_6, v17_7, v17_8, v17_9, v17_10, v17_11, v17_12, v17_13, v17_14, v17_15)
    stashPair(17, v18_1, v18_2, v18_3, v18_4, v18_5, v18_6, v18_7, v18_8, v18_9, v18_10, v18_11, v18_12, v18_13, v18_14, v18_15)
    stashPair(18, v19_1, v19_2, v19_3, v19_4, v19_5, v19_6, v19_7, v19_8, v19_9, v19_10, v19_11, v19_12, v19_13, v19_14, v19_15)
    stashPair(19, v20_1, v20_2, v20_3, v20_4, v20_5, v20_6, v20_7, v20_8, v20_9, v20_10, v20_11, v20_12, v20_13, v20_14, v20_15)
    stashPair(20, v21_1, v21_2, v21_3, v21_4, v21_5, v21_6, v21_7, v21_8, v21_9, v21_10, v21_11, v21_12, v21_13, v21_14, v21_15)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ROTATION - the only part of this file that runs on every bar
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Everything else here is written on the last bar. This is not, and it cannot
// be: the traded length is the ranking's answer at the last ROUND BOUNDARY,
// rounds end scattered through the history, and a boundary can only be
// noticed on the bar it happens on. Skip the history and the length on screen
// would be the answer to "what is best over everything loaded" - which is the
// question the ranking table already answers, and not the one this file was
// rebuilt to ask.
//
// What runs per bar is deliberately small: twenty-one rotWatch calls, one
// three-digit unpack each, and a comparison. The ranking pass itself runs
// only on a boundary.
//
// THE COLD START. Before any length has closed 'Ignore lengths with fewer
// wins than' windows there is no trusted row, so there is no pick, so nothing
// is traded and the pairs table is empty - the honest state, and the one the
// design asked for. Nothing is being watched then either, so there is no
// event to end a round on, and the ranking is instead probed every SEED_PROBE
// bars until it produces its first pick. A hundred bars is fine granularity
// for something that takes thousands of bars to happen, and it costs a
// two-hundredth of what probing every bar would.
SEED_PROBE = 100

if barstate.isconfirmed
    array.set(rotEvt, 0, 0)
    array.clear(rotWinNames)
    array.clear(rotWinEnts)
    array.clear(rotWinCls)
    array.clear(rotWinStart)
    array.clear(rotWinDeep)

    // THE DAY OPENS. The DAY CLOSES table is a record of ONE trading day, so
    // it starts over here, and jrnDayAt is stamped with the moment it did -
    // that stamp is what the table's title prints when no day has been picked
    // from the calendar.
    if dayOpens and jrnDayNow
        clearCloses()
        jrnDayAt := time

    // GENERATED - twenty-one rotWatch calls, each handing over the pair's
    // nine packed trading-machine floats - three of closure counters, three of
    // window ages and three of threshold-crossing ages - the traded length's
    // three digits of each are picked INSIDE rotWatch. Regenerate, never
    // hand-edit.
    rotWatch(0, use01, sym01, v01_31, v01_32, v01_33, v01_34, v01_35, v01_36, v01_37, v01_38, v01_39)
    rotWatch(1, use02, sym02, v02_31, v02_32, v02_33, v02_34, v02_35, v02_36, v02_37, v02_38, v02_39)
    rotWatch(2, use03, sym03, v03_31, v03_32, v03_33, v03_34, v03_35, v03_36, v03_37, v03_38, v03_39)
    rotWatch(3, use04, sym04, v04_31, v04_32, v04_33, v04_34, v04_35, v04_36, v04_37, v04_38, v04_39)
    rotWatch(4, use05, sym05, v05_31, v05_32, v05_33, v05_34, v05_35, v05_36, v05_37, v05_38, v05_39)
    rotWatch(5, use06, sym06, v06_31, v06_32, v06_33, v06_34, v06_35, v06_36, v06_37, v06_38, v06_39)
    rotWatch(6, use07, sym07, v07_31, v07_32, v07_33, v07_34, v07_35, v07_36, v07_37, v07_38, v07_39)
    rotWatch(7, use08, sym08, v08_31, v08_32, v08_33, v08_34, v08_35, v08_36, v08_37, v08_38, v08_39)
    rotWatch(8, use09, sym09, v09_31, v09_32, v09_33, v09_34, v09_35, v09_36, v09_37, v09_38, v09_39)
    rotWatch(9, use10, sym10, v10_31, v10_32, v10_33, v10_34, v10_35, v10_36, v10_37, v10_38, v10_39)
    rotWatch(10, use11, sym11, v11_31, v11_32, v11_33, v11_34, v11_35, v11_36, v11_37, v11_38, v11_39)
    rotWatch(11, use12, sym12, v12_31, v12_32, v12_33, v12_34, v12_35, v12_36, v12_37, v12_38, v12_39)
    rotWatch(12, use13, sym13, v13_31, v13_32, v13_33, v13_34, v13_35, v13_36, v13_37, v13_38, v13_39)
    rotWatch(13, use14, sym14, v14_31, v14_32, v14_33, v14_34, v14_35, v14_36, v14_37, v14_38, v14_39)
    rotWatch(14, use15, sym15, v15_31, v15_32, v15_33, v15_34, v15_35, v15_36, v15_37, v15_38, v15_39)
    rotWatch(15, use16, sym16, v16_31, v16_32, v16_33, v16_34, v16_35, v16_36, v16_37, v16_38, v16_39)
    rotWatch(16, use17, sym17, v17_31, v17_32, v17_33, v17_34, v17_35, v17_36, v17_37, v17_38, v17_39)
    rotWatch(17, use18, sym18, v18_31, v18_32, v18_33, v18_34, v18_35, v18_36, v18_37, v18_38, v18_39)
    rotWatch(18, use19, sym19, v19_31, v19_32, v19_33, v19_34, v19_35, v19_36, v19_37, v19_38, v19_39)
    rotWatch(19, use20, sym20, v20_31, v20_32, v20_33, v20_34, v20_35, v20_36, v20_37, v20_38, v20_39)
    rotWatch(20, use21, sym21, v21_31, v21_32, v21_33, v21_34, v21_35, v21_36, v21_37, v21_38, v21_39)

    winsSinceRot += array.size(rotWinNames)
    if array.size(rotWinNames) > 0
        lastWinLen := activeLen
        // THE RECORD, gated on the day alone: the first closes of a chart can
        // land before the first trading window opens, and they are still
        // closes of that day. activeLen is read HERE, ABOVE the recompute
        // block, so a record names the length its window actually closed on
        // and not the one the rotation moved to a line later - which is what
        // makes the MA column change on the line AFTER the ► rather than on
        // the ► itself.
        // time_close, not time (user request, 2026-08-16): the scanner's
        // Closed column prints the winning candle's CLOSING time, and this
        // stamp has to be the same minute - the bar's opening time read one
        // minute earlier on every record.
        if jrnDayNow
            for y = 0 to array.size(rotWinNames) - 1
                pushClose(array.get(rotWinStart, y), array.get(rotWinDeep, y), time_close,
                     array.get(rotWinNames, y), array.get(rotWinEnts, y),
                     array.get(rotWinCls, y), activeLen)

    // The one boundary condition, set by rotWatch above on any watched pair -
    // and only for a close the trading window let through. See the Rotation
    // group for the threshold and for what used to stand beside it.
    roundOver = activeIdx >= 0 and array.get(rotEvt, 0) == 1

    if roundOver or (activeIdx < 0 and bar_index % SEED_PROBE == 0)
        // AS OF THIS BAR. The totals the ranking is built from are the totals
        // the machines had closed by now, so a length is judged on what it had
        // actually done at the moment it was chosen - no lookahead anywhere in
        // this, which is the whole reason the pass had to become callable.
        computeRanking()
        newIdx = pickSlot()
        winsSinceRot := 0
        if newIdx >= 0 and newIdx != activeIdx
            if activeIdx >= 0
                activeSwitches += 1
            activeIdx   := newIdx
            activeLen   := sweepFrom + newIdx * sweepStep
            activeSince := time
            // The baselines belong to the length that just stopped being
            // traded. Dropped rather than translated: one bar of blindness
            // costs at most one win going uncounted, and carrying a counter
            // across two different machines would invent one.
            for s = 0 to 20
                array.set(baseCls, s, -1)
                array.set(baseEnt, s, -1)

        // A RECOMPUTE THAT KEPT THE SAME LENGTH LEAVES NO TRACE, and that is
        // what went with the journal (2026-08-16): it kept one row per
        // recompute, so a round that re-read the ranking and changed its mind
        // about nothing was still visible. Nothing records that now. What the
        // DAY CLOSES table shows instead is the WINDOW that ended the round -
        // the ► - and the length change if there was one, which is the half
        // worth acting on.

if redrawNow
    // The ranking again, now for the DRAWING - and a bar where a round just
    // ended computes it twice, which is a few hundred operations on a bar the
    // chart is redrawing anyway. The alternative is threading a "did it run"
    // flag between two top-level blocks, and a flag that is wrong once is a
    // table that describes a ranking nobody computed.
    computeRanking()

    array.clear(rotArmedNames)
    array.clear(rotPairNames)
    array.clear(rotPairDigs)
    array.clear(rotPairStks)
    array.clear(rotPairThrs)
    array.clear(rotPairOpns)
    array.clear(rotPairSteps)
    array.clear(rotPairMkts)
    array.clear(rotPairSides)
    array.clear(rotPairStamp)
    array.clear(rotPairKeys)
    for k = 0 to 3
        array.set(rotTally, k, 0)

    // GENERATED - twenty-one rotOne calls, each picking the traded length's
    // LIVE state out of that pair's fifteen. Regenerate, never hand-edit.
    rotOne(use01, sym01, 0, pickN(activeIdx, v01_16, v01_17, v01_18, v01_19, v01_20, v01_21, v01_22, v01_23, v01_24, v01_25, v01_26, v01_27, v01_28, v01_29, v01_30))
    rotOne(use02, sym02, 1, pickN(activeIdx, v02_16, v02_17, v02_18, v02_19, v02_20, v02_21, v02_22, v02_23, v02_24, v02_25, v02_26, v02_27, v02_28, v02_29, v02_30))
    rotOne(use03, sym03, 2, pickN(activeIdx, v03_16, v03_17, v03_18, v03_19, v03_20, v03_21, v03_22, v03_23, v03_24, v03_25, v03_26, v03_27, v03_28, v03_29, v03_30))
    rotOne(use04, sym04, 3, pickN(activeIdx, v04_16, v04_17, v04_18, v04_19, v04_20, v04_21, v04_22, v04_23, v04_24, v04_25, v04_26, v04_27, v04_28, v04_29, v04_30))
    rotOne(use05, sym05, 4, pickN(activeIdx, v05_16, v05_17, v05_18, v05_19, v05_20, v05_21, v05_22, v05_23, v05_24, v05_25, v05_26, v05_27, v05_28, v05_29, v05_30))
    rotOne(use06, sym06, 5, pickN(activeIdx, v06_16, v06_17, v06_18, v06_19, v06_20, v06_21, v06_22, v06_23, v06_24, v06_25, v06_26, v06_27, v06_28, v06_29, v06_30))
    rotOne(use07, sym07, 6, pickN(activeIdx, v07_16, v07_17, v07_18, v07_19, v07_20, v07_21, v07_22, v07_23, v07_24, v07_25, v07_26, v07_27, v07_28, v07_29, v07_30))
    rotOne(use08, sym08, 7, pickN(activeIdx, v08_16, v08_17, v08_18, v08_19, v08_20, v08_21, v08_22, v08_23, v08_24, v08_25, v08_26, v08_27, v08_28, v08_29, v08_30))
    rotOne(use09, sym09, 8, pickN(activeIdx, v09_16, v09_17, v09_18, v09_19, v09_20, v09_21, v09_22, v09_23, v09_24, v09_25, v09_26, v09_27, v09_28, v09_29, v09_30))
    rotOne(use10, sym10, 9, pickN(activeIdx, v10_16, v10_17, v10_18, v10_19, v10_20, v10_21, v10_22, v10_23, v10_24, v10_25, v10_26, v10_27, v10_28, v10_29, v10_30))
    rotOne(use11, sym11, 10, pickN(activeIdx, v11_16, v11_17, v11_18, v11_19, v11_20, v11_21, v11_22, v11_23, v11_24, v11_25, v11_26, v11_27, v11_28, v11_29, v11_30))
    rotOne(use12, sym12, 11, pickN(activeIdx, v12_16, v12_17, v12_18, v12_19, v12_20, v12_21, v12_22, v12_23, v12_24, v12_25, v12_26, v12_27, v12_28, v12_29, v12_30))
    rotOne(use13, sym13, 12, pickN(activeIdx, v13_16, v13_17, v13_18, v13_19, v13_20, v13_21, v13_22, v13_23, v13_24, v13_25, v13_26, v13_27, v13_28, v13_29, v13_30))
    rotOne(use14, sym14, 13, pickN(activeIdx, v14_16, v14_17, v14_18, v14_19, v14_20, v14_21, v14_22, v14_23, v14_24, v14_25, v14_26, v14_27, v14_28, v14_29, v14_30))
    rotOne(use15, sym15, 14, pickN(activeIdx, v15_16, v15_17, v15_18, v15_19, v15_20, v15_21, v15_22, v15_23, v15_24, v15_25, v15_26, v15_27, v15_28, v15_29, v15_30))
    rotOne(use16, sym16, 15, pickN(activeIdx, v16_16, v16_17, v16_18, v16_19, v16_20, v16_21, v16_22, v16_23, v16_24, v16_25, v16_26, v16_27, v16_28, v16_29, v16_30))
    rotOne(use17, sym17, 16, pickN(activeIdx, v17_16, v17_17, v17_18, v17_19, v17_20, v17_21, v17_22, v17_23, v17_24, v17_25, v17_26, v17_27, v17_28, v17_29, v17_30))
    rotOne(use18, sym18, 17, pickN(activeIdx, v18_16, v18_17, v18_18, v18_19, v18_20, v18_21, v18_22, v18_23, v18_24, v18_25, v18_26, v18_27, v18_28, v18_29, v18_30))
    rotOne(use19, sym19, 18, pickN(activeIdx, v19_16, v19_17, v19_18, v19_19, v19_20, v19_21, v19_22, v19_23, v19_24, v19_25, v19_26, v19_27, v19_28, v19_29, v19_30))
    rotOne(use20, sym20, 19, pickN(activeIdx, v20_16, v20_17, v20_18, v20_19, v20_20, v20_21, v20_22, v20_23, v20_24, v20_25, v20_26, v20_27, v20_28, v20_29, v20_30))
    rotOne(use21, sym21, 20, pickN(activeIdx, v21_16, v21_17, v21_18, v21_19, v21_20, v21_21, v21_22, v21_23, v21_24, v21_25, v21_26, v21_27, v21_28, v21_29, v21_30))

    scanned    = array.get(tally, 0)
    unresolved = array.get(tally, 1)
    trusted    = array.get(rankI, 0)

    // What the ranking would pick if a round ended on this bar. It is not what
    // is being traded and must never be drawn as though it were - it is the
    // one number that says a rotation is PENDING, which is worth knowing when
    // the length has to be copied into maRejection.pine by hand.
    nextSlot = pickSlot()
    nextLen  = nextSlot >= 0 ? sweepFrom + nextSlot * sweepStep : -1

    table.clear(swTable, 0, 0, MAX_BLOCKS * 11 - 2, 79)

    // Cleared whether or not it is drawn - a table left with yesterday's cells
    // in it while the switch is ticked would be the one thing on screen nobody
    // could explain.
    table.clear(clsTable, 0, 0, CLS_BLOCKS * 7 - 2, 46)
    if not hideCloses
        drawCloses()

    // The drawing, handed to the functions declared above - moved there
    // because a single if statement has a compiled-size ceiling (CE10205),
    // which this block hit as the cockpit grew.
    if not hideSweep
        drawRanking(rankOrder, scanned, unresolved, trusted, array.get(rankI, 1), array.get(rankI, 2),
             array.get(rankF, 0), array.get(rankI, 3), array.get(rankI, 4), array.get(rankI, 5),
             array.get(rankI, 6), array.get(rankF, 1), array.get(rankF, 2), array.get(rankF, 3), array.get(rankI, 7))
    else
        drawPickRow(trusted)
    pairsDrawn = drawCockpit(trusted, nextLen)

    // THE LAST WIN LINE - the alert's message, parked on the table instead of
    // sent. Updated on the same confirmed bar the win was counted on, then
    // held by the var until the next win overwrites it. It stays HERE rather
    // than in drawCockpit because it WRITES the lastWinTxt global, and Pine
    // forbids a function assigning a global.
    //
    // The row it lands on follows the pairs table, which is as tall as the
    // watchlist has non-idle pairs - so the count comes back from drawCockpit
    // rather than being recomputed here from a formula that would have to be
    // kept in step with it.
    lwBlocks = math.min(math.max(int(math.ceil(usedN / math.max(rowsPerBlock, 1))), 1), MAX_BLOCKS)
    lwSpan   = lwBlocks * 11 - 2
    // + 7 rather than + 6 since the worst-window line was added between the
    // ROTATION row and the pairs header. pairsDrawn covers everything below
    // that; this constant covers everything above it.
    lastRow  = rowsPerBlock + 7 + pairsDrawn
    if array.size(rotWinNames) > 0
        winJoin = array.get(rotWinNames, 0)
        if array.size(rotWinNames) > 1
            for x = 1 to array.size(rotWinNames) - 1
                winJoin := winJoin + ', ' + array.get(rotWinNames, x)
        lastWinTxt := winJoin + ' on MA ' + str.tostring(lastWinLen) +
             ', detected ' + str.format_time(time, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1')
    table.cell(swTable, 0, lastRow, ' LAST WIN:  ' + lastWinTxt + ' ',
         bgcolor = C_FOOT, text_color = lastWinTxt == '-' ? C_TXT_NO : #6EF0A5, text_size = txtSize)
    for k = 1 to lwSpan
        table.cell(swTable, k, lastRow, '', bgcolor = C_FOOT, text_size = txtSize)
    table.merge_cells(swTable, 0, lastRow, lwSpan, lastRow)

    // THE WIN ALERT - dynamic alert(), because alertcondition() carries a
    // CONST string and could not name the pair; the price is the one-time
    // setup in the header (Create Alert -> 'Any alert() function call').
    // freq_all rather than once_per_bar: two pairs can close wins on the same
    // bar and each deserves its own message. Confirmed realtime bars only - on
    // the historical pass TradingView ignores alert() calls, so history cannot
    // spam. The time in the text is the time the win was DETECTED on this
    // chart; on a pair bar the chart has no bar of its own for, that is one bar
    // later than the pair's close - detection time, and labelled as nothing
    // grander.
    //
    // THE ALERT THIS FILE STILL OWES, and it is deliberately not here yet
    // (user decision, 2026-08-15): the length itself changing. That is now an
    // event - a round ends, the ranking is re-read, activeLen moves - and it
    // is the ONE event that needs a human, because the new number has to be
    // typed into maRejection.pine before the next entry is taken on it. It is
    // cheap to add when it is wanted: activeSwitches increments on exactly
    // that bar, so an alert() guarded on its delta, on realtime confirmed bars
    // only, is the whole of it. What it is waiting for is living with the
    // rotation long enough to know whether every switch deserves a message or
    // only the ones that move the length by more than a step.
    if barstate.islast and barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(rotWinNames) > 0
        rotNextTxt = activeIdx < 0 ? ' No traded length yet (' + str.tostring(trusted) + ' trusted rows).' :
             nextLen > 0 and nextLen != activeLen ? ' Ranking now leans MA ' + str.tostring(nextLen) + '.' : ''
        for x = 0 to array.size(rotWinNames) - 1
            alert('MAR1 ROTATION ' + timeframe.period + ': ' + array.get(rotWinNames, x) +
                 ' closed a WIN on MA ' + str.tostring(lastWinLen) +
                 ', detected ' + str.format_time(time, 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'UTC+1') + '.' + rotNextTxt, alert.freq_all)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE STATUS LINE - the one channel out of Pine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pine runs on TradingView's servers with no disk and no network, and the only
// thing that leaves it is the numbers it plots into the status line. That is
// DOM, and the browser extension in po-payout-filter reads it: it merges the
// states published here with Pocket Option's payouts and draws the OPEN &
// TRADEABLE line at the foot of the chart.
//
// EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS mar1Scanner.pine's FORMAT, deliberately and to the
// digit, so the reader needed no new parser for the states - three packed
// numbers of ten digits, then the pair-list checksum, then the traded length.
// What it did need is a way to tell three publishers apart in one legend, and
// that is the leading digit:
//
//   1234512345    mar1Scanner.pine       - ten bare digits
//   71234512345   mar1GatesScanner.pine  - the same ten behind a 7
//   81234512345   THIS FILE              - the same ten behind an 8
//
// When more than one of them is on a chart, or on a tab, the reader prefers
// this one (user decision, 2026-08-15). That is what makes the rotation the
// authority: the length it trades is the length it publishes states for, and
// a scanner sitting on some other length would be describing a machine nobody
// is trading.
//
// THE DIGITS ARE THE SECOND MACHINE'S - the hours-gated one the PAIRS table is
// drawn from, on the TRADED length. So the line at the foot of the chart names
// exactly the pairs that table names, which is the whole point of publishing
// from here rather than from a scanner nobody re-tunes.
//
// The alphabet is the scanner's, not this file's, because the reader's is:
//
//   1  not in the scan - unticked slot, blank symbol, or slot 22-30, which
//      this file does not have (it carries 21 against the scanner's 30)
//   2  idle
//   3  armed - and HELD is published as armed too. The reader has no third
//      answer, and the scanner has no HELD state at all: a pair whose gate is
//      open but whose clock is outside the trading hours reads ARMED there,
//      so it reads 3 here. The distinction is kept where it can be acted on -
//      the PAIRS table and the ROTATION row's proposal, which never offer one
//   4  running
//   5  no data
//   6  pre-armed
//
// BEFORE THE FIRST PICK EVERY LIVE SLOT IS 5, and that is honest rather than
// convenient: with no traded length there is no machine to report, pickN hands
// back na, and na is what 5 means. The line then says nothing is open, which
// is true. It cannot be 1 - that is 'not in the scan', and the reader shouts
// about a board of thirty ones because on a scanner it means the wrong
// timeframe.
LIST_ALPHABET = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789'

// The checksum of the pair list, byte for byte the scanner's. It exists
// because a Pine plot title is a constant string and cannot be built from an
// input, so the reader keeps its own pasted copy of the names and this is what
// catches that copy going stale. Sensitive to composition AND order, since a
// reordering the reader cannot see would rename every slot on the line.
//
// The scanner sums thirty slots and this file has twenty-one. The two land on
// the same number for the same watchlist: an absent slot scores zero, exactly
// like the blank slots the scanner carries past its twenty-fifth pair.
symScore(bool on, string raw, int slotIndex) =>
    total = 0
    if on and raw != ''
        name = shortName(raw)
        for j = 0 to str.length(name) - 1
            total += nz(str.pos(LIST_ALPHABET, str.substring(name, j, j + 1)))
    total * slotIndex

var int pubList = na
if na(pubList)
    rawSum = symScore(use01, sym01, 1) + symScore(use02, sym02, 2) + symScore(use03, sym03, 3) +
         symScore(use04, sym04, 4) + symScore(use05, sym05, 5) + symScore(use06, sym06, 6) +
         symScore(use07, sym07, 7) + symScore(use08, sym08, 8) + symScore(use09, sym09, 9) +
         symScore(use10, sym10, 10) + symScore(use11, sym11, 11) + symScore(use12, sym12, 12) +
         symScore(use13, sym13, 13) + symScore(use14, sym14, 14) + symScore(use15, sym15, 15) +
         symScore(use16, sym16, 16) + symScore(use17, sym17, 17) + symScore(use18, sym18, 18) +
         symScore(use19, sym19, 19) + symScore(use20, sym20, 20) + symScore(use21, sym21, 21)
    // 99991 is the largest prime under 100000, so the sum always fits in five
    // digits and cannot be mistaken for a packed state.
    pubList := rawSum % 99991

// One slot's live value, as the reader's alphabet. The order of the tests is
// the order the answers outrank each other: a slot nobody asked for first, then
// a slot that answered nothing, then the states.
pubDigit(bool on, string raw, float v) =>
    not (on and raw != '') ? 1 :
     na(v) or v < 0 ? 5 :
     rvDig(v) == 0 ? 2 :
     rvDig(v) == 1 ? 6 :
     rvDig(v) == 3 ? 4 : 3

// Slot 1 is the most significant digit, so the number reads left to right in
// the same order the settings dialog lists the pairs.
pubPack(int d1, int d2, int d3, int d4, int d5, int d6, int d7, int d8, int d9, int d10) =>
    d1 * 1000000000 + d2 * 100000000 + d3 * 10000000 + d4 * 1000000 + d5 * 100000 +
     d6 * 10000 + d7 * 1000 + d8 * 100 + d9 * 10 + d10

// GENERATED - twenty-one digits, each picking the traded length's LIVE state
// out of that pair's fifteen. Regenerate, never hand-edit.
pd01 = pubDigit(use01, sym01, pickN(activeIdx, v01_16, v01_17, v01_18, v01_19, v01_20, v01_21, v01_22, v01_23, v01_24, v01_25, v01_26, v01_27, v01_28, v01_29, v01_30))
pd02 = pubDigit(use02, sym02, pickN(activeIdx, v02_16, v02_17, v02_18, v02_19, v02_20, v02_21, v02_22, v02_23, v02_24, v02_25, v02_26, v02_27, v02_28, v02_29, v02_30))
pd03 = pubDigit(use03, sym03, pickN(activeIdx, v03_16, v03_17, v03_18, v03_19, v03_20, v03_21, v03_22, v03_23, v03_24, v03_25, v03_26, v03_27, v03_28, v03_29, v03_30))
pd04 = pubDigit(use04, sym04, pickN(activeIdx, v04_16, v04_17, v04_18, v04_19, v04_20, v04_21, v04_22, v04_23, v04_24, v04_25, v04_26, v04_27, v04_28, v04_29, v04_30))
pd05 = pubDigit(use05, sym05, pickN(activeIdx, v05_16, v05_17, v05_18, v05_19, v05_20, v05_21, v05_22, v05_23, v05_24, v05_25, v05_26, v05_27, v05_28, v05_29, v05_30))
pd06 = pubDigit(use06, sym06, pickN(activeIdx, v06_16, v06_17, v06_18, v06_19, v06_20, v06_21, v06_22, v06_23, v06_24, v06_25, v06_26, v06_27, v06_28, v06_29, v06_30))
pd07 = pubDigit(use07, sym07, pickN(activeIdx, v07_16, v07_17, v07_18, v07_19, v07_20, v07_21, v07_22, v07_23, v07_24, v07_25, v07_26, v07_27, v07_28, v07_29, v07_30))
pd08 = pubDigit(use08, sym08, pickN(activeIdx, v08_16, v08_17, v08_18, v08_19, v08_20, v08_21, v08_22, v08_23, v08_24, v08_25, v08_26, v08_27, v08_28, v08_29, v08_30))
pd09 = pubDigit(use09, sym09, pickN(activeIdx, v09_16, v09_17, v09_18, v09_19, v09_20, v09_21, v09_22, v09_23, v09_24, v09_25, v09_26, v09_27, v09_28, v09_29, v09_30))
pd10 = pubDigit(use10, sym10, pickN(activeIdx, v10_16, v10_17, v10_18, v10_19, v10_20, v10_21, v10_22, v10_23, v10_24, v10_25, v10_26, v10_27, v10_28, v10_29, v10_30))
pd11 = pubDigit(use11, sym11, pickN(activeIdx, v11_16, v11_17, v11_18, v11_19, v11_20, v11_21, v11_22, v11_23, v11_24, v11_25, v11_26, v11_27, v11_28, v11_29, v11_30))
pd12 = pubDigit(use12, sym12, pickN(activeIdx, v12_16, v12_17, v12_18, v12_19, v12_20, v12_21, v12_22, v12_23, v12_24, v12_25, v12_26, v12_27, v12_28, v12_29, v12_30))
pd13 = pubDigit(use13, sym13, pickN(activeIdx, v13_16, v13_17, v13_18, v13_19, v13_20, v13_21, v13_22, v13_23, v13_24, v13_25, v13_26, v13_27, v13_28, v13_29, v13_30))
pd14 = pubDigit(use14, sym14, pickN(activeIdx, v14_16, v14_17, v14_18, v14_19, v14_20, v14_21, v14_22, v14_23, v14_24, v14_25, v14_26, v14_27, v14_28, v14_29, v14_30))
pd15 = pubDigit(use15, sym15, pickN(activeIdx, v15_16, v15_17, v15_18, v15_19, v15_20, v15_21, v15_22, v15_23, v15_24, v15_25, v15_26, v15_27, v15_28, v15_29, v15_30))
pd16 = pubDigit(use16, sym16, pickN(activeIdx, v16_16, v16_17, v16_18, v16_19, v16_20, v16_21, v16_22, v16_23, v16_24, v16_25, v16_26, v16_27, v16_28, v16_29, v16_30))
pd17 = pubDigit(use17, sym17, pickN(activeIdx, v17_16, v17_17, v17_18, v17_19, v17_20, v17_21, v17_22, v17_23, v17_24, v17_25, v17_26, v17_27, v17_28, v17_29, v17_30))
pd18 = pubDigit(use18, sym18, pickN(activeIdx, v18_16, v18_17, v18_18, v18_19, v18_20, v18_21, v18_22, v18_23, v18_24, v18_25, v18_26, v18_27, v18_28, v18_29, v18_30))
pd19 = pubDigit(use19, sym19, pickN(activeIdx, v19_16, v19_17, v19_18, v19_19, v19_20, v19_21, v19_22, v19_23, v19_24, v19_25, v19_26, v19_27, v19_28, v19_29, v19_30))
pd20 = pubDigit(use20, sym20, pickN(activeIdx, v20_16, v20_17, v20_18, v20_19, v20_20, v20_21, v20_22, v20_23, v20_24, v20_25, v20_26, v20_27, v20_28, v20_29, v20_30))
pd21 = pubDigit(use21, sym21, pickN(activeIdx, v21_16, v21_17, v21_18, v21_19, v21_20, v21_21, v21_22, v21_23, v21_24, v21_25, v21_26, v21_27, v21_28, v21_29, v21_30))

// 80000000000 is the leading 8. Eleven digits against an exact-integer range
// of 2^53, so the value crosses into the DOM without a rounding of any kind.
pubState1 = 80000000000 + pubPack(pd01, pd02, pd03, pd04, pd05, pd06, pd07, pd08, pd09, pd10)
pubState2 = 80000000000 + pubPack(pd11, pd12, pd13, pd14, pd15, pd16, pd17, pd18, pd19, pd20)
pubState3 = 80000000000 + pubPack(pd21, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1)

plot(pubState1, 'STATE1', color = color.new(color.teal, 0),
  display = display.status_line + display.data_window)
plot(pubState2, 'STATE2', color = color.new(color.teal, 0),
  display = display.status_line + display.data_window)
plot(pubState3, 'STATE3', color = color.new(color.teal, 0),
  display = display.status_line + display.data_window)

// The checksum, and its SIGN carries whether this is the live bar - the same
// trick, for the same reason, as in both scanners. TradingView's status line
// shows the values of the bar under the CROSSHAIR, not of the last bar, so a
// reader with only the digits to go on would quietly report a four-hour-old
// picture as current. It has to be the token immediately after STATE3: that is
// where the reader looks for it.
plot(barstate.islast ? pubList : -pubList, 'LISTSUM', color = color.new(color.teal, 0),
  display = display.status_line + display.data_window)

// THE TRADED LENGTH, published so the reader can refuse to draw a line the
// chart is not going to agree with.
//
// maRejection.pine is where the real entries are taken, and its MA Length is
// typed in by hand - Pine cannot cross-write an input, so nothing here can set
// it and nothing checked that the two agreed. Now both publish: this file
// sends 5000000 + the length it trades, maRejection.pine sends 4000000 + the
// length it is set to, and the extension compares them. When they differ it
// says which is which instead of naming pairs, because a pair armed on MA 41
// is not a pair to enter while the chart is drawing MA 32.
//
// Seven digits with a fixed leading digit, the same addressing CFGSUM (6 and
// 7) and CFGGRP (8 and 9) already use, so the value can be picked out of a
// whole legend without identifying which row published it.
//
// 5000000 exactly - no traded length yet. The reader reads that as "no pick"
// and says nothing about the length rather than reporting a disagreement with
// a number that does not exist.
plot(5000000 + (activeIdx < 0 ? 0 : activeLen), 'MALEN', color = color.new(color.teal, 0),
  display = display.status_line + display.data_window)
